window.__SEARCH_INDEX__={"v":"cc099c0fe6","texts":{"2026-11-11_001":"450万移民改变了美国：他们究竟是谁，又去了哪里？ A Border Surge Reshaped America. Now, Data Shows Who 4.5 Million Migrants Were and Where They Went An unprecedented surge in border crossings over the past decade reshaped America: Millions of people from more than 150 countries arrived, bringing the U.S. immigrant population to its highest levels ever. Now, data obtained exclusively by The Associated Press paints a picture of who these migrants are, where they went, and what happened after the border. Blocks, neighborhoods and entire cities were remade in places like Elkhart, Indiana; Knoxville, Tennessee; and Dodge City, Kansas. This was no longer just a story about the border. It was a transformation unfolding in the everyday lives of Americans. Via a Freedom of Information Act request, AP obtained federal records on the self-reported destinations of 4.5 million migrants released in the United States after crossing the Mexican border — both legally and illegally — between October 2018 and July 2025. The Border Patrol encountered migrants from 174 countries in one year alone, a sharp departure from earlier periods when they were nearly all Mexican and Central American. Some went to cities that have long been magnets for immigrants — New York, Miami — but increasingly, others found their way to smaller cities with less developed immigrant footholds. Cincinnati, for example, saw a large number of arrivals from West Africa, Uzbekistan and India. New York City’s Queens borough registered at least 25 nationalities with significant arrivals, including five countries that each sent more than 7,000 people: Ecuador, China, Colombia, Venezuela and India. While Florida was the most popular state overall, Latin American immigrants increasingly looked elsewhere for more promising job prospects and better quality of life. Nearly six of every 10 migrants released at the border were women and children, a dramatic shift from a few years earlier when migrants were predominantly men. In 2023, Samba Diallo and roughly 2,000 of his countrymen arrived in Lockland, Ohio, increasing the town’s population by more than 50%, so suddenly it seemed to some that it happened overnight. No one knew they were coming, and Lockland has no formal services for immigrants. They piled into apartments, sometimes 12 or 15 in a space designed for a single family. They slept on mattresses pressed side by side on the floor. The fire chief warned there had been some small fires — they were unaccustomed to American stoves — and in a crammed apartment complex, this could pose a serious hazard. The influx of immigrants shocked this small town. One evening in 2023, Vincent Wilson, a local bicycle shop owner, arrived to teach an English class at a Christian-run center next to his bike shop. Instead of the group of a dozen or so West Africans he’d known for years, 160 people lined up around the building. Down the street, the lobby at a food and clothing pantry was packed, another line stretching out the door. Wilson realized they were walking miles a day. He put out a request for people to donate old, unused bikes. They didn’t speak much English at first, but Wilson figured out that the language of bicycles is universal: point, grunt, giggle and everyone understands. The Mauritanians became more visible in the community, walking around town, often in groups, sometimes wearing traditional robes unusual on American streets. Wilson became a familiar face to the newcomers, helping them navigate a country whose language and customs were still foreign to them. Khalidou Sy, a well-known hip-hop artist back home, became the Mauritanian community’s unofficial mayor. He spoke at churches and community meetings, explaining who all these new neighbors were. He also did some schooling of his fellow countrymen, he said. Mauritania is a more casual society, and here people strictly follow rules, he remembers scolding. Don’t whistle at women. Cross the street at the crosswalk. Rumors and lies spread. Rumors began circulating that the newcomers to Lockland were eating the ducks in the city park. Right-wing media reported about what was happening here as an “invasion.” KKK flyers started showing up, complaining of “illegal immigrants” and calling on “red-blooded white Americans” to “take your stand.” A group of neo-Nazis arrived from out of town, waving swastikas on a highway overpass. City leaders started sounding the alarm. The West Africans weren’t committing serious crimes, officials told the local media. The major concern was their overcrowded living conditions. Sy feels like he’s part of a great American tradition, the “melting pot,” he said, like the Irish and Italians before him. But he’s yet to put up an American flag his realtor gave him. “I love it, I wish I was American so I could hang it outside, but I cannot pretend,” he said. Maybe one day — his asylum application is still pending. For some Americans, immigration threatens the country they have always known. For newcomers like Sy, it represents the very tradition that made America what it is. The question is whether Americans can still agree on what that identity means.","2026-11-10_001":"千亿帝国掌舵人的私密丑闻：黄仁勋承认出轨，对方被美情报部门认定为间谍 Nvidia Opens Investigation Into Jensen Huang's Relationship With Chinese Spy Nvidia said on Sunday that it was opening an investigation into Jensen Huang, its chief executive and co-founder, after he acknowledged to the company’s board that he had been intimate with a business executive who U.S. intelligence determined was a Chinese spy. In a message to the company, Mr. Huang apologized. “I was unfaithful to my wife,” he said. “My actions hurt her and our children. There is no excuse for what I did. I am deeply sorry.” The relationship and affair are a rare misstep for Mr. Huang. Over the past three years, he could do no wrong. His chipmaker, which makes the technology to build artificial intelligence, grew into one of the world’s most valuable companies and the engine of the data center boom powering the U.S. economy. He started being followed by autograph-seeking throngs at events. And his black leather jackets, which he wears daily, became collectors’ items. Mr. Huang has credited his wife, Lori, whom he married in 1985, with making it possible for him to build the company. Public scrutiny of Mr. Huang’s ties with Ms. Huang began after The New York Times first reported last month that American intelligence agencies had discovered that Mr. Huang had developed a close relationship with a Chinese citizen and chip buyer, who U.S. officials concluded was gathering intelligence for Beijing. The chip buyer was Ms. Huang or Huang Le, whose biography said she worked in China as a TV reporter before becoming a top executive at a Singapore-based data center company that had become one of Nvidia’s biggest customers in Southeast Asia. Megaspeed was formed in 2023, when a Chinese online gaming company split off its Singaporean subsidiary. Megaspeed is among the firms in Southeast Asia that buy chips to build data centers and then rent their computing power to companies in China. Megaspeed is essentially a middle man. Megaspeed’s sister company in Malaysia bought at least $4.8 billion worth of advanced AI equipment containing Nvidia chips and technology between June 2024 and February 2026, according to trade records obtained from ImportGenius, a trade platform. It was an exceptional amount of money for a small, obscure company. The U.S. government has been investigating Megaspeed since late 2024, when a Commerce Department official visited its data center in Malaysia and found the site to be suspicious. The Nvidia equipment was still packed in boxes, suggesting it was being shipped in or out rather than installed there. Investigators suspect that some Nvidia chips acquired by Megaspeed are also finding their way illegally into China. Singapore has conducted its own investigation into Megaspeed. In 2025, Singaporean authorities brought Alice Huang in for questioning, but subsequently released her. As Megaspeed’s chip purchases rolled in, Mr. Huang and Ms. Huang began spending time together. Their relationship became a preoccupation in Washington, where officials were already alarmed by the large volume of Megaspeed’s purchases. Nvidia and Mr. Huang initially said there was nothing problematic or unusual about his relationship with Ms. Huang. In an interview with Bloomberg News last month, he said that his interactions with Ms. Huang had been “related to Megaspeed, and it was all completely normal and not unusual.” The U.S. government, which had been investigating Megaspeed for possibly steering advanced Nvidia A.I. chips to China, began its surveillance of Ms. Huang more than a year ago. In the course of the surveillance, they found that she was in communication with a U.S. citizen who was identified as Mr. Huang. Officials who had seen that intelligence said they were certain the relationship was romantic. That undercut Nvidia’s original misleading statements to The Times and others over the course of the last year about the nature of Mr. Huang’s relationship with Ms. Huang. As news about the U.S. surveillance of Ms. Huang broke, Mr. Huang’s room to maneuver all but disappeared. Congress wanted him to testify, probably under oath, and reporting on the surveillance raised the possibility that U.S. intelligence knew the nature of his relationship with Ms. Huang. There was also pressure on Nvidia’s board. Nvidia’s board said that it had “no reason to believe that either national security or Nvidia secrets were compromised,” but that “the board’s inquiry will examine all available information.” Mr. Huang, 63, will continue to lead the company during the investigation, the board said. He also received public support from President Trump. Asked by reporters whether he would bring Mr. Huang on his upcoming trip to China, Mr. Trump said, “I’d certainly take him.” He also described Mr. Huang as “an honorable guy.” As a co-founder, Mr. Huang has set up a unique structure at Nvidia where he dictates all aspects of its business while overseeing some 60 people who directly report to him. “Today’s news will not slow Nvidia,” Mr. Rizzo said. “Jensen is committed to continuing to lead Nvidia as C.E.O. without interruption.” Many questions about the situation remain unanswered, at least publicly.","2026-11-09_002":"Leaving the cosy confines of college for the tundra of the job market has always been bracing. Not every graduate hears a calling to a particular profession. Many want to do good but also need to pay off debt. AI is making the transition even bumpier, automating entry-level jobs and threatening entire careers. On July 13th more than 200 economists and tech luminaries warned that AI could cause “large-scale job displacement”. Finding your life’s work has never been so hard. As graduates trawl LinkedIn—sending AI-polished applications only to be rejected by AI-powered recruiters—they may wonder why they bothered with college at all. Among recent American graduates, 40% work in roles that do not require a bachelor’s degree, according to the New York Fed. Gen Z’s career anxiety has produced a rash of advice books. Bill Gurley, Jodi Kantor and Benjamin Todd all offer different answers to the same question. Mr Gurley encourages students to pursue careers they genuinely love. Ms Kantor likewise argues that fulfilling work can also be financially rewarding. Yet both authors also demonstrate why highly successful people often make poor career advisers. Their own careers are exceptional, shaped by survivorship bias rather than ordinary experience. Stories of successful restaurateurs or famous podcasters make inspiring anecdotes, but they offer little guidance to the average graduate. Hard work and passion alone rarely guarantee success. Benjamin Todd’s 80,000 Hours, the strongest and most original of the three books, takes the opposite approach. Drawing on 15 years of advising people at the beginning of their working lives, it examines careers not from the perspective of those who have already succeeded, but from the choices facing ordinary people. The result is a more systematic framework for thinking about work. First, perhaps do not follow your passion. Career books have long encouraged people to discover what they enjoy and then find matching jobs. But many people simply do not know what they want. Even when they have a genuine passion, there may be little demand for it in the labour market. Large numbers of students love music, art or sport, yet relatively few jobs exist in those fields. Passion alone is not a career strategy. “‘Follow your passion’ gets it backwards,” Mr Todd argues. Instead, become highly skilled at something valuable to other people, and passion may develop afterwards. Helping others increases life satisfaction, while mastering difficult skills is rewarding in itself. If you become excellent at your work, you gain more leverage to shape your career than if you pursue an existing passion but remain mediocre. To explain how people can make a positive difference, Mr Todd draws on the philosophy of effective altruism. Donating effectively may sometimes produce greater social impact than working directly in a helping profession. He also identifies AI regulation and preventing great-power conflict as careers with unusually large potential benefits. On money, he argues that beyond roughly $75,000 of annual household income, additional earnings contribute relatively little to self-reported happiness. His discussion of AI is equally counter-intuitive. Automation eventually destroys jobs, but it often increases demand for complementary skills before doing so. Banks actually employed more clerks after ATMs were introduced because lower operating costs made it worthwhile to open more branches. Rather than searching for careers that can never be automated, workers should continually adapt, moving towards the skills that become more valuable as technology changes. That may include AI model training, cyber-security or service industries whose demand rises as AI lowers costs. Still, Mr Todd’s philosophy has its own limitations. A relentless focus on maximising global good implies that every career choice should be judged by its overall impact on humanity. Taken to its logical conclusion, this way of thinking can become paralysing. Even John Stuart Mill struggled with depression, while Sam Bankman-Fried became a cautionary example of effective altruism taken disastrously wrong. Ultimately, careers rarely follow a straight path. Tony Blair promoted rock concerts before entering politics. Maya Angelou worked as a streetcar conductor before becoming a celebrated writer. Colonel Sanders founded Kentucky Fried Chicken at the age of 62. Getting your career right often takes years of experimentation and changing direction. In an age when AI is transforming the labour market faster than ever, certainty is becoming increasingly rare. Anyone worrying about how to spend their 80,000 working hours could do worse than spending a few of them reading Mr Todd’s thoughtful book.","2026-11-09_001":"一家银行塌了，把拉美这位“明星优等生”一直背着光的那一面，掀到了台前 A bank collapse exposes the dark side of Latin America’s star economyUeno Bank’s seizure has toppled Paraguay’s president and tarnished its glossy pitch to investors The stunning downfall of Paraguay’s president and collapse of its most popular bank have exposed cracks in a free-market success story hailed by investors and the global conservative movement in recent years. The presidency of Santiago Peña, a smooth-talking former IMF economist, unravelled in less than 72 hours this week after regulators seized control of Ueno Bank, a flashy digital lender used by more than half of Paraguayan adults that had become insolvent. Lawmakers quickly launched impeachment proceedings against Peña, a former investor in Ueno’s holding company who installed its boss to lead Paraguay’s central bank and entrusted the lender with hundreds of millions of dollars of public money. The senate approved Peña’s ouster on Friday. The episode, which has left 2.9mn people unable to access their savings, has shocked Paraguayans after their technocratic president spent three years in office promising to build a “modern” state that might one day join the OECD, and travelling the world to promote Paraguay as a haven for foreign capital. “We thought we were improving, but actually we’re the same as always,” said Elizabeth Gonzalez, 28, a barista in a café in downtown Asunción, a few blocks from where hundreds of protesters are maintaining a vigil after larger demonstrations earlier this week. Paraguay has emerged as an oasis of consistent GDP growth and macroeconomic stability within Latin America over the past decade. Peña also secured the country’s first investment-grade credit ratings. But the rapid implosion of Peña’s government, analysts said, would draw attention to deep-rooted problems with corruption and clientelism that have marred Paraguay’s economic development story. “The underbelly of the success story has been an institutional landscape that is . . . captive to a very small number of groups and families,” said Mariano Machado, Americas principal at risk intelligence firm Verisk Maplecroft. “That’s much more visible now than it was at the start of this week.” Paraguay has been ruled by the same party — the rightwing Colorados — for 75 of the last 80 years. Peña’s impeachment was largely the result of a rift with party president Horacio Cartes. Cartes picked Peña as a candidate in 2023 elections. But earlier this year Peña moved to form his own movement within the Colorado Party, sidelining Cartes’s influence. “Peña thought he could walk alone and that Cartes would fade into irrelevance,” said Sebastián Acha, a consultant and former lawmaker in Paraguay. “Cartes did not agree.” The central bank had already received at least 10 warnings since last year regarding Ueno’s substantial lending to entities related to its own parent company and other financial issues, according to Paraguayan newspaper ABC Color. Ueno Bank had a prominent role in Peña’s vision of a modern Paraguayan economy. Founded as a fintech in 2021, it quickly obtained a banking licence and waged an aggressive marketing campaign to bring on new clients, including sponsoring the national football team. Ueno took in more than $850mn in public money, including a tenth of the social security agency’s investment fund, and many small businesses began to rely on its payment system in Paraguay’s heavily informal economy. Ueno founder Miguel Vázquez spoke about becoming the country’s first unicorn and listing on the New York Stock Exchange. “Everything that man said was just window dressing, because it was all a sham,” said Eduardo Lopez, 35, a businessman at the protest who works in exporting goods to the US. “It affected all businesses . . . and it makes the country look bad. It’s a disaster.” In the early hours of Tuesday morning, the central bank froze Ueno accounts and began a process to wind down its operations. The monetary authority said it would guarantee deposits up to the equivalent of about $38,000 per customer and that there was no risk to the wider financial system. The response has raised hopes that Paraguay’s institutions will strengthen in the wake of the Ueno fiasco. “We are seeing the start of corrective measures,” said José Tomás Sánchez, a director at ICA consultancy in Asunción, arguing that the rapid arrest and charging of those involved “sends a very strong signal . . . that you can no longer do this kind of thing in Paraguay.” But others were less optimistic. “It’s pretty clear that this crisis was only resolved because Cartes decided to abandon Peña for political reasons,” said Ramiro Blazquez Giomi, strategist at financial services group Stone X. “It suggests there haven’t been the kind of institutional improvements that we had hoped to see. “We’re suffering the consequences of Peña splitting from Cartes,” said Batarram Caballero, 23, president of the student centre at the National University of Paraguay. “This is a group of people who have meddled and are destroying our country.”","2026-11-08_001":"The blame started before voting in America’s midterm elections ended and the extent of the Republicans’ drubbing was revealed. In an interview on election day Donald Trump said that America would cut off military aid to Israel “at the end of the year”. Israel no longer needs it, he explained; the country is “thriving”. But there was another reason, the president added: “It’s a bad talking point.” J.D. Vance, the vice-president, was more direct. On the day after the election, he blamed the war in Iran, and also America’s relationship with Israel, for the Republicans’ defeat. Israel has been falling steadily out of favour in America. Its brutal campaigns in Gaza and Lebanon had already angered voters and politicians on both ends of the political spectrum. The unpopular war with Iran has drawn further attention to the $3.8bn Israel receives annually from America, nearly all of it as subsidies for the purchase of American-made arms and other military supplies. America’s alliance with Israel featured in a number of high-profile contests, including that of Abdul El-Sayed, the senator-elect from Michigan who fiercely criticised military aid. Other candidates took pride in not accepting donations from AIPAC, the pro-Israel lobby. AIPAC itself said two weeks before the election that it would stop endorsing candidates, a tacit admission of the toxicity of its brand. Barack Obama signed the current ten-year $3.8bn package, which is set to run out in 2028. The Israeli government was aware that negotiating a new package would be tricky. Growing numbers of Democrats are particularly hostile to Israel, and the Trump administration—and Republicans more widely—are loth to give money to foreigners. In light of this, Binyamin Netanyahu, Israel’s prime minister, told The Economist in January that he favoured gradually phasing out American aid over the next ten years. Talks had started in recent months about the future of aid, but Mr Trump’s election-day announcement took Israeli officials by surprise. “It’s been clear for years that the terms of the alliance with America were going to have to change,” says Alon Pinkas, a former diplomat who served as Israel’s consul-general in New York. “Now it looks like Netanyahu has agreed with Trump to end the current aid agreement, without anything ready to replace it.” The future of the relationship is unclear. Mr Trump has suggested that Israel will be able to continue buying American arms, on its own dime. But Israeli officials worry that a Democratic-controlled Congress will limit the kinds of weapons Israel can buy and restrict how it can use them, especially in the occupied Palestinian territories. For over half a century successive American administrations have been committed to maintaining Israel’s “qualitative military edge” in the Middle East, supplying it with advanced weapons systems, years before America’s Arab allies. That may be up for debate as well. An end to American aid would blow a $7bn hole in Israel’s finances in the next two years. After three years fighting multiple wars, it has been on a spending spree to refresh the IDF’s arsenal, ordering planes, helicopters and more. Israel will owe another $10bn after 2028 for kit it has already ordered, according to the Wall Street Journal. Israel’s economy could withstand that, though it would mean cuts to the national budget in the coming years. “In financial terms it’s regrettable, but not an earthquake,” says Yigal Newman, a professor of finance at the Hebrew University in Jerusalem. “American aid is barely half a percent of Israel’s GDP.” But, he points out, it would also be a blow to Israel’s standing as America’s key ally. Israel’s relationship with America not only boosts its military and economic power, it gives Israel diplomatic cover as well. America frequently deploys its veto in the United Nations Security Council to block anti-Israel resolutions. It puts pressure on other countries, such as Britain, which has announced sanctions on Israel over the violent settler attacks on Palestinians in the occupied West Bank. If the new Congress is considering similar sanctions, Israel will be more exposed internationally. All is not lost for the alliance, which goes far beyond arms sales. America maintains radar and intelligence-gathering installations in Israel and recently expanded an airbase used by American aircraft in the Negev. The two countries co-operate intensively on missile defence and intelligence sharing. Israel has access to America’s surveillance satellites. America’s arms companies make use of the Israel Defence Forces to test their weapons on the battlefield. But the relationship has fundamentally changed. Mr Netanyahu, who lost his majority in the Knesset on October 27th, has long presented himself to Israelis as the ultimate expert on America. Yet he has overseen the most drastic deterioration in his country’s standing in America in Israel’s history. Israeli diplomats privately accept that their prime minister, his hardline policies and hard-right coalition are “toxic in America”. A change in government in Jerusalem would help. But the alliance is becoming more brittle.","2026-11-07_001":"US stocks shed more than $3tn in a day of chaotic trading which saw the tech-heavy Nasdaq 100 plunge 6.4 per cent after Democrats’ sweeping midterm election victory upended Wall Street’s mammoth AI bet. The market value drop on the S&P Composite 1500 index, a broad basket of America’s publicly traded companies, was the biggest since the turn of the millennium, according to FT calculations based on FactSet data. Traders stampeded out of tech shares on fears that Democrats’ stunning victory over Donald Trump’s Republicans in Tuesday’s midterms would cool the AI spending boom that has driven Wall Street to record highs this year. “[Investors] are throwing in the towel and indiscriminately liquidating,” said Adam Crisafulli, president of market research group Vital Knowledge. Selling among chipmakers was brutal, with Nvidia, the company most closely associated with the AI surge, tumbling 9.3 per cent, pushing its market value down more than $400bn. The S&P 500 index of US blue-chip companies lost 4.4 per cent, its worst session since last year’s “liberation day” ructions, in a session marked by wild swings. The broader Nasdaq Composite fell 5.5 per cent, its steepest drop in 19 months, closing more than 10 per cent below its October 6 record and confirming a technical correction. The Philadelphia semiconductor index plunged more than 12 per cent. “With the blue wave, investors fear that peak [AI capital spending] is behind us,” said Charles-Henry Monchau, chief investment officer at Swiss private bank SYZ Group. One hedge fund portfolio manager said the move was driven by quick-moving investors ditching large positions, known as “de-grossing”. “People are starting to get worried now, today the move was so swift,” said another hedge fund executive. Investors fear that newly elected politicians who campaigned against data centres will hit the brakes on AI infrastructure development, eroding Wall Street’s expectations for vast spending on chips, servers and facilities that have driven up earnings expectations and powered stocks to record highs this year. “Guys who would normally be bargain-hunting, excited by these low prices, they’re not jumping in . . . everyone’s hiding out in cash,” said the chief executive of a New York brokerage. Vincent Mortier, the investment chief of Amundi, said the “honeymoon” gains for US stocks on the day after Democrats won control of Congress had been “very short-lived”. He added that the tech repricing was “harsh and quick as the fundamental question marks resurface under the microscope of the new political US picture — data centres backlash, new regulation”. Abdul El-Sayed, the Democratic senator-elect from Michigan, has said there should be a “full-on pause” in frontier development, arguing that “you’re seeing the pace of technological development outpace the pace of safety”. Incoming South Carolina senator Annie Andrews proposed a two-year ban on new data centre construction to stop communities “getting steamrollered” by “incredibly wealthy, powerful companies”. The Democrats’ win in Congress was “a horrible day for America”, said a US portfolio manager with large positions in tech companies including Anthropic. The results of the midterms would have “massive ramifications” for the price of chips used in AI servers, the initial public offering pipeline and AI data centre companies, the person said. Several Big Tech bosses spent heavily to support the electoral chances of Trump’s Republicans, who have sought to lighten regulation of the AI sector. Semiconductor groups were among Thursday’s biggest declines. Qualcomm dropped 9.5 per cent, Broadcom fell 10 per cent and Micron shed 17 per cent. Memory stocks Sandisk and Western Digital — among the market’s best performers this year — plunged 22 per cent and 17 per cent, respectively. But the selling was not limited to tech stocks. Caterpillar, the world’s biggest heavy machinery maker, slid 6.4 per cent, while retail giant Walmart dropped 1.1 per cent. Data centre operators were also hammered, with IREN and Applied Digital shedding more than 14 per cent and CoreWeave nearly 10 per cent. Even the “hyperscalers” lost ground, with Meta down 4 per cent and Amazon and Alphabet each off 2.2 per cent. Elon Musk’s companies, which are both based in Texas where Democrats won the race for governor and their first Senate seat for more than 30 years, had another bad day. Tesla fell 10.8 per cent, adding to a 3.3 per cent drop on Wednesday, while SpaceX was down 9.1 per cent, on top of a 3.8 per cent drop the previous day. Gina Hinojosa, the governor-elect of Texas, which accounts for about a third of planned new US data centre capacity, has backed an extended moratorium on approving new construction.","2026-11-06_001":"In mid-February, one of President Trump’s economic advisers arrived at the Oval Office with charts showing how the administration’s policies might be making America great again. Household income was up, particularly among low-income Americans. Retirement accounts were soaring. And the nation’s economic growth was even better than projected. “This is great stuff,” said Trump. He had completed his first year back in office, and his power seemed limitless. Within days, Trump made a decision that would change everything. After campaigning on ending foreign wars, Trump wagered on one of his own, triggering an open-ended conflict that has seen the deaths of more than 20 U.S. servicemembers, cost U.S. taxpayers more than $47 billion, and pushed up the cost of everything from freight to fertilizer. On Tuesday, Republicans suffered losses that amounted to one of the most decisive repudiations of a president in modern history. It was likely a direct result of Trump’s decision to go to war in Iran, sending gas prices up 40%, leaving many of his most loyal supporters disillusioned and effectively drawing a curtain on the Trump age. Conservative commentator Matt Walsh described the war as “the greatest political blunder in the last two decades,” and a betrayal of Trump’s America First movement. “We’re going to have to find a new leader because Trump abandoned it,” he said in an interview. While Trump publicly defended the war, privately he asked people around him whether he should change course, including junior staffers who gathered for a photo. The war wasn’t the only issue contributing to Tuesday’s crushing defeat for Republicans. Trump also lost his advantage on immigration. After first lady Melania Trump complained about people she knew getting deported, the president would tell aides that the architect of his aggressive immigration campaign, Stephen Miller, had gone too far. When Trump’s aides told him in the spring and summer that polls showed voters concerned about his handling of affordability, he said the polling was fake, the senior administration official said. Trump also pushed out of Senate races Republican candidates who would have faced better odds, including in Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina and Texas. He instead anointed candidates he believed would be loyal to him. Democrats won in all four of these states. “It really didn’t have to be this way,” said Rep. Ralph Norman, the Republican who lost the South Carolina Republican nomination to Sen. Darline Graham after Trump swooped in. “Pride goeth before the fall. It’s going to be a wake-up call for the Republican Party.” By August, polls showed Republicans had lost their edge with voters on the economy. Trump aides grew deeply concerned in August when internal polling showed voters overwhelmingly blamed Trump for inflation over Democrats. In September, gasoline prices in the U.S. shot up 16 cents in a single week. Not long after, diesel prices hit a record high. On one Republican call, a pollster said, “People will mark the last week of September as the week the bottom fell out.” When Trump wanted to veto a popular bipartisan housing bill, senior White House official James Braid sent a note to other staffers asking them to help persuade the president to sign it. Trump shut down the lawmakers and advisers favoring the bill, scuttling a news conference so late that some lawmakers arrived anyway. Nobody cares about this, he told one adviser. Aides eventually persuaded him to let the housing bill become law without his signature. Allegations of corruption involving the administration and the Trump family became a top concern for voters. Yet Trump continued to focus on pet projects, including a new White House ballroom and a proposed 250-foot triumphal arch. He interrupted Oval Office meetings to show off his latest plans, at one point leading a guest outside to see how he attached gold letters reading “The Rose Garden.” Aides said it was a waste of time to tell him to stop talking about the ballroom because he held several long meetings a week on the topic and enjoyed giving tours. On Monday, the day before the midterm election, Trump gave a nearly hourlong speech at the Lincoln Memorial reflecting pool that aides had billed as a final argument to voters. Trump mostly talked about his renovations of the capital, including cleaning up graffiti, resodding the mall and replacing rusty pipes. One of the project’s biggest foes, he said, had been algae. There wasn’t enough time, he said, to discuss his plans for the nation. “The biggest issue is that I wasn’t on the ballot,” he told The Wall Street Journal. Yet, Trump had said repeatedly that voters should imagine that he was on the ballot. By October, some Republicans felt that a spending blitz by Republican super PACs, including Trump’s MAGA Inc., would stabilize the map. The money couldn’t compete with gas prices and other pocketbook troubles. “It was about the economy,” said Chris Winkelman, president of the Congressional Leadership Fund, a super PAC run by Johnson allies. “It was always about the economy.”","2026-11-05_001":"After a 28-year odyssey spanning 29 countries, 36,000 miles, facing down polar bears, kidnappers and a spell in Russian custody, Karl Bushby was finally back in the arms of his longsuffering mother, Angela. “Where have I been?” he joked as Angela sobbed into his chest with a mix of relief, pride and no doubt the odd sleepless night. Surrounded by television cameras and with a Sky News helicopter overhead, the 57-year-old was given a hero’s welcome as he completed his record-breaking voyage on foot from Punta Arenas in Chile, the southernmost city on the South American continent, to his home town of Hull on Monday. “There has never been a welcome home quite like this, I’m sure,” said Bushby, shaking his head in disbelief as he greeted a crowd of well-wishers in the city’s Queen Victoria Square. “This is absolutely madness.” It has been 28 years since Bushby, then aged 29, left the army to walk the world on what he intended to be a 12-year journey. He finally crossed a misty Humber Bridge back to the city on Sunday – on the same date he set off in 1998. In that time he has stuck by two simple rules: not to return home until he had fully completed his feat, and not to use transport to advance. In all, he walked 36,000 miles across four continents, a frozen sea, deserts, jungles and mountains. Quite a trek for a man not keen on walking. “It’s boring as hell,” he told the Guardian last month. His return on Monday will make Bushby the first person to have walked continuously across the world, visiting 29 countries, having already exceeded the record for the longest unbroken foot journey (far outstripping the 25,000-mile circumference of the world). The voyage took him across the frozen sea of the Bering strait between Alaska and Russia and across the notorious Darién Gap in Central America. He described last month how he had been threatened at gunpoint, faced down a polar bear, was convicted of being a spy, and been held in custody in Russia and sentenced to deportation. Bushby strode into Queen Victoria Square like a modern folk hero, his passage only slowed by the dozens of people rushing to shake his hand and take photographs. Yet for a man who had navigated much of the planet, he did appear to get a little lost on his way through the crowds of his home city. “I’ve no idea which direction I’m going. Everybody point the way,” he said. “Sorry to keep you all waiting – it’s been a while.” Through the crowd he pushed his heavily loaded trolley, dubbed the Beast 2 (his first one broke down) laden with essentials such as medicine, sleeping bags, headtorches – and two very worn tyres. About three miles away, Angela, 75, was waiting at the gate of Bushby’s childhood home as she had promised. After a tearful embrace, they took a long look at each other. “You’ve changed a bit,” Bushby told her. “I’m older,” his mother replied through tears. “We both are,” he said. “Well done for hanging in there.” He was then whisked inside the small terrace house, festooned with union flag bunting, where he was reunited with his son, now 36, and a 12-year-old granddaughter whom he was meeting for the first time. Earlier on a makeshift stage, Bushby thanked the city for his hero’s welcome. “It took a world to get me here,” he said. “The kindness of strangers – so many, from individuals to nation states, to get me back here. I didn’t do this alone. There was a big team, the biggest team: Earth. “We faced down governments, we faced down some of the hardest challenges, we had to create two world records just to get back here. It was a global effort.” Karl Turner, the Labour MP for Kingston upon Hull East, brought with him a message from the prime minister, Andy Burnham, who congratulated Bushby on his “truly remarkable” feat and thanked him for his service as a paratrooper. In a statement read on stage by Turner, Burnham said: “Your journey has taken nearly three decades, crossed continents and tested you in ways most of us can barely imagine. “But today, as you return to the city from which you left 28 years ago, you bring with you something far greater than any world record or a remarkable personal achievement. You bring a story of what can be accomplished through extraordinary determination and self-belief.” As he was ushered away for a big lunch before the walk to his mother’s house, Bushby had one last message for the city he left in 1998: “I guess I’ll be seeing you around.”","2026-11-04_001":"美国中期选举进入最后24小时：经济明明还不错，为什么选民还是这么不满？ On the eve of elections that will decide whether President Donald Trump's Republican Party keeps control of Congress, voters' minds are on the economy, with high and rising prices a key issue and many blaming his policies, specifically tariffs and the Iran war, for the naggingblaming his policies, specifically tariffs and the Iran war, for the nagging inflation. In short, Americans are in a sour mood. The numbers, however, tell a more complex story. On the upside, the unemployment rate remains historically low, more people than ever are employed, overall economic growth is solid and the artificial intelligence investment boom has — until recently — been driving up the stock market, padding retirement accounts for millions. Countering that momentum, of course, is inflation. Though not nearly as high as just a few years ago, it is elevated and ever-present enough that people feel the pinch whenever they fill their gas tanks, pay a utility bill or order a meal. Trump is not on the ballot on November 3, but voters have his economic record in mind as they head to the polls. And the vibe is skeptical, if not downright grumpy. Consumer surveys show the mood has generally deteriorated since Trump's second term began in January 2025, though there are nuances: One widely tracked measure of confidence published by the Conference Board perked up in October, while the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index registered a near-record low. Brian Jacobsen, chief economic strategist at Annex Wealth Management, said the confidence index tracks the macroeconomy, while the sentimentmacroeconomy, while the sentiment index is \"more personal,\" asking people how they are doing financially and how affordable they find their lives to be. \"Sentiment is the bigger driver of election outcomes, but confidence is the bigger driver of markets,\" he said. One argument for macroeconomic confidence derives from the labor market. Firms may not be hiring much, and new graduates are having a hard time finding jobs, but companies are not conducting mass layoffs either. Weekly unemployment insurance claims are near a 57-year low. The unemployment rate, at 4.2% in September, has measured no higher than 4.5% for five straight years — the longest stretch on record. There is also the updraft from stock market gains to consider. The main US equity indexes have notched dozens of record highs this year, as Trump frequently reminds attendees at his rallies. Most Americans hold some stock, though higher-income households have disproportionately more. Outlays by higher-income households, buoyed by the rising value of their stock holdings, have helped underpin consumer spending, which has been a strong driver of economic growth. Data last week showed the US economy grew at a 3.4% annualized pace last quarter, its strongest performance this year. AI-related demand is fueling many of the gains in the stock market and in the real economy, and business investment rose at an annualized 12.7% pace last quarter. Still, some economists warn of potential fragility — and cracks may be starting to appear. As the midterm elections neared, a wave of corporate earnings results from the likes of Amazon.com (AMZN.O) and Intel (INTC.O) added to growing worries about the sustainability of the AI spending boom, sending indexes such as the Nasdaq Composite sharply lower. \"A sharp reset in AI expectations could undermine corporate confidence, trigger a pullback in capex (capital expenditure) and weigh on GDP growth, just as higher oil prices, tariffs and tighter immigration restrictions push inflation higher, raising the risk of a stagflationary environment in which growth slows even as price pressures persist,\" Torsten Slok, chief economist at Apollo Global Management, wrote in a mid-October note. For much of Trump's second term in the White House, wages were growing faster than prices, meaning that workers could expect their paychecks to cover as much or more of the same goods and services as before. That situation changed earlier this year after the US and Israel launched attacks on Iran in what has become an eight- month war that has lifted fuel prices globally and contributed to price acceleration more broadly. Demand for AI is also adding to inflation, Fed policymakers and economists say. Wage growth is also slowing. Consumer prices and average hourly earnings have both risen about 5.5% since January 2025, data from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics shows. The upshot: from a purchasing-power perspective, workers are no worse off, but also no better off, than when Trump came into office in January 2025. The US central bank raised its policy rate in September and is expected to increase borrowing costs again in December, delivering what Fed Chairman Kevin Warsh calls \"monetary medicine\" to bring down inflation without, he says, the need \"to throw sand in the gears of the economy and throw people out of work.\" Longer-term borrowing costs have also risen, adding to the affordability woes that voters have cited as weighing on them. The combination of 30-year mortgage rates that have topped 7% and a shortage of housing supply that has driven up home prices has conspired to put home purchases out of reach for many people.","2026-11-03_001":"德国30多城爆发抗议，部分地区演变成打砸冲突：为什么欧洲年轻人越来越愤怒？ Mass demonstrations in Germany that passed largely peacefully on Saturday erupted in violence as night fell and rioters faced off against police in Frankfurt, Cologne, Hamburg and Berlin. Authorities blocked off Frankfurt’s main shopping street Die Zeil and arrested more than 30 people after demonstrators smashed shop windows and made off with streetwear and other goods. Officers arrested one person suspected of breaking into a gun store and stealing two weapons, which were retrieved. Police used water cannons to break up the crowds as protestors threw Molotov cocktails and set off fireworks. By early Sunday, the streets had emptied, while police maintained a visible presence. Several thousand mostly young people gathered at the Main river embankment on Saturday afternoon before weaving their way through the financial district and toward the European Central Bank in the city’s east. Red flags flew over a diverse crowd, with some wearing hammer and sickle motifs and others bearing rainbow flags and traditional Middle Eastern scarves. One protestor dressed as German Chancellor Friedrich Merz became an instant selfie magnet while others called for him “to resign.” Banners read: “Eat the rich,” “We can’t feed the poor but we can fight a war?”, “your housing market is making us homeless” and “Send Merz to the frontlines.” The protests were organized by a group known as “Gen Z regelt” — loosely translated as “Gen Z sorts things out” — which had stressed in the run-up to the protest that it wouldn’t tolerate any form of violence against people or property. The crowds fell short of the more than 150,000 participants initially expected across more than 30 cities. The group said it plans another countrywide protest on Nov. 14. “We are young people who no longer want to watch decisions being made about our future without us,” it said in a post on its website, calling on people of all ages to join the demonstration “for education, health, climate protection and an affordable life.” Authorities had feared a repeat of the violence that swept through France last month, when blockades and marches morphed into nationwide cost of living protests that drew more than half a million people and descended into riots, arson and looting. “I have repeatedly signaled that we must expect left-wing extremists with a potential for violence to infiltrate this demonstration, and that is exactly what has happened,” ARD television’s Tagesschau cited the interior minister for the state of Hesse, Roman Poseck, as saying. In Berlin, a mass gathering at Brandenburg Gate proceeded with little incident, but police made almost 60 arrests in other parts of the city for property damage, assault, resisting law enforcement and violations of the Weapons and Explosives Act. The unrest in France has emboldened activists in other parts of Europe, including Zurich, where several hundred people, masked and dressed in black, gathered around Langstrasse and Helvetiaplatz in the city center for an unauthorized protest on Saturday night. Participants erected barricades using rubbish containers and other objects, setting some of them on fire. A car was set alight in nearby Zwinglistrasse. Police used water cannons and rubber bullets to disperse the crowds. Youth-driven protests have erupted across Europe in recent weeks, from Belgium and Portugal in the west to Serbia and Albania in the east. Although grievances are slightly different in each country, the prevailing sentiment is one of anger over affordability, crumbling infrastructure and inequality. Students in France began to demonstrate in mid-September to denounce overcrowded classrooms, teacher shortages and outdated buildings. After the violent unrest in France, authorities rolled out tougher security measures and pledged aid for businesses and shop owners that suffered damages. In Italy, Premier Giorgia Meloni’s government passed new security measures aimed at stemming violent protests, including tougher penalties on minors after a series of incidents across the country. More than 300,000 people joined a fresh protest wave in France on Saturday that sparked renewed violence, although on a lesser scale than the midOctober riots. Paris deployed about 70,000 law enforcement officers, similar to the force used during demonstrations last weekend. Police arrested 779 people, authorities said, adding that five officers were injured in Paris. Images circulated by the National Gendarmerie showed one officer being hit by a Molotov cocktail, an attack Interior Minister Laurent Nunez described as “attempted murder” in comments to journalists late on Saturday. France has launched an attempted murder investigation after the incident, Agence France-Presse reported on Sunday. Student unions have called for blockades at high schools and universities this week and urged students to join a major cross-sector strike scheduled for Nov. 5 across the country.","2026-11-02_001":"大国掰手腕，小国发大财：谁在中美竞争中坐收渔利？ For better or worse, many poor countries’ fortunes hinge on their natural resources. Since their industries are less sophisticated and their service sectors smaller than in rich places, an underground bounty can make a big difference to their economic prospects. Rising global demand for oil or ores can spur growth, attract investment and fill fiscal holes. Now the world may be entering another supercycle, propelled by the race between America and China to secure supplies of minerals critical to artificial intelligence, electrification and other technologies expected to define the 21st century. Chinese minerals diplomacy is alive and well. Donald Trump’s administration has already signed mineral-themed agreements with more than 20. Today the rivals are both courting a much smaller group of countries capable of getting minerals out of the ground quickly. On October 16th the World Bank said it would quintuple, to $20bn, a fund to help countries capture some of the surging demand. Yet in order to succeed, their governments cannot rely only on their subterranean resources or their cosiness with America and China. They must also show ability to supply stuff at speed. The urgency is palpable. In May 2025 Mr Trump was forced to rein in tariffs he had imposed on Chinese goods, after China retaliated by restricting exports of seven rare-earth minerals used to make semiconductors and weapons. America wants to break China’s chokehold. The wheedling has worked. According to officials, in 2025 the American government and American firms spent $15.4bn on foreign mines, up from nearly nothing in 2024, and more is expected this year. In response, Chinese state-owned mining giants splurged well over $30bn abroad, far more than in 2024. So far the biggest beneficiary has been the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC). Most Western investors abandoned the vast, conflict-torn, landlocked central African country when prices of tin, diamonds and other of its abundant resources collapsed. The Chinese have pledged $1bn to expand a copper mine and propose to build a much bigger one that could churn out up to 500,000 tonnes per year. Now America is back, too. A government-financed fund is in talks to buy 40% of one of the last remaining Western-owned operating mines, a copper concern run by Glencore, a Swiss-based company. Private firms have followed. The DRC is so indigent that the cash is already transforming its economy. In 2025 foreign mining investment was equivalent to roughly 8% of its GDP. Two other big winners are Brazil and Kazakhstan. But they are nevertheless benefiting mightily from American and Chinese interest. America’s approach has been the more focused. In Brazil it has set about building a rare-earth empire. In September USA Rare Earth took over Serra Verde, the only producer of four of the scarcest such minerals outside Asia. A further package pulled together by the Department of War and a loan from the DFC will push American investment to nearly $5bn. In Kazakhstan America is eyeing tungsten, which is vital to high-tech arms-manufacturing. In November 2025 Kaz Resources, a firm backed by the administration and by investors including Eric Trump and Donald Trump junior, is stumping up $1bn to develop deposits at Kairakty and Northern Katpar. A further $1.6bn could come via the DFC and EXIM. Last year Mr Trump’s officials spoke about recruiting countries to America’s camp while pressing them to spurn Chinese advances. Most, they said, would have to pick a side. The experience of the DRC, Brazil and Kazakhstan shows that this is not how things have played out so far. American and Chinese mineral-hunters are piling into the same handful of countries because only so many have lots of half-finished, old or dormant mines that can be completed or revived quickly and cheaply. Now America is becoming impatient—and so behaving more like the Chinese. In the short run, smash-and-grab may benefit the superpower rivals, too. Yet the strategy has its limits. It relies on others to prospect for and develop existing deposits, even as prospectors and developers grow more cautious. Despite forecasts of surging demand for copper, which may outstrip supply by 8% within ten years, the world’s biggest producer of the red metal, Chile, has cut back output, largely owing to underinvestment in decaying assets. Higher prices should in theory attract investment in new projects. But these can take a decade or more to pinpoint and develop. In the meantime, all the meddling by the governments of America and China is confusing the price signals miners rely on to decide whether an untapped deposit is worth the hassle. The result is more investment paralysis—but, for the lucky few places with ready deposits, it’s payday. ■","2026-09-01_001":"美国欠钱、五常内斗、战争也管不了：联合国真的还有存在的必要吗？ It may be both the most prestigious and the most frustrating job in international diplomacy: members of the United Nations are weighing who should succeed António Guterres as Secretary-General when he completes his final term in December. The timing couldn’t be more critical, with U.N.’s fortunes at a historically low ebb. With major powers repeatedly blocking the U.N. from intervening in crises and the U.S. rolling back funding, some predict that whoever succeeds Guterres will simply have to do “less with less” and manage a process of institutional decline. Yet, at a time when global challenges—wars, epidemics, and climate change—continue to proliferate, there is a compelling case for preserving the U.N. as a space for global deal making. The U.N. is an organization that rests on international cooperation, but the current global moment is one of international competition. A shrewd leader could help states de-escalate conflicts and cooperate on global challenges out of necessity, not idealism. Maybe the biggest challenge for the next Secretary-General lies inside the Security Council, the organization’s most powerful body. The five permanent, veto power-wielding members—the United States, China, Russia, Britain, and France—have repeatedly blocked intervention in crises from Ukraine to Myanmar. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine and the U.S. decision to attack Iran suggest that some of the council’s most powerful members no longer feel bound by its guiding principles. If the U.N.’s political situation is its main headache, its financial position is a close second. After the Trump Administration cut off the bulk of U.S. funding in 2025, U.N. officials have been wondering how to pay the bills. The U.S. is supposed to cover about a fifth of the organization’s core costs and a quarter of its peacekeeping budget, but currently owes the U.N. about $4 billion. European governments are also scaling back development and humanitarian aid. This is a markedly different landscape from the one Guterres encountered in 2017, when U.N. members had just agreed to the Paris climate accord and Sustainable Development Goals, including a pledge to eradicate extreme poverty by 2030. That brief moment of optimism proved ephemeral. Rising tensions among major powers have eroded governments’ trust in one another and their faith in the U.N.’s negotiation-based approach. Increasingly, governments are turning instead to smaller, looser decision-making clubs such as the Group of 7 or the BRICS. Eight candidates are currently jostling to replace Guterres, but the selection process creates its own dilemma. The Security Council’s five veto powers are central to vetting candidates and recommending one to the General Assembly. Anyone who wants to be Secretary-General must avoid offending them. Talking about what is wrong with the world today without irritating at least one of the major powers is quite hard. On the diplomatic front, the incoming leader’s priority will be to demonstrate the ability to insert himself or herself into one or more conflicts, signaling a shift from the Guterres era. Conflict prevention and peacemaking have become crowded fields, with a growing number of middle powers leading peace processes the U.N. might once have overseen. The greater danger is that the U.N. fails to engage in meaningful crisis diplomacy at all, cementing the impression that it is no longer a significant peacemaking force. The best approach may be to frame the U.N. as a sort of global risk reduction organization. The pandemic, regional conflicts and new technologies have demonstrated how crises can cause global disruption. The U.N. does not have the power to halt these trends, but it is a space in which governments can discuss risks and explore steps to mitigate them. Its advantage is that it remains a neutral space where different blocs of governments can engage in dialogue. The role of the Secretary-General is perhaps to be both the world’s most prominent event organizer and a sort of political therapist for world leaders. The new leader cannot heal all the world’s divisions, but with courage and political acumen, the Secretary-General can make international disputes a little less toxic. That may be a limited vision of what the U.N., sometimes dubbed the “parliament of man,” is for. But in an era of multiplying dangers and distrust, it is a noble and perhaps essential one.","2026-09-02_001":"曾发誓“绝不把石油交给美国”，如今委内瑞拉签下百年大单：川普拿走了什么？ As Nicolás Maduro’s vice president in Venezuela, Delcy Rodríguez once accused her political rival of planning to hand over the country’s riches — its vast oil reserves — to the United States. “They are her owners,” Rodriguez said in 2024 of María Corina Machado, then the U.S.-backed opposition leader. “Her master gives her orders to do what? To hand over the oil, the gas, the gold … to hand over and trample on our historical dignity.” Now, Rodríguez is the country’s interim leader, thanks to President Donald Trump. And on Friday, Trump announced what he called the “biggest oil deal in world history” — a pact that would give the U.S. a long-term controlling stake in nearly a quarter of Venezuela’s untapped oil reserves. The deal calls for the U.S. to enter a joint venture with a private company, creating a new oil giant that the Trump administration said will hold 100-year contracts at the Venezuelan oil fields. It would become the second-largest private oil company by reserves in the world, after Saudi Aramco. The agreement appears to contradict much of what Venezuela’s government has stood for since then-President Hugo Chávez proclaimed it a socialist state. For Venezuelans, it also creates a sense of history repeating itself, with a twist: A government long criticized for its opaque oil deals, and widespread corruption, is now entering a murky, long-term arrangement with a new protector, the United States. It remains unclear how much Venezuela will earn from the deal; how much it will cost U.S. taxpayers, given the need for expensive front-end investment; and how much will simply end up enriching the private company — and the controversial businessman, Alejandro Betancourt — at the center of it all. But she added to the confusion over the deal by contradicting Trump and other U.S. officials on some of its details. She said the project was signed for a term of 25 years, not 100 years, with a production target of more than 1.5 million barrels per day. She estimated the country’s revenue would reach $209 billion for the Venezuelan state, or $19 per barrel. She added, “There is something that must be absolutely clear: Venezuela retains ownership of and sovereignty over its resources, while it uses capital, technology, and operating capacity to leverage the recovery of a strategic industry hard hit by sanctions.” One key player who clearly stands to benefit is Betancourt, a Venezuelan businessman with extensive experience in the country’s oil industry, who over the past decade has faced multiple investigations into alleged money laundering around the world, including in the United States. As Betancourt grew more important to the Trump administration, serving as a critical intermediary with Venezuela’s oil industry and broker of early oil deals this year, U.S. officials lobbied to resolve a money laundering investigation against him in Switzerland. The U.S. also has not acted on a Swiss arrest warrant against him, said the people, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss a sensitive situation. Instead, he has been allowed to enter the country repeatedly for meetings with the Trump administration, people familiar with his travel said. Beyond Betancourt’s role, Palmieri said, he worries that new agreement could create conditions for the interim Venezuelan government to delay elections and remain in power to ensure the new arrangement is carried out. “What is the quid pro quo?” Palmieri asked. “If it’s just the U.S. continuing to exercise total control over the regime, it just means that the United States owns more and more of what happens in Venezuela over the next few years.” Francisco Rodríguez, a Venezuelan economist at the University of Denver, said very little is known about the agreement, which has been presented as a done deal. “That in itself is the problem,” Rodríguez said, noting there does not appear to have been any public bidding process, or an effort to go through the country’s national assembly to approve the plan. “This is precisely the type of lack of transparency that the country got used to during Chavismo,” he said of the ruling socialist party, “and embodies everything that has been wrong about how those governments handled Venezuelan affairs.” Roxanna Vigil, a former Treasury Department and White House official, said the first Trump administration had made a strong case, including through sanctions, “for why the Maduro regime was one of the most corrupt regimes in the hemisphere, maybe even in the world. “Are we a better manager of Venezuela’s money? A more trustworthy custodian?” she asked. “Or are we really just partnering with the same people who destroyed Venezuela?”","2026-09-03_001":"靠5美元衣服横扫全球，Shein如今上市就破发：欧美年轻人不买了吗？ Shares in online fast-fashion retailer Shein fell 8% in their first day of Hong Kong trade on Tuesday, with investors worried about setbacks that long delayed its listing and undermined its competitive advantages. The stock fell as much as 10% despite only 6.6% of the company being floated. Shein raised about $1.7 billion in the IPO, which valued the company at around $26.5 billion — far below its 2022 peak of nearly $100 billion. The debut caps a four-year effort to tap public markets. Shein initially pursued a New York listing before turning to London, but intense scrutiny of its business practices in the West and regulatory obstacles frustrated both attempts. Hong Kong eventually became its destination. The dramatic fall in valuation reflects how much has changed since the pandemic e-commerce boom, when social-media influencers showing off “Shein hauls” helped turn the company into one of the world’s fastest-growing fashion retailers. Known globally for selling $5 tops and $10 dresses, Shein built its success around an extraordinarily fast supply chain and the ability to ship cheap products directly from China to consumers. But tariff and duty changes in the United States and Europe have weakened that advantage. The removal of the U.S. “de minimis” exemption for low-value parcels and similar changes in Europe have made cheap cross-border shipping more expensive, putting pressure on the model that fuelled Shein’s global expansion. At the same time, growth has become more expensive to sustain. Revenue growth slowed from 21% to 8%, while marketing expenses rose faster than revenue in 2025. Shein reported a $99 million net loss in the first quarter of 2026, compared with a $395 million profit a year earlier. Investors are increasingly asking whether the company can continue expanding its user base without customer acquisition becoming substantially more costly. Competition is another problem. Shein faces rivals including Temu and AliExpress while consumers have become less responsive to the ultra-cheap cross-border model. Daily active users in Europe have fallen around 45% since the EU scrapped its duty exemption on small parcels. One analyst described this as less a problem unique to Shein than “the end of an era for cheap cross-border shipping”. Shein’s enormous global reach remains an advantage, but much of its customer loyalty has always belonged to the price tag. That helps explain why investors remained cautious even after Shein’s huge valuation reset. At around 15 times forward earnings, the company came to market at more than twice PDD’s multiple despite weaker growth visibility and significant regulatory and trade risks. A heavily discounted valuation, analysts warned, is not necessarily the same thing as a cheap stock. The market is increasingly treating Shein less like a disruptive high-growth technology platform and more like a retailer facing margin and execution pressures. Shein is trying to change that story. One potentially important shift is its gradual transformation from a pure retailer into a retailer-plus-platform. Third-party brands increasingly sell through Shein, allowing the company to generate service revenue and monetise its infrastructure beyond its own inventory. It is also becoming less dependent on apparel, expanding into categories such as home and living. The goal is to turn Shein’s supply-chain and customer-acquisition capabilities into a broader lifestyle commerce platform. Yet diversification brings risks of its own. Hosting third-party merchants creates additional compliance and reputational challenges at a time when Shein is already under intense regulatory scrutiny. Its weak trading debut also stands in contrast with the extraordinary investor appetite surrounding Chinese companies in artificial intelligence, robotics and memory chips. Those are the sectors where investors currently see structural growth and where demand for new listings has been strongest. Shein’s Hong Kong debut therefore marks a striking reversal from the optimism surrounding the company four years ago. In 2022, it was a nearly $100 billion private company, celebrated as one of China’s most successful global consumer brands. Today it is worth roughly a quarter of that peak and faces slower growth, tougher trade rules, fierce competition and persistent regulatory scrutiny. Shein spent a decade proving how many consumers would buy a $5 dress. Its first day in Hong Kong showed that shoppers and shareholders may be buying two very different things.","2026-09-04_001":"继马拉松之后，又一种运动全球爆火：一年150万人参赛，Hyrox到底是什么？ Inside Strawberry Arena, a cavernous stadium in Stockholm, thousands of chiselled men and women are puffing and slick with sweat. A few are the colour of the fruit. And little wonder: they have run a kilometre, covered the same distance on a ski-training machine, then run again. Between laps of the stadium they have also rowed, lunged, done burpees, carried kettlebells, pushed and pulled a sled and lobbed a medicine ball. A respectable race time is about 90 minutes; the fittest strive to finish in under an hour. This cross between a relay race and high-intensity interval training is called Hyrox. (The name is believed to be a portmanteau of \"hybrid\"—a reference to its mix of cardio and strength exercises—and \"rock star\", to make it seem cool.) To the unathletic, it may all sound nauseating. But Hyrox claims to be the \"fastest-growing mass-participation fitness concept in the world\". At the first race, in Hamburg in 2017, some 650 people took part. Last season more than 1.5m athletes lined up across 105 events in 35 countries; in the 2026-27 season more than 2m are expected to compete across 107 race weekends on six continents. September alone features events in 12 cities, including Athens, Mumbai and Washington. The format has certainly set pulses racing. The hashtag #hyrox has been viewed more than 500m times on Instagram and TikTok. Peloton, a home-exercise firm, offers Hyrox training classes. Later this year a four-day European cruise will allow athletes to train at sea, with tickets for a cabin starting at a bracing £2,200 ($3,000). There is a Hyrox dating app and bank card. \"Hyrox is not just a race or an event,\" one acolyte proclaims online. \"Hyrox is a lifestyle.\" Big brands have taken the hint. Puma, Red Bull and Centr already sponsor the series, and in April Amazfit signed a three-year global deal to become Hyrox's exclusive wearables partner, kitting athletes out with everything from smartwatches to smart rings. In August Amazon went further, striking a wide-ranging global partnership that makes it title sponsor of Hyrox's London event this December and gives it dedicated storefronts—selling supplements, sleds and recovery gear—in six European countries. Financiers have noticed too. In June L Catterton, an LVMH-backed private-equity firm, agreed to buy a majority stake in Hyrox from Infront, a Swiss sports-marketing group controlled by China's Wanda, which had held the reins since 2022. Hyrox's revenue reportedly reached around €140m ($150m) in 2025, with earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation of about €30m—modest by the standards of a Premier League football club, but proof enough that this is now a business, not just a hobby. The hype is partly explained by a boom in gym culture: men and women are pumping iron more than they used to. Across America, Britain and Europe, fitness-club memberships have reached a record high. Isabella Helm, a 25-year-old trainee accountant, loves Hyrox and trains six days a week in London: \"You get addicted to feeling fitter and stronger.\" Hyrox adds a social element to what is generally a solitary pursuit. There is also the adrenalin rush that comes with competition. Leaderboards foster friendly rivalry. Athletes can race solo, in pairs or teams; friends can pay to watch on in comfort from the stands. Participants often turn races into mini-holidays. As the format spreads, there is no shortage of new destinations to visit. Danny and Zoe Rae are British influencers who, like a number of couples, combine races with recreation and doing \"something nice\". \"It's a great way to travel,\" Mr Rae avers. The sport attracts racers of all ages. The average age of participants is 36, but the oldest at those championships was 75. Laura Hitt trains several older people at her gym in Exeter, in south-west England. She says that \"it's one of the most accessible sports I've been part of.\" Hyrox is kinder on the body than other fitness races—such as CrossFit, which peaked in popularity in the 2010s—because it does not require complex weightlifting. This may explain why it appeals to retired athletes as well as hobbyists. Will Hyrox run out of breath? Some grumble that tickets are scarce and cost upwards of £150—even without accounting for travel, hotels and months of gym fees. But Moritz Fürste, the co-founder of Hyrox (and an Olympic-medal-winning hockey player), believes the format has room to grow, and hopes it will be included in the Olympics in 2032. He concedes that it is expensive, but that demand is still far exceeding supply. \"It's like tennis and golf,\" he observes: \"Once it becomes your sport, you'll keep coming back.\" ■","2026-09-05_001":"美国人开始抵制AI，原因却不是怕失业：居然是怕...... A spectre is haunting America—the spectre of data centres. The computer-filled warehouses powering America’s boom in artificial intelligence have become less popular than almost every other kind of infrastructure—even nuclear-power plants. Counties and states are rushing to wrap them in red tape or ban them altogether. On August 31st Donald Trump weighed into the fractious debate, warning that local opponents would “end up being backwards and poor” if they killed the “Golden Goose”. The backlash is scrambling traditional political alignments, reshaping the coming elections and unnerving tech firms and investors. For a sense of how quickly the politics has changed, consider Texas, Illinois and Pennsylvania. In 2019 J.B. Pritzker, Illinois’s governor, signed a bipartisan law creating incentives for data-centre construction, calling them as critical as roads, trains and schools. Greg Abbott, Texas’s governor, boasted last year that his state was “the epicentre of AI development”. Josh Shapiro, Pennsylvania’s governor, was similarly enthusiastic. Yet all three have since become more cautious as public opposition has grown. In August Mr Pritzker warned that data centres risked “higher electric rates, higher water rates, or…environmentally unsound practices”, after pausing applications for tax credits introduced in 2019. Mr Abbott ordered Texas utilities to audit data centres seeking electricity, warning that they could endanger the reliability of the grid. Mr Shapiro has also tightened rules, giving communities more power to veto projects while attacking his opponent as pro-data centre—increasingly, a political term of abuse. Public opinion has shifted dramatically. A little over a year ago some polls showed majority support for local data-centre construction. By March, a Gallup poll found 71% opposed. An Economist/YouGov poll at the end of August put opposition at 63%. Chicago illustrates the change. Long before the AI boom, the city and its suburbs hosted scores of data centres with little controversy. Now residents are packing public meetings to stop new developments. In the first eight months of this year, American counties considered 409 moratoriums or similar measures, nearly seven times last year’s total. Some proponents have blamed foreign powers. Mr Trump observed that “China could not be happier”, while OpenAI said a Chinese firm had produced images designed to undermine American support for data centres. Yet OpenAI itself concluded that the campaign generated “little or no observable engagement”. The real cause of the outrage appears much closer to home. Americans are sceptical of AI, distrust the firms building it and fear job losses. But polls suggest that what worries them most about data centres is their local impact. A Fox News poll found 75% cited concerns such as energy use, water bills and traffic as the main reason for opposing a data centre nearby; just 11% pointed to AI itself. Some fears, particularly around water consumption, may be overblown. Crop irrigation still consumes vastly more water than data centres. Electricity is a more serious question. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory estimates that data centres consumed 4.4% of America’s electricity in 2023 and could account for 9.5-15.3% by 2030. Wholesale electricity prices are being pushed up, which could eventually affect households. Yet the Electric Power Research Institute estimates that data-centre growth actually reduced average electricity prices by 6% between 2019 and 2024 by spreading the grid’s fixed costs. Thirty states have also approved or proposed special tariffs to ensure large customers pay for infrastructure upgrades. The question is whether public opinion can still be changed. An Echelon Insights poll found that opposition could be reversed if respondents were told about just one potential benefit, such as lower property taxes or more spending on schools. Another poll found that most Americans would accept several thousand dollars to support a local data centre, with a median acceptable payment of $10,000. Tech firms are already showering gifts on communities. Trade unions also remain enthusiastic supporters because data centres bring investment and well-paid construction jobs. Still, the backlash shows few signs of abating. Tax exemptions have been paused or made conditional in several states, while satisfying local demands will inevitably raise construction costs. Yet investment may simply flow to more permissive states. Texas alone has more data centres under construction than Georgia, Arizona and Pennsylvania combined. Enough projects, analysts argue, will find communities willing to accept them in return for tax revenue, jobs and other benefits. As Mr Trump put it: “Let Data Reign.”","2026-09-06_001":"英伟达到底有多恐怖？一家公司的价值，已经超过很多国家一年的经济总量 NVIDIA was named in 1993 after the Latin word for envy. Sure enough, the American colossus inspires plenty of it. Insatiable global demand for its graphics-processing units (GPUs), the chips that power artificial intelligence, has made Nvidia the world’s most valuable company, worth $5.4trn. Next year it may also become the most profitable, generating $370bn in net income. By 2029 its sales could hit $1trn. Jensen Huang, Nvidia’s boss, is truly the magician at the heart of the AI boom. His firm’s share price is 14 times what it was on ChatGPT’s release in late 2022. The ten biggest public companies championing AI make up 40% of the value of the S&P 500 index. Nvidia alone accounts for 8% and unlike, say, Apple or Tesla, which make smartphones and cars, it is almost solely a bet on AI. The company has produced about 15 cents of every dollar the American stock market has returned since 2023. Yet many also see Mr Huang as a magician in a more worrying sense, fearing that he is an illusionist inflating a dangerous bubble. Through $1trn-worth of deals, Nvidia provides data-centre landlords and AI labs with cash or guarantees so that they can buy its GPUs. Some call it the “bank of AI”. At the very least, Nvidia’s critics say, its financial engineering smacks of the “vendor financing” which pumped up the revenues of networking-gear makers like Cisco in the dotcom mania of 2000-01, whose collapse brought about a recession. Look more closely, however, and the worries are mostly unjustified. If Nvidia’s bets on AI come good, they could accelerate the technology’s adoption, boosting productivity and living standards. If they misfire, the cost will fall chiefly on Nvidia’s shareholders. That is how capitalism is supposed to work. True, the dotcom and AI booms share unnerving similarities: an exciting new technology, an epic bull run, hubristic tech bosses. Nvidia’s rise from seller of chips to video-gamers and cryptocurrency miners to linchpin of the economy has been so rapid that many people have yet to learn how to say its name (“en-vidia”, not “nuh-vidia”). Just as Cisco’s sales of routers and switches presupposed exponential growth in web traffic, Nvidia’s GPU revenues assume endless demand for AI tokens. Today it is the pace of AI adoption that is hard to forecast. If it does not soon soar, Nvidia’s customers may call in the guarantees just as the chipmaker’s own sales nosedive. Nvidia’s financial wizardry is partly defensive. Its latest GPUs no longer have the market to themselves. Roughly half Nvidia’s revenue comes courtesy of America’s cloud-computing “hyperscalers”, chiefly Amazon, Google, Meta and Microsoft, which are designing their own silicon. Non-Nvidia AI chips account for 38% of the market, up from 26% in 2023. To stay ahead, Nvidia used to spend over a fifth of sales on research and development. Now it spends less than a tenth. Yet the differences from the dotcom boom are more important than the similarities. Nvidia’s balance-sheet is extraordinarily robust. The company has $99bn of cash and is churning out more. In each of the past three years annual sales have roughly doubled. Gross margins have fattened from less than 60% to 75%. This means that, whereas Cisco used debt, Nvidia can use cash to backstop its deals with buyers of its GPUs. Even if its commitments came due and its cashflows levelled off starting next year, by 2028 it would still be less leveraged than all but 39 non-financial firms in the S&P 500 are today. Profits would need to drop by 60% from that plateau for Nvidia to forsake its investment-grade credit rating. Mr Huang thinks that the biggest obstacle to the AI revolution is not lack of demand for AI but inadequate infrastructure. The markets will not provide capital on the scale that is necessary, so Nvidia is offering financing itself. Nvidia has an advantage in understanding the balance of risks and rewards. Although this bet is big enough to affect the economy, it is an entrepreneurial one. Every company that reinvests cash rather than returning it to shareholders also gambles that it can beat the market. Companies exist to make such concentrated bets. If investors want to diversify, they can do so themselves. And Mr Huang is hardly alone. The hyperscalers, the world’s most successful companies before Nvidia came along, are making the same bet. So are some big names on Wall Street. Last month Goldman Sachs, BlackRock, Blackstone and others joined Nvidia in a $500bn data-centre initiative. And so are Mr Huang’s shareholders, who haven’t yet rushed for the exit. If they are all wrong, it is their money on the line. And if they are right, the AI era may arrive a bit sooner. ■","2026-09-07_001":"中美“太空战”已经不是科幻片了：卫星开始互相追逐，甚至能直接抓走对方 In June, a secretive unmanned Chinese spaceplane monitored by U.S. military analysts released a small satellite hundreds of miles above Earth. The object then looped around another Chinese satellite before returning towards the spaceplane, according to U.S. space-tracking firm LeoLabs. Little is known about the satellite or the mission. But the U.S. military operates a similarly secretive uncrewed spaceplane. Both programs reflect the rapidly evolving field of military space operations as Beijing and Washington prepare for the possibility of conflict extending into space. Chinese researchers linked to the military are developing technologies ranging from satellites designed to pursue and even capture other spacecraft to systems that could extend fuel supplies while doing so, according to documents reviewed by Reuters. The United States and Britain have also conducted their first joint military operation in space in the path of a suspected Chinese spy satellite. U.S. Space Force chief General Chance Saltzman said China was rapidly developing ground- and space-based weapons designed to interfere with or destroy targets. Satellites have become indispensable to modern warfare. For decades they have provided communications, navigation and intelligence services to armed forces on Earth and typically remained on fixed paths once in orbit. Increasingly, however, militaries view them as active weapons platforms capable of launching attacks by disrupting another satellite through jamming or laser dazzling, or even capturing enemy spacecraft. Disruption of U.S. space capabilities could affect everything from surveillance to missile defense. The competition comes as Earth’s orbit becomes increasingly crowded. SpaceX operates about 11,000 Starlink satellites, China is building rival networks, and U.S. President Donald Trump’s Golden Dome missile defense program envisions space-based interceptors. In a joint U.S.-British operation last year, U.S. space forces directed a military satellite to swoop close to a UK one as China’s Shiyan 12-01 satellite passed beneath them. Experts told Reuters the operation appeared intended as a strategic signal to Beijing, demonstrating allied unity and capability. The U.S. and Britain publicly announced the operation but did not disclose its proximity to a Chinese spacecraft. China’s foreign ministry said it was not familiar with the specific details, but said Beijing opposes the weaponization of space and supports its peaceful use, while accusing Washington of stoking an arms race through efforts including Golden Dome and potential space-based weapons. Chinese state media has described Shiyan 12-01 as conducting space environment surveys. U.S. military leaders increasingly argue that space must be treated as a potential battlefield. U.S. Space Command head General Stephen Whiting has warned that adversaries could target American space assets early in a conflict and said the United States must adopt a more active warfighting posture. That means protecting American systems while also being prepared to threaten or strike hostile assets. “We must be well-prepared for conflict in space. And if deterrence fails, we will fight and win,” he said in April. Western officials warn of threats including ground-based lasers, electronic jammers, cyberattacks and anti-satellite missiles. In orbit, the United States, China and Russia all operate maneuverable satellites capable of approaching and inspecting other spacecraft. Such systems could potentially carry weapons, including lasers or projectiles. Some specialists note that disrupting satellites remains easier from Earth than through spacecraft-on-spacecraft attacks. In June, the U.S. Space Force disclosed an earthbound electromagnetic weapon designed to disrupt adversary satellites. Although the United States remains the world’s leading space power, China is rapidly expanding both the number of satellites it operates and the complexity of its missions. Chinese satellites have demonstrated what U.S. officials describe as orbital “dogfighting,” involving multiple satellites moving in a synchronized way in proximity. Chinese spacecraft have also shown the ability to capture a defunct satellite and move it into a different orbit. Last year, China conducted what was likely an on-orbit refueling attempt, according to a U.S. Space Force official. Refueling could be a major breakthrough because satellites are normally constrained by the fuel they launch with; replenishing it could determine whether a spacecraft remains a hunter or becomes the hunted. Chinese institutions are pursuing technologies that allow satellites to calculate fuel-efficient maneuvers while pursuing other spacecraft, manage hundreds of satellites simultaneously, and even use multiple spacecraft with nets to capture targets. Such technologies can have non-military uses, but China’s PLA Daily said controlling space was essential for safeguarding national security, seizing the strategic initiative and achieving victory in war.","2026-09-08_001":"全球多国物价再次上涨，央行又开始加息：新一轮“涨价潮”要来了吗？ Who remembers the “soft landing”? In 2022-23 central bankers dreamed of bringing high inflation down without triggering a recession. Some countries came awfully close to achieving it. But with victory tantalisingly close, a growing number of central banks are now redoubling their fight against inflation. Price pressure is building. The world is a long way from the pain of four years ago, but the diagnosis is not promising. On September 2nd New Zealand’s central bank raised rates. Rate-setters on the other side of the Tasman sea have already increased borrowing costs three times this year. The Bank of Japan did so in June and the Bank of Korea in July and August. On September 10th the European Central Bank (ECB) is expected to raise its deposit rate to 2.5%. Many traders expect that, before long, the Federal Reserve will follow. Policymakers are responding to growing evidence of rising price pressure. Average annual inflation across rich countries has stopped falling towards 2%, the target in most of them, and has instead edged up in recent months. In August annual inflation in the euro zone reached 3.3%, the highest in three years. Switzerland has inflation of just 0.8% a year, reflecting economic policy that is one of the wonders of the modern world. But even that is a two-year high. And in some places prices are evoking the sad memories of the “cost-of-living crisis”. Inflation is close to 6% in Lithuania, for instance, up from 3.1% in January. Energy is the main culprit, but not the only one. War in the Middle East has caused the price of oil and natural gas to rise. In Europe, natural-gas prices are more than twice as high as before the Iran war. They could go further as the continent’s storage tanks are refilled ahead of the winter. Already euro-zone energy inflation is easily in double-digits. The typical American, meanwhile, is paying just over $4 for a gallon of petrol, up from less than $3 at the beginning of the year. The problem, however, may be bigger than fuels. The Economist estimates that across the rich world, average annual “core” inflation, which strips out energy and food, has increased from 2.7% in early 2026 to 2.9% today. To examine countries’ underlying inflationary pressure in more detail, we found comparable figures for 23 rich countries on services prices, which are largely determined by the price of labour (rather than more volatile commodities). Services inflation is rising in two-thirds of our sample. This is despite the fact that in many countries measures of wage growth continue to decline. (One explanation for this puzzling result is that wages are not well measured; another is that falling productivity growth in services makes it harder for firms to absorb pay rises.) There is a third suspect: artificial intelligence. Subdued productivity growth has been the norm across rich economies for years, with America the main exception. But even there, economists worry that AI's disruptive effect on white-collar and professional-service jobs could eventually crimp wage growth just as its productivity gains show up—a trade-off that complicates the usual assumption that faster wage growth alone explains sticky services inflation. So far there is little evidence that people are revising up the price rises they anticipate in the future—the thing that would really spook policymakers. Most market-based measures of inflation expectations have not moved meaningfully in recent weeks. A recent survey by the ECB suggests that consumers expect lower inflation over the next year than they did a few months ago. Fancy leading indicators of inflation—including from Alternative Macro Signals, a consultancy, which analyses millions of news articles—do point to strengthening global inflationary pressures. But these tools’ predictive record is patchy. Whatever the future holds, many central banks are in no mood to take chances. Critics have argued that in 2021-22 policymakers dawdled in responding to surging inflation: rate-setters do not want to make the same mistake twice. They “shouldn’t wait” for inflation to become entrenched before responding, Slovenia’s central-bank governor has counseled. Isabel Schnabel, who sits on the ECB’s board, recently warned that waiting until energy costs pass through to pay could once again leave policymakers behind the curve. A new tightening cycle may, then, just be getting started. UBS, a Swiss bank, reckons that many of the central banks which have not yet started tightening will do so soon. Candidates include Sweden’s, and even Switzerland’s. For all the appearance of a soft landing, the fight against inflation never really ended. ■","2026-09-09_001":"中国汽车出海杀疯了：5年出口暴涨7倍，现在开始把工厂开到全世界！欧洲人都看傻了 When Ford opened the industry’s first large-scale overseas outpost, in Manchester in 1911, it set the route by which ambitious carmakers went from dominating at home to spanning the globe. Over a century later, with Ford in retreat, exports of China’s cheap but technologically advanced cars are roaring, and its firms are taking the next step to becoming global giants. When localising production, Chinese carmakers will not be able to bring all of their competitive advantages with them. But many will travel—to the dismay of foreign rivals. China’s mighty carmakers, which pump out around a quarter of the world’s cars, have competitors on the run everywhere. Vicious competition for a domestic market forecast to shrink by 10% this year has intensified a price war and encouraged overseas sales. In five years foreigners’ share of the local market has roughly halved, while exports have risen seven-fold. In 2026 exports could hit 10m vehicles, over 40% more than last year, according to AlixPartners. There are plenty of reasons to manufacture closer to foreign customers. Carmakers want to eliminate the expense of shipping entire vehicles and avoid tariffs. A presence on the ground also helps with adapting cars to local tastes and regulations. China’s firms are already assembling cars, or plan to do so, across much of the developing world, including Indonesia, Kazakhstan, South Africa, Egypt, Brazil and Mexico. But it is the vast European market they regard as their biggest prize. If tariffs imposed by the EU in 2024 have hurt Chinese vehicle imports, it hardly shows. In the second quarter of this year Chinese carmakers captured 11% of sales in western Europe, overtaking Japanese rivals. But tougher deterrents are in the works. The EU’s proposed Industrial Accelerator Act would tie purchase subsidies and tax breaks for corporate fleets to vehicles meeting certain local-content thresholds, alongside other measures favouring production within the region. Chinese carmakers are hurriedly updating their plans. BYD is close to opening a plant in Hungary that could eventually churn out 300,000 cars a year. It has put on hold another new factory in Turkey as it searches for a pre-existing site in Spain or France. A brand new factory takes around three years to get up and running; acquiring and retrofitting an existing plant is quicker and cheaper. Volkswagen, meanwhile, plans to phase out production at four sites in Germany. An even swifter approach is to take over part of an underutilised factory. AlixPartners reckons there is spare capacity to make 2.5m cars a year across European factories. Chery signed a deal in June with Nissan to borrow part of its Sunderland factory in Britain, and a similar agreement in July allows Geely to build EVs at a Ford plant in Valencia. Such deals may speed market entry, but the next stage will be independent production. SAIC has confirmed plans for a new factory in Spain, while Xpeng is searching for a permanent European base. European car bosses may hope that the cost advantage of around 30% their Chinese rivals currently enjoy will be eroded by localised production. If so, they are bound to be disappointed. China’s edge comes from lean corporate structures, streamlined manufacturing and a high degree of vertical integration. Chinese cars typically contain around 1,000-2,000 different parts, whereas European competitors are still wrangling 3,000-10,000. China has also mastered the “software-defined vehicle”, developed around a centralised computer rather than dozens of separate micro-controllers. All this lowers costs and makes Chinese carmakers much quicker at developing new vehicles, which typically takes around two years, compared with at least double that for foreign competitors—a trait the industry calls “China speed”. Much of the advantage will persist as production moves abroad. Research and development will remain in China, and manufacturing methods perfected at home will be replicated globally. Chinese firms will face higher supplier, labour and energy costs, but they can choose cheaper manufacturing locations such as Hungary or Slovakia. Local labour costs may soon matter less. In China carmakers are working on “dark factories”, so automated that the absence of human workers means lighting is not required. Once a dark factory is ready, the Chinese will “just pack it, ship it over and unpack it,” reckons Daniel Hirsch of Oliver Wyman. AlixPartners estimates Chinese carmakers will have capacity to make 3.4m cars overseas by 2030, up from 1.2m last year; others put the figure as high as 6m. What is certain is that China speed will not slow soon.","2026-09-10_001":"全世界的孩子都越学越差？这一代到底怎么了？全球教育出了什么问题？ Every three years the OECD, a club of mostly rich countries, releases the results of international school tests sat by 15-year-olds all across the world. There were reasons to hope that the latest data, published on September 8th, would bring positive news. Marks in mathematics, reading and science had collapsed in the previous round of testing, which was carried out just after the pandemic. Policymakers dared hope that, with the calamity well in the past, grades might start to rebound. That is not what happened. On the contrary, in rich countries—the primary focus of the tests—scores in all three disciplines have fallen further. In literacy, the decline is actually speeding up. These data suggest that there are long-running factors pushing down achievement in schools, and that without swift correction, pupils’ abilities could yet plumb further depths. Anyone who cares about the young of today—and the economies of tomorrow—ought to be alarmed. The OECD’s project, known as PISA, takes a measure of young people’s aptitudes as they approach the end of their compulsory school days. The results are used to create an international league table of sorts. This year, as in almost every round since 2000, Asian school systems rule the roost. Children in China—taking part for the first time since 2018—have wiped the floor with almost everyone else (though scores there are skewed because the tests are sat in only four rich provinces). In science they score 100 points above the average for OECD countries. Yet zoom out, and the dominant story is of long-term decline. The average scores in the 23 OECD-member countries that have taken part in every edition of PISA reached a peak around 2012 and have been falling ever since. In both maths and reading, their 15-year-olds are now testing at levels that might have been achieved by 14-year olds a decade ago. Children who were already among the poorest performers are falling even further behind. Across education systems, the gap between low- and high-performing students is now the largest ever recorded by PISA, in all the disciplines it tests. Some 29% of 15-year-olds in OECD countries are considered low performers in all three of reading, mathematics and science—up from 25% when the tests were run four years ago. But falling scores will also intensify long-running worries about the impact of technology on children’s learning—and on their reading skills in particular. Ubiquitous phones and widespread access to social media mean young people are getting less practice making sense of complex texts. Screens are clearly diverting children from books: the number of nine-year-olds in America who say they read them for pleasure has fallen from nearly 60% in the 1990s to 37% now. Declining literacy could well be dragging down marks in other disciplines—because reading well is key to progressing in subjects such as maths and science, too. PISA may itself share some blame for setting school reformers on a dodgy course. When the tests were first carried out in 2000, Finland shocked the world by coming top of the table. Policymakers who trekked to Helsinki for lessons returned with messages that teachers in their home countries were only too happy to receive. It argued that teachers should spend less time talking in front of blackboards and more time helping pupils work things out for themselves. What countries could serve as new models? Asian school systems have shared somewhat in the declines recorded in recent years, but generally less than other rich places. Asian countries are much less obsessed than Western ones with keeping classes small: they generally choose to have fewer teachers, whom they can pay more and train more intensely. That seems to work better than adding ever more staff to schools—the main way that America, in particular, has long tried to drive up scores. Britain’s march up the rankings deserves more attention than it has received. Starting some 15 years ago under a Conservative-led coalition government, school reformers in England—by a long way the largest of the country’s four separate school systems—zigged when much of the rest of the world zagged. The nation made its curriculums more fact-filled (some prefer to say “knowledge-rich”) at a time when policymakers elsewhere had been waxing lyrical about instilling vague soft skills. It made exams more rigorous, deprioritised coursework (on which it is easy to cheat) and toughened inspections. Test scores are not the measure of a person, nor proof that they are destined for failure or success. But good grades are highly predictive of fatter earnings, better health and longer lives. There is not yet much evidence that artificial intelligence will reduce the returns on a top-class education, but there are plenty of reasons to think the technology will make mincemeat of the weakly skilled. Time for school-reformers to hit the books. ■","2026-09-11_001":"苹果2026发布会炸了：第一台折叠iPhone来了：卖到1.6万元，凭什么？ Apple kicked off its most consequential product event in years on Wednesday, where it introduced a truly new iPhone, a new chief executive, as well as higher prices. It hopes consumers will concentrate on the first two. The headline is the first foldable iPhone, which will start at $1,999. An almost tablet-like device, the new iPhone Duo will fit comfortably in a pocket and allows people to use more than one app at a time. It comes in two colors: white and dark gray. Equally important was the man introducing it. John Ternus took over as Apple's CEO last week, becoming Apple's third CEO since 1997, when Steve Jobs returned. Hopes are high that the longtime hardware engineering executive can spark a new wave of innovation and the foldable iPhone is the most significant Apple device in a decade. Ternus smiled broadly as he strode on stage at Apple's headquarters to kick off the event. \"We've been imagining quite a lot,\" he said to the hundreds of gathered media in the audience. \"We think you're going to like what you're going to see.\" Tim Cook and the rest of Apple's executive team sat in the front as he spoke. The bad news for consumers was that Apple increased iPhone prices, though not as much as some analysts projected. The cost of memory and storage chips inside the smartphone has quintupled since last fall, prompting Apple to raise prices across its device lineup to protect its profit margins. The highlight of the foldable iPhone Duo is the larger internal screen, which enables iPhone users to have two apps open at the same time. They will be able to watch a video while texting, or look at an email inbox on the left with a message window on the right. Another highlight: the \"crease\" — where the internal screen folds—is effectively invisible when open, unlike competing Android devices where it can often be noticeable, degrading the consumer experience. One noticeable trade-off is the device's weight. While thin when unfolded, the Duo's two halves and sturdy construction make it noticeably bulkier than other iPhone models. For its new Pro model phones, Apple emphasized the on-device AI capabilities powered by its upgraded A20 Pro chip, which it said runs faster and dissipates heat more effectively. It also showed off a new variable aperture for the models' camera, that opens and closes automatically depending on the amount of light in the scene, like a human pupil expanding in low light. Apple said the price of iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max will both jump $100, to $1,199 and $1,299, respectively, compared with last year's 17 Pro. That is not as large a price jump as some feared, and suggests Apple could take a hit on its profit margins for the devices. Notably two devices weren't on the menu: the base model iPhone, along with the second generation of the iPhone Air, which are expected next year. That means customers who want the latest model iPhone will have to pay up for the Pro models or the Duo, potentially a clever strategy by Apple to push customers to more expensive models. Foldable phones aren't new. Samsung released its first Galaxy Fold in 2019. Some companies even have tri-fold phones that haven't one but two hinges. But the category has stalled, with shipments of foldables falling 15% in the first half of the year, IDC says. Apple's entry will be a shot in the arm for the category, IDC says, capturing 44% of the revenue generated by all foldable sales in 2026 even though it launches with just over three months to go in the year. As new CEO, Ternus was the star of this year's show. At last year's event, while a mob of gadget reviewers rushed the displays of iPhone 17s, and swarmed Cook, Ternus stood to the side, chatting quietly with other Apple executives and almost entirely ignored. Ternus has been head of hardware engineering for Apple since 2021, leading the team that built the foldable iPhone. That team also incorporated Apple's highly rated chips in its Mac computers, one of its less appreciated but more consequential accomplishments of recent years. It is no accident that Apple timed his transition so that, for his first product rollout, Ternus introduced its biggest update to its most important product. One knock on Apple's Cook era has been that the company didn't release more innovative products. Though the foldable was developed on his watch, Apple is anxious to set a tone that a new innovative day has dawned at the company.","2026-09-12_001":"AI十年内会杀死全人类？Anthropic研究员发出最严重警报！1.5亿人围观 The sky streaked orange and red as my mother drove us home to Albuquerque through the Tijeras Pass, which separates the Sandia Mountains to the north from the Manzano to the south. “Look out for mushroom clouds,” my father said to her. “If you see them, turn around.” I was already pondering these worn fantasies this week, as the 25th anniversary of 9/11 loomed, when Jacob Coxon wrote a social-media post about quitting his job at Anthropic because AI technology would, in his estimation, “kill us all by the end of the decade.” The sentiment takes up unfinished business; ruination has been building up in contemporary culture. This fantasy—of the end of the world, and of being present for it—is among the most powerful emblems of the 20th century. The atomic bomb inaugurated it, and nuclear armaments remain the reference technology for apocalyptic threat and reverie. We believe in the fantasy, and pine for it, because nothing feels better than the belief that you might witness the conclusion of humankind. The threat of annihilation from nuclear weapons is simple and easy to explain. For one part, a single warhead can level an entire city. For another, the mere launch of a single missile all but assures the release of all of them. Likewise, the AI superintelligence of the Terminator films is both straightforward and obviously threatening. Skeleton-like metal robots want only to destroy humanity. Real AI is not so straightforward. I used it on the morning of Coxon’s post to help me nail down the quantity of various sugars I would use in a gelato recipe. By the afternoon, it had undergone a costume change, into the angel of death. The nature of AI’s extinction threat is unclear and second-order. In an interview with Wired, Coxon struggled to say exactly how AI might bring about the end of civilization. “The classic example is, like, synthesizing a new virus or taking down critical infrastructure by doing some sort of hacking spree,” Coxon said, before admitting that “the latter might be less likely to kill literally everyone.” Coxon is less a whistleblower than a poster; he posted a fairly common sentiment that just happened to catch wind. It’s the anniversary of 9/11. OpenAI just claimed to have solved an intractable problem in mathematics. That company and Anthropic, where Coxon worked, are racing to multitrillion-dollar IPOs. An ongoing cultural battle about the moral and aesthetic vice of AI recently turned in on itself, when the journalist Kelsey Piper accused writers who mistrust Pangram, an AI-detection tool, of essentially trying to hide their own clandestine uses of AI. As I write this, Coxon’s post has been seen more than 150 million times, a number equal to roughly 2 percent of the global population. The situation might feel unsettling, validating, preposterous, or even stupid, depending on your perspective. AI companies and proponents have an incentive to make the technology seem powerful, even threatening, because power gives rise to profit. AI detractors have reason to take the AI extinction threat seriously as evidence that those in charge are irresponsible and reprobate. During the Cold War, cloak-and-dagger paranoia was spread by spies, agents of statecraft, confidants, whistleblowers, and journalists—either as truth to power or as crackpot conspiracy theories. But today, in the internet age, conspiracy theories are everyone’s primary method of exchange. Information’s tendency to spread harmfully, like disease—the original meaning of media “virality” facilitates further spread. Whether the threat Coxon has in mind is likely or even lucid is not important. Instead, the threat’s enticement has proved so lurid that people feel compelled to embrace it. That is different from saying that there is no threat. For example, the author Annie Jacobsen noted that the “superhuman system that can hack anything” that Coxon described could, in theory, hack the labs that store dangerous biological agents. If “what’s inside gets released,” Jacobsen wrote, “it’s goodbye humans.” Thinking of AI as a dangerous superintelligence capable of eradicating civilization is much more fulfilling than pondering the threat of a cluster of haphazard computer programs. We will probably never reclaim, if that is the right word for it, the lucidity of threat we felt at the Cold War’s climax. We do seem to long for that clarity, and the yearning for it has inspired us to patch back together some of its structure. The most satisfying dream of humankind is to be alive to witness its end. “I caused it” and “I prevented it” and “I tried to warn you” amount to equally appealing versions of that fantasy. Even “I posted about it” feels pretty good.","2026-09-13_001":"“9·11”25周年：改变美国的一天！如何催生了特朗普的“帝王式总统制”？ Twenty-five years after 9/11, the share of people with no direct recollection of the attacks is rising; just over 100m Americans, roughly a third of the population, were not alive at the time. Yet the counter-terror apparatus that President George W. Bush and Congress enacted in response—hastily drafted legal authorities to wage war, surveillance powers, permissive CIA operations and new bureaucracies—remains largely in place, even as the threat of jihadist violence has receded. Donald Trump’s adaptation of these powers may at last be seeding a debate about correcting the overreach. According to a new YouGov poll, 48% of Americans believe the expeditionary wars and surveillance that followed 9/11 have been worth the cost. But only 37% of Democrats agree, compared with 71% of Republicans. Talk of reform is growing in some Democratic circles. “We have an opportunity to rein in some of these authorities and remake agencies that have been abused,” says Dan Herman of the Centre for America Progress, a Democrat-aligned think-tank. America’s counter-terror regime arose initially with overwhelming support from Democrats and Republicans alike. When the hijacked planes hit the World Trade Centre and Pentagon, the nation braced for further onslaughts. Spooks fretted that al-Qaeda might use chemical, biological or even nuclear weapons. Terrorism became the primary concern of voters. But the dreaded wave of follow-up attacks never came. America launched expeditionary wars that ultimately cost $8trn, killed an estimated 900,000 people and may have provoked more terrorism than they prevented. Yet the initial counterattack in Afghanistan, followed by focused efforts against al-Qaeda, proved remarkably successful. Since 9/11 al-Qaeda has mustered just one fatal attack inside America, when a Saudi air-force officer killed three Americans at a naval base in 2019. The vast resources America injected into its counter-terror apparatus played a part. Congress created the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to “secure the United States from terrorist threats or attacks”. The number of FBI joint terrorism task-forces—which fuse intelligence across federal and local agencies—ballooned from 35 to over 200 today. Better intelligence detection and sharing snuffed out countless plots. Surveillance was a factor, such as Section 702, allowed spooks to snoop on foreign suspects abroad without a warrant, sweeping up Americans’ communications in their dragnet. Other statutes broadened the scope of the Foreign Terrorist Organisation (FTO) designation and bolstered the powers of the Treasury to go after the networks financing terrorist groups. Since returning to office, Mr Trump has cynically repurposed these tools. The president has dramatically broadened the definition of terrorism. His administration has branded a range of Latin American drug gangs as FTOs. His air campaign against suspected drug-smuggling boats in the Caribbean and Pacific has killed at least 277 people so far. Narco-terrorists are “the ISIS and al-Qaeda of the western hemisphere”, Pete Hegseth, the secretary of war, said recently, “and they should be treated as such”. The administration has used the 9/11 toolkit to advance its domestic agenda, too. DHS has become the engine of Mr Trump’s deportation campaign, with thousands of its agents, some of whom worked in counterterrorism, reassigned to immigration enforcement. A presidential directive signed in September 2025 orders the FBI’s terrorism task-forces to track loose, left-wing movements like Antifa, which the administration has labelled “domestic terrorists”. The State Department has designated Antifa’s overseas offshoots as FTOs and pressed allies to follow suit. The administration is also pulling powerful financial counter-terror levers. In Minneapolis, for example, federal agents used authorities created by the Patriot Act of 2001 to seize financial records from labour unions and progressive groups without warrants. In Texas, eight anti-ICE protesters were sentenced in June on spurious charges of “providing material” support to terrorists, a statute designed for al-Qaeda and ISIS financiers. Attempts at reform have so far been embryonic. Congress let Section 702 lapse in June, in part over concerns that Mr Trump is politicising the intelligence apparatus, though the authority will run until early 2027. What happens then will test the reform caucus in Congress. Democrats would like stronger safeguards, but have been unable to break through from their minority position in the legislature. Tim Kaine, a Democratic senator from Virginia, has called on Congress to repeal the 2001 AUMF, but has struggled to garner support. Perhaps it will take a younger congressional intake, for whom 9/11 is a hazier memory, to move things along. Americans who came of age during the past 25 years, notes Mr Herman, “are eager to push back against the abuses that we’re all seeing.”","2026-11-01_001":"特朗普重返白宫背后，藏着一个你可能从没听过的男人！中期选举还能复制奇迹吗？ They are both young, bald and fathers of four. They are also the brains behind Donald Trump. But while Stephen Miller is infamous as the US president’s ideological enforcer, James Blair could, in the words of Time magazine, “be the most important person in Donald Trump’s Washington you’ve never heard of”. Blair has sufficiently impressed Trump to earn the nickname “Brilliant James”. And a well-positioned source told the Guardian: “I would say that James is probably twice, five times, 10 times more effective than Stephen Miller.” Blair, 37, is a data-driven political operative who played a key role in Trump’s historic comeback to win the 2024 presidential election. Now he faces his stiffest challenge yet: helping Republicans hold the House of Representatives and Senate in the next month’s midterm elections. Blair cut his political teeth in the partisan pressure cooker of Florida politics. At 22, he was interviewed for a position under Richard Corcoran, then a powerbroker and future speaker of the Florida house of representatives. By 2018, Blair was serving as senior adviser to the gubernatorial campaign of Republican Ron DeSantis and joined his administration as deputy chief of staff, where he forged his defining alliance with political strategist Susie Wiles, now Trump’s chief of staff. Wiles hired Blair again for Trump’s improbable bid to regain the White House. In March 2024, Blair became political director of Trump’s presidential campaign, helping shape its voter-targeting strategy. Blair’s core contribution to the 2024 campaign was his radical willingness to discard Washington’s conventional wisdom. While traditional operations rely on costly brick-and-mortar field offices and heavy television advertising, Blair argued that broadcast television was losing influence to digital-first strategies, taking the bold step of both closing field offices and outsourcing get-out-the-vote operations to independent super political action committees (Pacs). “He took a lot of abuse, but the president’s comfortable win was read as a confirmation of Blair’s data-driven approach.” After Trump’s second inauguration, Blair entered the White House as a deputy chief of staff, bringing political affairs, intergovernmental affairs and legislative affairs under his control, giving him an unusually broad portfolio. The president told a holiday reception last year: “I’d hear from other people he’s a total political genius, but I didn’t see it. And you know what? He’s not quiet. He’s incredible – the job you’ve done, thank you, James.” As the 2026 midterms approached, Blair stepped away from the White House to take charge of the political action committee Maga Inc, taking the lead in the effort to preserve Republican control of Congress. By the end of August, it had $415m in the bank. But Blair is aware of the economic headwinds facing the party. Whipple recalls a conversation with Blair in December regarding the midterm outlook. “Blair said essentially that it doesn’t matter what we do about Jeffrey Epstein or any of the other problems we have. If we don’t do something about affordability, we are fucked. And so here we are.” As election day approaches, Blair’s data models have run headlong into the realities of midterm politics. Trump is not on the ballot, making voters who are less likely to turn out in midterm elections notoriously difficult to mobilise compared with a presidential cycle. Blair’s political headaches grew earlier this month when it was revealed that he and LaCivita travelled to Republika Srpska, a self-governing region of Bosnia and Herzegovina. The New York Times reported that the pair received payments to advise the regional government before local elections. Trump distanced himself from both men, describing them as “outside consultants” in a 7 October Truth Social post and warning that if he found a conflict of interest, he would “terminate them, immediately!”. Blair responded defensively to the criticism, issuing a rare public statement. “All of this mudslinging is ridiculous, and counterproductive,” he insisted. The setback came as little surprise to those who believe Blair had been riding for a fall. One source who worked closely with Blair for many years said: “He’s an arrogant asshole who always thinks he’s the smartest guy in the room. His arrogance will result in the worst Republican election result for 20 years.” That view is rejected by Blair. “I work for candidates whose cause I believe in. I work intensely hard, and I don’t apologise for that.” In the meantime, on 3 November voters will determine whether Blair can defy the odds again. If the results are as catastrophic for Republicans as opinion polls suggest, he could face the wrath of a capricious president, looking for a scapegoat. “Brilliant James” could find himself with a less desirable nickname. “James Blair can be a raging political genius, and it’s not going to change that Donald Trump is an insane person.”","2026-10-31_001":"The Americans changing politics for the better — 2026/10/31 The Americans changing politics for the better Midterm elections are always a referendum on the sitting American president, but rarely has an incumbent so wilfully put himself in the spotlight in defiance of his own unpopularity. As the campaign ends, Donald Trump is commandeering rallies in Republican strongholds, a sign of his party’s vulnerability and his unwavering faith in his own star power. Away from the spotlight, however, something more surprising—and more important—has been happening: Hispanics and Asians have emerged as America’s most valuable voters. The curse of American politics is its bitter partisanship. As voters have sorted themselves along racial, educational and geographical lines, the parties have spent less time trying to win converts from the other side and more whipping their own supporters into a permanent frenzy: 70% of Republicans are hostile towards Democrats and more than 80% of Democrats return the favour. The result is a style of politics that tugs public life towards the extremes, punishes compromise and puts getting people angry above getting things done. Hispanic and Asian-American voters offer the best hope of arresting this baleful trend. They have become America’s crucial swing voters, with an outsize impact on races across the country. Compared with other blocs, their numbers are growing faster, their ideology is more fluid and their priorities are less divisive. That means they are likely to be good for America not just in these midterms, but also for many elections to come. No group captures the potential for opening up politics as much as Hispanics and Asian-Americans. One reason is demography. They are a growing fraction of the population—especially Hispanics, who are three times more numerous than Asian-Americans. Since 2006 their combined share of the electorate has increased by nine percentage points, to reach 21%. They are also highly diverse. The grandchildren of an immigrant from Ho Chi Minh City have a different outlook from a new arrival from Bangalore. Neither fits neatly into the familiar Democrat-Republican template. And they are more likely to switch their votes. In 2012 Democrats thought they had a lock on Latinos and Asians. In 2024 the story was how they were becoming Trumpian. It has now become clear that they were swing voters all along. White voters changed little between 2020 and 2024, while both Latinos and Asians lurched hard to the right. They were then the fastest to turn against Mr Trump in early 2025. The margin of support for Democrats has risen by 17 points among Latinos and 14 among Asians since 2024, compared with ten for whites and five for black voters. Part of the explanation for this is that these voters are the epitome of striving Americans who want to work hard and get ahead. Higher prices for food and fuel hit Hispanic voters particularly hard; housing costs affected Asian voters. Latinos are more than twice as likely to start businesses as white voters. Asians are likely to spend more time in education. Many are recent arrivals or the children of immigrants, so their opinions have not yet hardened along party lines. In contrast to the rest of the voting population, if Hispanics and Asians hold one left-leaning position, they will not necessarily endorse others. Likewise, wanting a secure southern border does not equate to support for the hardline enforcement that has frightened Hispanic employees into staying away from work. Instead, many are moderate. They have little truck with culture wars or polarisation. The economic policies they care about diverge from the tribal messages the parties use to stir up their bases. Whereas policies on abortion and trans rights are set at the extremes, economic management is suited to the swing voter’s pragmatic preference for the common good. Almost anything that gives the parties a reason to appeal to the centre will benefit America, especially when it involves the virtues of hard work and prosperity. Democrats will need a winning economic message and a coherent plan for immigration. Republicans should reject their ferocious deportations and the ugly ultranationalism that denigrates Hispanic and Asian citizens for not being “heritage Americans”. All Americans should therefore welcome these new swing voters. Thanks to them, fewer elections will be decided by hard-core party members in primaries, and more by the average voter in the general election. If politicians win more credit by accomplishing something in government, they will perhaps spend less of their time demonising the other side. If the economy seems to matter more and culture wars less, America will be more prosperous and better governed. Hispanic and Asian immigrants want the chance to thrive. Their votes will help ensure America thrives with them.","2026-10-30_001":"Spain's Sánchez declares three days of mourning after at least 68 killed in Toledo train crash — 2026/10/30 Spain's Sánchez declares three days of mourning after at least 68 killed in Toledo train crash The Spanish prime minister has declared three days of mourning after at least 68 people were killed and dozens more injured in a collision between two high-speed trains that officials suggested was caused by an \"inexplicable\" error at the central command centre. The trains, one from Spain's state-owned Renfe and the other from the private rail company Iryo, were travelling on Wednesday morning in the same direction on the line that stretches from the Spanish capital, Madrid, to the southern region of Andalusia. After the Iryo train came to a halt, the other slammed into its rear, knocking several carriages off the track near the town of Urda. Josimar Medina woke a little before 9am on Wednesday to find himself airborne and hurtling through his carriage on the fast train from Madrid to Seville. \"I remember flying through the air and looking for my girlfriend, who was also shooting through the air,\" said Medina, a 37-year-old tourist from the northern Peruvian city of Chiclayo. Once he landed, he looked around and realised just how lucky he and his girlfriend, Vitoria Maia, had been. \"I saw people with cuts, people covered in blood, people missing limbs, and I saw one person who had nothing left below the chest,\" said Medina. The Peruvian stood recounting the details of Wednesday's deadly rail crash, in which at least 68 people were killed and dozens more were injured, wearing a light brown T-shirt stained with the blood of a young American man he had helped move to safety. The couple climbed out of the train and helped the injured while they waited for medics and Guardia Civil officers to arrive at the scene of the crash, which took place amid vineyards and olive groves near the small town of Urda. Fredrich and Arabella Barilea, a couple from the Philippines who now live in Dubai, were on the Iryo train, and Fredrich was sent flying by the impact. \"One guy was shouting: 'Get out! Get out! Another train could hit!'\" said Fredrich. \"We saw all the people were also getting off. Many of them had head injuries and were covered in blood. We just got as far away from the train as we could.\" \"It all happened in a split second,\" said Arabella. \"The table we were sitting at just disappeared in the crash. I feel like we're on our second life.\" Spain's transport minister, Óscar Puente, said that rear-end collisions like that which had taken place on Wednesday were \"highly unlikely\" and suggested that the accident may have been caused by human error made in \"inexplicable\" circumstances. He said the LZB signalling system, which automatically halts a train if the line ahead is occupied, had gone down at about 8.30am local time due to a computer malfunction. As a result, an estimated six trains had been halted along a stretch of around 63km of track, he said. The task of monitoring the rail lines then fell to a 24-hour command centre in Madrid. Minutes after the computer malfunction, the centre instructed at least two of the trains to continue as normal, despite the fact that there was still a \"stop signal\" in place, said Puente. \"It was the control centre, through its operators, that made the decision to resume traffic, under circumstances that, from our point of view, are inexplicable,\" he said. \"Clearly, it was a wrong decision.\" Giving initial details about the crash, Puente said the Iryo train had been travelling ahead on the tracks and had been sitting stationary when the Renfe train was instructed by the command centre to resume its journey. The Renfe train then travelled approximately 25km before colliding with the Iryo train about 17 minutes later. The control centre \"had more than enough time to verify the error, and they did not fix it\", Puente added. Puente said that similar erroneous instructions had been given to at least one other train on Wednesday morning. \"And it was the engineer of one of the trains who corrected the command centre and told them he can't resume normal operation – it has to be [an] on-sight operation,\" Puente said. The tragedy was the second major deadly high-speed train accident this year in Spain, rattling a country where the extensive rail network has long been a point of pride. In January, 46 people were killed and dozens more injured after two trains – again, one from Renfe and one from Iryo – collided on the same line near the town of Adamuz in Andalucía. But, as also happened at Adamuz, the solidarity and kindness on display is likely to turn to anger over the coming hours as people seek answers as to how one of Europe's most modern and extensive high-speed rail networks could experience two deadly collisions that have between them claimed at least 114 lives in the space of just 10 months. The opposition People's party leader, Alberto Núñez Feijóo, called for an immediate investigation into what had happened, as well as a \"complete and independent review\" of Spain's entire rail network. \"Spain has the right to know what is going on with its infrastructure,\" he told reporters.","2026-10-29_001":"Don't expect supersize profits from Anthropic soon — 2026/10/29 Don't expect supersize profits from Anthropic soon Breakneck growth is commonplace in Silicon Valley. The rise of Anthropic is something else. In the first quarter of last year—when Claude Code, its hit programming tool, was first released—the artificial-intelligence lab pulled in $376m of revenue. By the second quarter of this year that figure had risen 30-fold, to $11.6bn, exceeding corporate mainstays such as McDonald's. In a few weeks this darling of the AI revolution will undergo an initial public offering (IPO) that will be, if not the largest of all time, then certainly close to it. On October 26th it publicly filed supporting paperwork that gave the outside world a closer look at its finances. Anthropic's growth is all the more remarkable because, unlike many zippy startups, it has already reached profitability. In the second quarter it eked out an operating profit of just under $1bn, having lost $1.7bn in the preceding three months. The company is particularly eager to draw attention to its spending on computing power, which fell from 251% of sales in the first quarter of 2025 to just 48% in the second quarter of 2026. Anthropic claims that improvements in its AI models have made it faster and more efficient to develop and serve its technology, allowing the company to make the most of its limited resources. But can Anthropic's stunning performance continue? A closer look at its filing suggests caution is warranted: growth is slowing; the company is paying higher prices to access scarce computing power; and a resulting shift towards directly purchasing AI chips will guzzle up much of the cashflow Anthropic would otherwise be on course to generate. Earlier investors who had hoped for a public valuation as high as $2trn may thus be disappointed. Anthropic's scorching revenue growth has put a target on its back. Claude Code made it an early leader in the race to automate programming. Tech giants such as Meta and Microsoft became huge customers, spending billions of dollars on Anthropic's technology. More recently those customers have been pushing staff to use internally developed alternatives. At the same time, OpenAI, Anthropic's arch-rival, has been gaining ground with its own offering, Codex, the daily usage of which has risen ten fold since early August. And after the short-lived craze for \"tokenmaxxing\" earlier this year, companies of all stripes have been more closely monitoring their spending on AI and imposing limits on their engineers. This month Anthropic told shareholders during a private gathering that revenue in the third quarter was up by about 45% from the preceding three months, a third of the rate it achieved in the second quarter. In its IPO prospectus Anthropic warns that, as the market matures, growth could slow \"more rapidly than we anticipate\". Moreover, it has become increasingly reliant on the tech giants to push its products. Almost half of Anthropic's revenue in the first six months of this year came from sales made through the cloud businesses of Alphabet, Amazon and Microsoft. Anthropic's spending on sales, marketing and distribution partnerships such as these chewed up 19% of revenue in the first half of 2026, up from 10% in the same period a year before. Another problem Anthropic faces is that, like other AI developers, it is struggling to secure the computing power it needs. In its prospectus it warns investors that its use of \"short-term compute\" could go up in the near term, weighing on margins. For example, Anthropic has agreed to pay SpaceX about $3.1bn per month to use its Nvidia chips into 2029. (Anthropic is able to back out from early next year with 90 days' notice.) That explains why Anthropic is starting to develop more of its own computing infrastructure, rather than relying solely on cloud providers. This strategy should help keep operating costs under control, but also provides forewarning that the lab will become a more capital-intensive business in the long run. The initiative, called \"Anthropic Direct\", involves procuring chips from providers such as Broadcom, which develops custom silicon, and installing them in rented data centres. That could make it harder to start throwing off the kind of free cashflow investors might hope for, and require the business to raise more capital. In Anthropic's prospectus, its founders argue that its spending plans \"may seem irresponsibly large at first glance\", but are justified by the exponential trajectory that AI is on. \"We will aim to be bold but sane,\" they write. With a technology so mind-bending, striking that balance may prove surprisingly difficult. ■","2026-10-28_001":"The Great Louvre Robbery Was Just the Start. It's Been the Year of the Heist. — 2026/10/28 The Great Louvre Robbery Was Just the Start. It's Been the Year of the Heist. In the year since thieves stunned the world by smashing their way into the Louvre Museum and stealing France's crown jewels worth at least $101 million, art heists have only intensified. From Wales to Sicily, more than 14 museums have been hit by smash-and-grab criminals since early 2025, suggesting museums haven't cracked the code on how to safely protect their treasures. Police are still looking for the Louvre's stolen trove of 19th-century jewelry. Experts have myriad theories about why art heists are increasing, from soaring gold prices making golden antiquities and precious gems more valuable to the misguided notion that stolen art can reap higher sums on the black market—it cannot. But museum directors and security firms agree the Louvre breach set an ominous precedent. \"Louvre was a wake-up call that anywhere can be hit,\" said Chris Marinello, chief executive of Art Recovery International. For its part, the Louvre created a new security coordinator position now held by Olivier Goupil, a veteran police officer. The museum also added more crowd-control barriers and anti-ram devices around its exterior. There are now window railings at the upper-level Apollo Gallery where thieves broke in. In a statement, the museum said the challenge is to balance its mission of protecting its collection while also sharing it with roughly 30,000 visitors a day. \"Museums are not fortresses,\" the museum said in an email. The Louvre heist revealed that thieves are increasingly stealing gold, bronze and jewels more than traditional paintings—a turnaround from a century ago when a thief stole its \"Mona Lisa,\" later recovered. In 10 of the subsequent museum break-ins across Europe over the past year, thieves bypassed paintings to steal objects that could be dismantled or melt down, because such commodities retain value as precious metals and gems. Black-market jewelers are often willing to re-cut and reshape bigger jewels into smaller untraceable ones that can be resold anywhere, investigators say. On Oct. 5, thieves broke into the C. Karoutzos Contemporary Art Center in central France and hauled off a 330-pound bronze book cover decorated with gold, gems and shells designed by surrealist Salvador Dalí. Museum owner Christian Karoutzos said his video cameras captured a few shadowy figures leaving the scene, but the book cover remains missing. On the other hand, authorities have had better success recovering stolen paintings. It likely helped that the works were identifiable and only valuable if left intact, Marinello said. \"You can't melt down a Renoir.\" In 1990, two men disguised as police officers spent 81 minutes crisscrossing Boston's Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum to cut out and carry off masterpieces by Vermeer and Rembrandt. This year's heist at the Magnani Rocca took less than three minutes. Geoff Kelly, a former FBI agent, said thieves are breaking in, grabbing what they can and fleeing the scene within minutes—before the police arrive. In July, Paris's Museum of Natural History suffered an undetected cyberattack that disabled its alarm and surveillance systems, police said. Two months later, robbers monitoring the digital breakdown were able to saw through an emergency exit door and use blowtorches and other weapons to steal about 13 pounds of gold worth around $2 million. An earlier audit of the museum's security protocols found that the Louvre's video surveillance and physical security systems relied on a weak password, \"LOUVRE.\" It was immediately changed, the museum said. Marinello said thieves are beginning to use artificial intelligence to identify museums with hefty holdings of gold or those housed in smaller, historic buildings that might be easier to breach. It's \"something that should scare the bejesus out of museum directors,\" he said. As an experiment, Marinello asked an AI tool to help him steal a painting; the tool refused to participate in a possible crime but still assisted him in locating rope and sledgehammers and other gear from the nearest hardware stores. The flip side: Museums say they're using AI-assisted video surveillance to help guards scour for bad actors by analyzing people's behavior as they move through the galleries rather than recording footage passively. Victimized museums are often compelled to cope by closing indefinitely or reopening with fewer treasures on view. The Louvre has reopened its Apollo Gallery, but all the remaining jewelry in it has been removed, resulting in an empty, ceremonial 17th-century gallery. At the Louvre, staff are still discussing when and how to put the theft's lone survivor back on public view: Empress Eugenie's 19th-century crown, which thieves dropped and crumpled outside the museum during their getaway. Conservators say one of its eight golden eagles is missing and so are 10 of its 1,354 diamonds, but its 56 emeralds and central globe survived. \"It will be exhibited at the Louvre when we have found the ideal location to create a dedicated, secure exhibition space,\" the museum said, \"but for now, not all conditions are in place.\"","2026-10-27_001":"How a Trillion-Dollar Industry Launders Illegal Gold — 2026/10/27 How a Trillion-Dollar Industry Launders Illegal Gold In an industry riddled with abuse, the world's banks and governments rely on one entity to keep gangsters, warlords and human rights violators from selling gold into the mainstream market. That group, the London Bullion Market Association, is the self-described \"global authority for precious metals.\" But a New York Times investigation found that the association has repeatedly allowed gold into the market that has been mined by criminals, cartel members and children. Refiners lie with impunity about the origins of their gold. And in the case of Madagascar, the L.B.M.A. helped change and backdate key paperwork that certified gold as free from abuse. The Times investigation shows that the divide between \"clean\" and \"dirty\" gold is an industry fiction. There is just gold. And as Wall Street, central banks and investors stockpile and trade the precious metal, they're profiting off a supply that includes gold from illegal sources. The price this year crossed the $5,000 mark. That has made it profitable to mine in inefficient, destructive and politically destabilizing ways. The industry works like this: Miners and brokers sell raw gold to refiners, which melt it, remove impurities, and form it into bars. The major international marketplace accepts bars only from the 67 L.B.M.A.-certified refineries. Those refiners must document the source of every ounce of gold in every bar. But for all its talk about being a global authority, the L.B.M.A. is mostly a paperwork collector. The association relies on refiners to inspect the mines and to self-report the details. Madagascar's central bank decided to capitalize on booming prices by buying and stockpiling gold, including from illegal mines that employ children. To see how it happened, I began at the mines themselves. I took a propeller plane from the capital, Antananarivo, and drove three hours through farmland to a mine called Madagold. The mine sits in a dry valley in the northern part of the island, surrounded by a busy market fueled by gold-rush cash. Everywhere I looked, at Madagold and at mines across the country, children were in danger. Children in Madagascar's mines crushed rocks and hauled buckets of ore. Toddlers wandered between 100-foot-deep shafts. In the gold-rich northern region of Diana, miners follow no laws, the government's regional mining supervisor, Firmin Razafindratondra, said. All of the area's gold mining, he said, is illegal. Outside Mananjary, on Madagascar's east coast, miners operated despite government shutdown orders. Excavators turned rice paddies into a giant pit. For that gold to be worth anything, it needed to be \"certified L.B.M.A.,\" Madagascar's central bank governor, Aivo Andrianarivelo, said. So he dispatched his gold chief to Switzerland to find a refiner. Refiners questioned the gold's origins and legality. \"Nobody wanted to touch this gold,\" said Marc Ummel, a gold-supply-chain specialist at the nonprofit group SwissAid. Small-scale mines are considered red flags. The L.B.M.A.'s rules say that any refiner accepting this gold would be required to conduct mine inspections. \"It's impossible,\" Mr. Ummel remembers telling Madagascar's banker. \"You will never be able to find an L.B.M.A. refiner.\" On that point, he was wrong. The central bank cut a deal in 2023 with the Istanbul Gold Refinery, one of the world's biggest L.B.M.A.-certified refiners. The Turkish refiner's auditor filed paperwork with the L.B.M.A. claiming that it \"does not do any business\" with mines that raise red flags. That claim freed the company from having to conduct inspections. The raw gold was melted into 12 kg bars at a plant next to Madagascar's international airport. From there, tens of millions of dollars in gold was shipped to Turkey to enter the legitimate supply. It was only by chance that anyone at the L.B.M.A. took a look at the Madagascar gold. The European Union happened to be considering certifying the association for a new oversight role. An E.U. inspector noticed the Madagascar deal and began scrutinizing it. How, the inspector asked, could a refinery process gold from Madagascar yet claim no red flags? L.B.M.A. staff escalated the problem to a \"compliance panel\" that included the chief executive, Ms. Crowell. The association could have punished the refiner. Instead, the L.B.M.A. helped cover up the problem. Association officials worked with the Istanbul Gold Refinery's auditor to create a new report that made it seem like the refiner had disclosed the red flags from the beginning and had taken steps to scrutinize the supply chain. The report was backdated, as if it had been filed in real time. That backdated report remains on the L.B.M.A.'s website, with no indication of any alterations. The matter was closed. The gold was certified as meeting the world's most rigorous standards. Now those same refiners who had turned Madagascar away are calling looking to buy gold, Mr. Andrianarivelo said, because the L.B.M.A. had declared that \"there was no issue\" with it.","2026-10-26_001":"US has for years asked Musk to alter Starlink service in Ukraine, Middle East, sources say — 2026/10/26 US has for years asked Musk to alter Starlink service in Ukraine, Middle East, sources say Well before the throttling this month of Ukraine's military access to Elon Musk's satellite communications system, the Biden and Trump administrations had several times asked SpaceX to turn its Starlink service on or off in various countries, according to six former senior US officials familiar with the matter. During the presidency of Joe Biden, the requests included switching off the service to prevent Iranian proxy militias in Yemen and Lebanon from using Starlink to communicate, and activating the service to provide internet access inside Iran and in Ukraine to support its military. Similarly, President Donald Trump's administration asked Musk, who is CEO of SpaceX, to make the service available in Iran following US-Israeli strikes in 2025 and 2026, in hopes it would bolster internet access and communications for anti government protesters. The recent blowup over restrictions to Ukraine's use of SpaceX satellite services has raised questions about the ties between the US government and Musk, whose company owns systems vital to the US military and to US allies. The Biden-era and Trump requests to Musk show a close relationship in which US administrations rely on an unprecedented degree of a private-sector company to help implement policy. The recent limits on Ukraine, despite being a US ally, have also rattled officials in Europe, some of whom argue that Europe must develop its own military satellite communications capabilities because no viable short-term alternative currently exists. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said last week that his country's access to Starshield, the military version of the Starlink system, had been intentionally limited, interfering with the performance of the country's military drones. He also said the speeds were reduced for Starlink, which Ukraine uses for some military activities including defense against Russian missile attacks. The White House, contacted for comment, referred Reuters to Trump's remarks on Friday, when he said he had not spoken with Musk about Starlink access issues for Ukraine and that \"if there is a problem, I'll get it straightened out.\" SpaceX did not respond to requests for comment, although Musk said in a social media post earlier this week: \"Starshield systems are owned and controlled by the US government. Any limits on speed or usage are decided by the US military and ultimately (by the president of the United States).\" For Ukraine, the Starlink internet service has become the lifeblood of frontline communication between units, who say there is currently no rival to the capabilities of SpaceX's products. Starshield receivers, largely impervious to radio jamming, are used as onboard guidance systems and to provide links to a remote pilot for Ukrainian drones, including deep behind Russian lines inside Ukraine. Musk's role as the strong-willed CEO of a company with outsized influence over US national security has at times raised questions over who is making decisions about SpaceX's satellite services. The Biden administration at times relied on Musk to help implement policy. Biden officials coordinated with SpaceX to thwart US adversaries by cutting the service, and to help allies by expanding Starlink's availability. They added that discussions between the government and SpaceX often focused on preventing groups such as the Iranian-linked Houthis in Yemen and Hezbollah in Lebanon from using Starlink terminals. In October 2023, during fighting between Israel and Hezbollah militants in southern Lebanon, intelligence officials told the White House that individuals linked to Hezbollah could have gained access to Starlink terminals. Officials had similar concerns about the Houthis in Yemen in 2024 during a US air campaign against the Iran-linked group. In both cases, the administration contacted SpaceX and asked it to identify the terminals and shut them off. At least some of the terminals were deactivated. In a 2024 submission to the US Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security, SpaceX said it works closely with US government agencies to secure export authorizations where required and to safeguard technology in support of US national security objectives and foreign policy goals. Soon after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Biden officials pushed SpaceX to expand Starlink use for the Ukrainian military. In a separate instance, the Biden administration asked SpaceX to turn on Starlink in Iran during a protest movement launched in September 2022 after the death of Mahsa Amini, an Iranian arrested for not wearing her hijab properly. Starlink terminals were moved into the country with the help of the administration. The Trump administration took a similar step earlier this year and discussed with Musk and SpaceX getting Starlink terminals into Iran and activated following US-Israeli strikes in the country in June 2025 and again in February 2026. Neither the Trump administration nor SpaceX has publicly confirmed that those terminals were turned on. The Wall Street Journal reported in February that the US government had sent thousands of Starlink terminals into Iran.","2026-10-25_001":"China has captured the market for hobbyist 3D printers — 2026/10/25 China has captured the market for hobbyist 3D printers The fastest-growing industrial product in China today is not the electric vehicle, lithium-ion battery or even humanoid robot. It is the humble 3D printer for hobbyists. Once geeky curios, 3D printers have quietly become one of China's most successful consumer electronics. Exports of the machines grew by 90% in the first half of this year. In the past decade China's global market share has risen from 19% to 96%, ending the dominance of Western firms. The personal 3D-printing market is now split among the \"four little dragons of Shenzhen\", says Chris Connery of Context, a London-based market-intelligence firm. Creality, Anycubic, Elegoo and the industry's leader, Bambu Lab, reign supreme globally (see chart). Their success shows, yet again, how fierce competition in China combined with generous government support can help improve quality and drive down export prices, making Chinese products hard to beat. The printers work by building objects layer by layer, usually by extruding molten plastic. Invented by Chuck Hull, an American, in 1983, they long remained expensive, unreliable and difficult to use. Today they routinely sell for a little more than $500 and have the sleek aluminium finish of an Apple computer. Comparable entry-level printers from Western firms start at more than double the price. Artificial intelligence allows users to create print designs for such things as tools, parts or toys by describing them in natural language rather than using complicated design-software. Just as Chinese factories helped to turn drones into recreational devices (as well as game-changers in warfare) by achieving large-scale production at low cost, so they are now doing with 3D printers. For most of its history, consumer 3D printing had an unspoken cost of entry: patience. Getting a printer to actually work often took longer than using it. Bed leveling, calibration, and trial and error troubleshooting weren't occasional annoyances; they were simply part of owning one. That was the deal, and for years, nobody in the industry seriously challenged it. Part of Bambu Lab's different approach can be traced back to its founding team. Several of its engineers came from DJI, the drone manufacturer, with backgrounds in robotics and consumer electronics. Drones present a similar design challenge to 3D printers: they're mechanically complex, but successful consumer products need to be usable without specialized training. Bambu Lab's founders had already worked through that kind of problem before applying it to printing. The company also didn't rush its first product. It spent 22 months on research and development before releasing its first printer, the X1, which launched on Kickstarter and raised about HK$55 million, or roughly $7 million, from 5,575 backers. The core of Bambu Lab's approach is automating steps that previously required manual adjustment. Its printers include cameras, LiDAR, and gravity sensors that handle bed leveling and calibration automatically on every print, without requiring the user to understand the underlying mechanics. LatePost described the result as \"a small AI-enabled robot.\" One Bambu Lab owner on Reddit summarized the difference simply: \"Bambu printers just work.\" Price wars have eaten into the printer dragons' profits even as sales boom. But the firms still excite investors. Bambu Lab was founded in 2020 by alumni of another Chinese company, DJI, the world's leading dronemaker. The printer-maker is reported to have a private valuation of about $10bn. Last year DJI itself invested in Elegoo, a Bambu rival. Creality listed on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange in May. It boasts a market capitalisation of HK$12bn ($1.5bn). The government cherishes 3D-printing firms. The business featured in its \"Made in China 2025\" industrial plan, which was launched in 2015 to ensure domestic strength and self-reliance in high-tech manufacturing (much to the West's concern). Officials see 3D printers as a way to produce components of aerospace, health care and military equipment, for which China often relies on Western suppliers. Policymakers have directed research institutes and state-run firms to buy them. Some competitors say Chinese firms have profited from Western open-source printing software. Last month an American court ruled that Bambu Lab had infringed on four patents held by an American company, and ordered it to pay $27.6m in damages. Although Chinese firms have captured the consumer market, they have struggled to do so for industrial 3D printers. Firms are wary of relying on Chinese products if it involves feeding them with sensitive designs (though Britain's defence ministry has been using Bambu printers, its government admits). Outside China, top-of-the-range machines are still mostly supplied by American and European outfits. They can sell for millions of dollars. Undeterred, Chinese suppliers are creating a new form of manufacturing at home and abroad. They are pioneering \"3D-printing farms\"—facilities with as many as 5,000 desktop 3D printers that operate like factories. Several have been built in China this year. Printing farms can mass-produce trinkets for zany consumer trends in short order, replacing expensive and slow injection-moulding techniques. Like the printers themselves, their output will be higher quality and cheaper than what came before.","2026-10-24_001":"Singapore goes after companies over toxic haze — 2026/10/24 Singapore goes after companies over toxic haze Singapore is trying to hold companies to account as it grapples with its worst air pollution in a decade, but the smog-choked city-state is running into the limits of prosecuting cross-border environmental laws. The government has in recent days threatened legal action against 10 businesses, several with deep ties to Singapore, that it says are behind wildfires in neighbouring Indonesia that have produced the haze. It has also raised questions about whether other companies listed on the local bourse may be involved, and has threatened to target company profits, access to financing and standing with investors. \"Singapore — which wants to continue being an international finance centre — gets a lot of business from these companies,\" said Ivan Png at the National University of Singapore, who has campaigned for anti-haze legislation. He added that the companies \"depend on Singapore for equity and financing — you would think that would make them want to pay attention to what the government is saying\". The issue has also threatened a rift with Indonesia, where the fires are blamed on slash-and-burn practices by paper and palm oil producers. This year's fires have been exacerbated by a \"super\" El Niño, a natural weather phenomenon that has raised temperatures and reduced rainfall across Indonesia. Singapore's air quality has been classified by the government as \"very unhealthy\" this month, at times ranking the worst in the world, according to monitoring platform IQAir. Outdoor activities and sports events have been cancelled and hospital admissions for respiratory complaints have surged. The smog cast a pall over this month's Singapore Grand Prix. Public frustration is mounting with what has become a regular nuisance in the city-state that generally prides itself on effective governance. Singapore has taken steps to try to bring companies suspected of field burning — which is also illegal in Indonesia — to book. In 2014, it introduced a Transboundary Haze Pollution Act, which empowered the government to pursue criminal charges against companies found to have contributed to haze-producing fires and levy fines as high as S$100,000 (US$78,000) a day, capped at S$2mn (US$1.6mn) per conviction. But initial attempts to enforce the law stalled, with the environment ministry complaining that its Indonesian counterpart did not provide the necessary information to pursue charges. No company has been fined under the law. This year, Singapore has vowed to toughen its stance. In recent days, the government has served legal notices on 10 companies — identified using maps, meteorological data and satellite imagery — to demand they stop burning. Three of the businesses were served similar notices in 2015. \"If companies refuse to co-operate, regardless of whether they have a presence in Singapore... we will use every legal and regulatory lever at our disposal to exert pressure where it matters.\" At least four of the 10 companies are suppliers to Asia Pulp and Paper, which is headquartered in Singapore and part of Indonesian conglomerate Sinar Mas. Two others are run by permanent Singapore residents. Shanmugam said the government would investigate APP and several other companies listed in Singapore, where many Indonesian conglomerates access global capital. \"Some of the fires or smoke-source points seem to be associated with Singapore-listed First Resources and Bumitama Agri, as well as First Borneo Plantations,\" he said. \"We will be asking more questions of these companies.\" Shanmugam added that banks that lent to the businesses had also questioned them, and the Monetary Authority of Singapore, the city-state's financial regulator, would track lenders' risk exposure. First Resources said it welcomed the investigation, adding it was \"confident that an objective review of the available evidence will prove that it does not engage in any land clearing by fire, whether directly or indirectly\". APP, Bumitama Agri and First Borneo Plantations did not respond to requests for comment. SGX, the local bourse, has separately asked seven listed companies — including Golden-Agri Resources, Wilmar and Kencana Agri — about possible links to the haze. Indonesia's police chief said this week that authorities had named 11 corporations, three of them foreign, as suspects in connection with the fires. \"Some countries are mocking, insulting us... when many of their companies [operating] here are responsible,\" Indonesia's President Prabowo Subianto said in a speech on Thursday, adding: \"We will announce those names when the time comes.\" He did not identify the countries. Indonesia's government communication agency said: \"Indonesia and Singapore maintain channels of engagement on environmental issues, including transboundary pollution.\" It added that it prioritises \"the protection of affected communities, the containment of fires, and effective enforcement of environmental regulations\". NUS professor Png called for stronger laws to provide an incentive to whistleblowers, pointing to successful efforts in the US to combat bribery under the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act. \"It is very hard to collect evidence,\" he said. \"But when you offer incentives, whistleblowers will come out.\"","2026-10-23_001":"How a Sydney professor built an AI army to scam the scammers — 2026/10/23 How a Sydney professor built an AI army to scam the scammers The day that Dali Kaafar decided to start a company that unleashes an autonomous army of AI bots to take on the trillion-dollar global scam industry began like any other. It was a warm Saturday afternoon in spring. The Macquarie University professor of cybersecurity was enjoying a picnic with his wife and two children at a Sydney park. At some point, his mobile rang. Kaafar could tell from the number that it was a scam call, and while he usually ignored them, on this particular day, Kaafar decided to have some fun with the fraudsters. He picked up the call. \"The kids were a few years younger then, and for their entertainment, I pretended not to understand what the scammers were after, or I would give them the wrong details,\" he says. The scammers persisted, insisting that there was something wrong with Kaafar's internet and they needed remote access to his computer. Kaafar feigned incomprehension when they spoke about his tepid upload and download speeds. He distracted and delayed them: what was this NBN they spoke of? \"At one point, I think I was even trying to order a pizza,\" he says. \"As I hung up that call I remember very clearly saying, 'this was all great fun, but imagine what technology can do with these scammers at scale,'\" he says. While his children were in hysterics, his wife was less impressed. \"My wife said, 'those are 44 minutes of your own life that you will never get back.' You ruined the whole picnic experience. I said, 'That's even more reason for technology to jump in to do it better.'\" The following Monday, Kaafar called together a cohort of PhD students and a postdoctoral researcher with expertise in AI security and natural-language processing. They would build voice bots to ape the tech insecurities of the average caller: umm-ing and ahh-ing their way through conversations with scammers and apologising for not being very good with technology or computers. \"But then we all immediately realised that by keeping the scammers on the line we can also extract and deliver this real-time intelligence, actionable intelligence, to stay on top of what scammers are doing.\" In 2024, Kaafar founded Apate.ai, named after the Greek goddess of deception and fraud. Apate.ai deploys thousands of digital decoy identities across phone, email, messaging apps and social platforms, designed to be discovered directly by scammers searching for victims. \"We are flipping the script on the economics of fraud by beating these bad actors at their own game.\" Crucial to the success of the fleet of Apate.ai bots is their believability: whether texting or talking, Apate.ai agents must resemble an actual person – rather than a minutely programmed chatbot – to keep scammers on the line. The agents have phone numbers and WhatsApp accounts; they have personalities, PayIDs and pet peeves. Some have distinctive accents; others employ broad Australian slang (\"no worries\", they may say or \"let me just get a cuppa\"). \"We used to give them cute names, like Ricky and Pete, and we each had our favourite. But as we evolved to more than 200,000 bots, now they just have ID numbers,\" says Kaafar. Each bot operates with a defined mission, which may include keeping a scammer talking for as long as possible, extracting crucial intelligence about the scammers' operations, or manoeuvring the fraudster onto another platform where the interaction can continue. The mission may result in gathering details of scammer impersonation tactics, phishing URLs, mule bank accounts and crypto wallets – crucial information to help authorities shut down these networks. It's not unusual for Apate.ai voice bots to keep scammers on the line for more than two hours, with the record at two hours and 12 minutes at the time of our interview. \"Literally a scammer is busy talking to a bot is a scammer that is not placing thousands of calls a minute to other potential victims,\" says Kaafar. Occasionally, scammers end the call because they cotton on that they are talking to a bot. The realisation that a bot has been wasting the scammer's time usually results in a slew of profanities. \"I think we're the only company around that has a KPI linked to f-words,\" he says. Kaafar is keen to emphasise that while delaying and obfuscating scammers is deeply satisfying, it offers benefits beyond merely blocking calls. \"What we're trying to do is distract them and disrupt them by presenting them with the perfect victim. Then we make it really painful for them where exactly it matters for them, which is how much money they'll be making. \"We want to put the foot on the gas,\" he says.","2026-10-22_001":"Musk's Promise to Give SpaceX Stock to Kids Is No Gift — 2026/10/22 Musk's Promise to Give SpaceX Stock to Kids Is No Gift One of the great joys, but also miseries, of following business news over the past decade or so has been riding the dodgy roller coaster of the Elon Musk experience. He has gone from a guy people considered a real-life Tony Stark to something more like a modern-day Henry Ford, in the worst possible way, with countless gut-wrenching twists in between. At no point, however, did anybody think: \"Hey, you know who's missing out on this? Children.\" Until today. SpaceX Chief Executive Officer Elon Musk pledged on Monday to donate one share of his company to each of America's 72 million children, vowing it would be the first such gift \"of many,\" if \"you had just this morning traveled here from another planet, then you might consider this a reasonably generous gesture from a near-trillionaire.\" The stock has been a bit stagnant since its June IPO, sure, but the company has billions of dollars in US government contracts, and Musk has promised its revenue will soar from less than $19 billion last year to $1 trillion by 2030. Such a gift would barely trouble Musk's vast wealth and power. It would cost about $10.5 billion, shaving Musk's net worth to $889.5 billion. And as my Bloomberg Opinion colleague Liam Denning points out, it would cut his SpaceX voting power, currently at 82% because of the stock's dual-class nature, by just 0.1 percentage points. But even a passing familiarity with Musk's whole vibe should raise many immediate concerns about this \"promise.\" For one thing, it was made on his social media app, X, formerly known as Twitter, on which Musk has promised many things, a large percentage of which have never happened. Most famous was his 2018 declaration that he had \"funding secured\" to take Tesla Inc. private at $420 a share. It got him in serious legal trouble when it turned out not to be true. If you have several hours to kill, you can peruse this website for a long list of other unfulfilled Musk promises, including a \"cross country summon\" feature that would let your Tesla travel the continent by itself, incredible-Journey style to find you; half-hour rocket trips from New York to London; 1 million robotaxis; and seafaring Teslas, to name a few. Buying stock in Musk is less an investment than it is subscribing to a streaming service for increasingly ridiculous science fiction. Also, it doesn't seem as if Musk spent long hours conferring with lawyers and accountants planning for this gift. Rather, the timing and Musk's track record suggest he saw SpaceX's AI rival, Anthropic PBC, making its own charitable pledge on Sunday and felt the urge to one-up it. The maker of Claude vowed to donate 5% of its shares, potentially worth $75 billion (if the company is worth $1.5 trillion), to a fund that will benefit the \"public good,\" including AI's destruction of jobs that kids want. Musk replied to Anthropic's X post making this announcement with a curt \"I recommend giving the shares directly to the public,\" suggesting he's just better at doing charity. His detail-free promise of one share per child followed hours later. How will children receive these shares? Nobody knows, and the odds are very good that nobody will ever have to find out. Anthropic's offer seems to be an olive branch to AI's large and growing contingent of haters and skeptics, including most of the American public, ahead of its IPO. Maybe it will also deflect some of the pressure to impose actual government regulation of the technology. Musk could use some good publicity, too, and not just because SpaceX is a growing AI giant. He has embraced White nationalism and let SpaceX's Starlink service undermine Ukraine's effort to defend itself from Russia's invasion. He manned the Department of Government Efficiency that slashed government jobs and offices without care for the repercussions, including the 700,000 people who have already died because of DOGE's assault on USAID. His behavior affects everyone invested in his companies, for better or worse, including those theoretical 72 million children. For a long time, \"worse\" has been a no-show; his fans have helped Tesla's stock price defy traditional market logic for years. How long can the magic last? As tough as children have it these days, with the climate in chaos, war in Europe and the Middle East and AI threatening to take all of the jobs, do they really need the headache of finding out?","2026-10-21_001":"Paris police issue overnight curfew in 2 areas of French capital to try to prevent further disorder — 2026/10/21 Paris police issue overnight curfew in 2 areas of French capital to try to prevent further disorder PARIS (AP) — Paris police issued an overnight curfew on Monday in two areas of the French capital, after a weekend marred by France's worst rioting in years that targeted city halls, other public buildings and thousands of businesses across the country. The order aims to protect \"people and property from the violence and looting,\" the Paris police statement said. The curfew prohibits anyone from entering or remaining on the Place de la République, where rioting took place Saturday, and the shopping neighborhood of Les Halles, which has been subject to looting. Earlier Monday, Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu had called for \"very limited\" and \"localized\" curfews. In northern France, local authorities imposed a curfew for children and teenagers under age 18 in several cities, including Lille. At least 2,400 people were arrested across the country. Authorities estimated Monday that at least 3,000 businesses were damaged or looted in several cities. Flames engulfed the 19th-century City Hall in Limoges, in central France, causing its roof to collapse and nearly destroying the building. The previous day, at the end of the protest against the rising cost of living, which brought together 5,500 people in Limoges, a group attacked the east wing of the city hall and started a fire. Within three hours, the roof of the historic building, inaugurated in 1883 and modeled after the Hôtel de Ville in Paris, was completely engulfed. \"I was horrified, but despite how violent what I saw was, I couldn't bring myself to leave.\" The day after the arson attack, Anne, a 47-year-old teacher, returned to the site and said she was still \"dumbfounded.\" Marine, 18, a literature student, on the verge of tears, said she felt \"heartbroken.\" \"I don't understand how things got to this point.\" \"Usually, our protests are always peaceful,\" continued Anne, who joined the march at 2 pm. \"There was some tension, we got a bit of tear gas, but I never felt unsafe, and then at 3:30 pm, suddenly, things deteriorated.\" On Sunday evening, a rally to condemn the violence drew 7,000 people. On Monday, cleanup crews and firefighters cleared shattered glass and other debris from the streets of Paris. Ouri Zagoury, owner of a video game shop near Paris' Place de la République, said that he closed the store Saturday afternoon, but then saw looters forcing their way in on surveillance cameras. \"There were so many people coming in and out. And even though I called the police, they said not to intervene, that they would come, but they never did, which is another problem,\" Zagoury said. Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez offered his resignation Sunday. But President Emmanuel Macron refused it \"given the need for continued command of law enforcement in the current context,\" Macron's office said. He instructed Nuñez \"to take all necessary steps to ensure full transparency about the failures in planning and intelligence-gathering that led to the violence.\" Nuñez acknowledged that authorities had \"lost control\" in some major cities on Saturday. \"You can't look shopkeepers in the eye when hundreds of businesses have been looted and say we haven't lost control in some of these major cities,\" Nuñez said. The protests in France began last month at high schools in Paris'working-class northern suburbs, where anger over teacher shortages and shabby facilities evolved into a broader movement. France's extremely strained public finances mean the government has precious little room for broader measures to address protesters'anger over the rising cost of living. The government has so far not committed to a substantial increase in funding for schools. However, it has pledged to send some 3,000 teachers, who are fully qualified but have not yet started work, to fill staffing gaps. The unrest will likely deal a blow to business confidence, which is already fragile due to a fiscal crisis and high energy prices resulting from the Iran war. The minority government is struggling to pass spending cuts in France's deeply divided parliament as part of its 2027 budget. The unrest has weighed on French stocks and the euro and put pressure on French government bonds. \"Le Pen no longer needs to spend any money to campaign,\" Jean-Baptiste Moreau, a former lawmaker from Macron's camp, told Reuters. \"It's not just a clear path ahead of her, it's a six-lane highway.\" Some protesters have already called for a new round of demonstrations on Saturday, October 24. Unions have called for strikes on November 5 to protest budget cuts and support hiking wages. The weekend violence has largely eclipsed the recent high school student protests. Schools are now on a break for two weeks, but there have been reports of protests at some universities. While mayors of France's biggest cities demanded answers from the government over what they say was a lack of preparation, authorities rushed to set up support measures for impacted businesses and bolster security as calm returned to the streets.","2026-10-20_001":"October 17 French protests: Crowds marched through the streets before violence, looting and chaos broke out — 2026/10/20 October 17 French protests: Crowds marched through the streets before violence, looting and chaos broke out On Saturday, 800,000 people took to the streets according to organizers, while Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez put the number at 550,000. The same pattern played out in many cities: On one side, a crowd of peaceful protesters; on the other, independent groups determined to clash with the police. While turnout across the country was massive, the protests were also marred by unprecedented property damage, violence and looting. In France, several worlds seemed to have collided over one long day. The roots of the protests stretched back to September, when calls for demonstrations against rising fuel prices began circulating on social media. Meanwhile, high school students were protesting teacher shortages, overcrowded classrooms and poor school conditions. The unrest also revived memories of the Yellow Vest movement, which began in 2018 as a protest against fuel taxes before growing into a broader rebellion against economic inequality and President Emmanuel Macron's government. Behind these different grievances lay a deeper frustration: many French people felt squeezed by rising living costs, disappointed by public services and increasingly distrustful of political leaders. Students, workers and Yellow Vest veterans saw an opportunity to unite their separate struggles. Responding to a call to action that notably cited the rising cost of living, but also building on the momentum of the high school student protest movement that began in late September, Saturday's demonstrations aimed to create a \"convergence of struggles\" between various groups, ranging from \"Yellow Vests\" to high school students, several professional sectors, left-wing political leaders and trade unions. At exactly 10 am, outside Gare Saint-Lazare train station, one Yellow Vest summed up his twofold ambition for the day: \"First, the revolution, but also to sleep in Macron's bed.\" He said this with a mixture of humor and seriousness, going on to denounce capitalism, the bourgeoisie, the police and the cost of living. Everyone in his entourage chimed in relentlessly, listing their demands, frustrations and anger, until their conversation was abruptly cut short when a BRAV-M motorcycle police unit suddenly barged in and descended on the group. Several small autonomous gatherings started forming in the morning. They aimed to head toward the Élysée Palace, the Place de la Concorde or the Opéra – basically, to push as far as possible. There was no official procession, no leader, no truly defined objective, just packs of individuals on the move, light on their feet. \"Strike, blockade, wild protest!\" mask-clad people shouted as they sprinted toward Avenue de l'Opéra. In Paris, the situation seemed to at least partially slip out of control from the early afternoon, while, at the same time, taking on several different forms. Place de la République, the starting point for the official demonstration was packed to bursting. Some violent and organized protesters began by attacking street furniture, then tried to break into the Republican Guard barracks, set a car on fire and smashed things left, right and center. While it might not exactly have been the much-heralded \"convergence of struggles,\" the march still brought together people from all walks of life. Lucie and Thomas, who both just graduated from the prestigious HEC business school, proclaimed their support for the movement: \"We are aware that we are privileged students and we have a duty of solidarity.\" The same pattern also played out in several other cities. On one side, large groups of demonstrators marched in dozens of peaceful processions from Rennes (Brittany) to Toulouse (south), despite the tensions. On the other, independent groups were determined to clash with the police. In the streets, groups of university and high school students, toughened up by weeks of protest, mingled with small groups that organized to confront law enforcement units. Protesters from working-class neighborhoods were there, as well as a good number of looters who could hardly believe their eyes: All the opulence of Paris's wealthy districts was suddenly within reach, whether by hand or by hammer. \"These neighborhoods are rich here. It's all from the profits made off the suburbs,\" shouted a very young man in the middle of a small group, as they made their way near Rue Lafayette. On several of Paris's major right bank streets, the same scenes, with the same violent acts, played out again and again. Paving stones were thrown. Cars, trucks and construction vehicles were set on fire. Starting from Madeleine, Kenzo, Salomon and Arc'teryx boutiques had their windows smashed and shelves ransacked. The Céline store on Avenue Montaigne was pillaged. In nearby streets, one guy and his friends were asking, \"Where is Chanel?\" On Saturday evening, Lecornu announced that 1,200 people had been arrested and 150 police officers and gendarmes injured. \"We will all stand united against the sowers of chaos, who seek only to bring down our institutions and prevent those who peacefully express their demands from doing so,\" he said.","2026-10-19_001":"El Niño Threatens Global Sugar Supply — 2026/10/19 El Niño Threatens Global Sugar Supply Conflict. Climate change. A withering El Niño. All are conspiring to spoil the world's sweet tooth. Global sugar prices have soared, as a hot, dry summer scorched much of the sugar-producing parts of the world, nudging overall food prices upward. Nowhere is that felt as acutely as it is in India, the biggest sugar consumer in the world and the second-biggest producer. The worst monsoon in 11 years and fears of a poor sugarcane harvest have spiked prices amid the Hindu festival season, when gods and mortals alike are feted with sweets. Making matters worse, high sugar prices are also complicating India's energy security goals. Sugarcane is a key crop for the country's biofuels push, all the more so as India seeks to curb oil imports while the waterways of the Middle East are choked by war. This year's El Niño has left many of the country's sugarcane fields parched, just a year after they were drenched by excess rains and devoured by pests. Protests have broken out over rising prices. The government has warned sugar traders against hoarding. And a country that usually exports sugar has taken the extraordinary step of opening the doors to imports this year, further raising global sugar prices. \"This crop is gone,\" Morarji Patil, a 30-year-old farmer, said as he stood beside his cane field one sweaty September morning in the Marathwada region of western India. Like other farmers here, Mr. Patil was already crushed by debt and high fertilizer prices before the blistering heat and scant rains. His cane stalks were thin and short, stunted by a lack of water this summer when they needed it most. Mr. Patil expected his yields to be 70 percent lower than normal. India represents an object lesson on the far-reaching effects of conflict and extreme weather on a nation's economy. Food prices are rising month by month. Key crops remain at risk as the long tail of El Niño drags out through next year, heightening the likelihood of extreme drought and flood. On top of that, India is extremely vulnerable to global oil supply shocks. It imports the vast majority of its crude oil. It's not just India. Extraordinary heat and drought this year have hit sugarcane in Brazil, the world's biggest sugar producer, and sugar beets in Europe. Global sugar prices are nearly 15 percent higher than they were a year ago. In India, sugar has long enjoyed state support. Most recently, the industry profited from a government directive that made 20-percent ethanol blends the standard fuel at Indian gas stations. The government said its biofuels push reduced oil demand and climate pollution, while also saving the country's precious foreign exchange. It called ethanol blends \"a key pillar of India's energy transition.\" It offered sugar refineries an additional source of revenue. It also infuriated Indian drivers. Aditya Kare, 21, was refueling his 2018 Hero motorcycle at a gas station in the nearby city of Latur. He said the ethanol blend had reduced his average mileage from 60 kilometers per liter to around 45, and he was having a hard time making do with the 100 rupees, a little more than $1, that his parents gave him for his commute to college. \"At home, we are planning to get an electric bike,\" he said. Sugarcane comes with a high cost. It is a thirsty crop, and its demand, for both food and fuel, is forcing many farmers to drill deeper for water and drain local aquifers. Mr. Patil's well goes down 800 feet. This year, even at that depth, there is no water. A dam on the Manjara River has become a dry ditch. Ponds are bare. \"This environment is not good for sugarcane,\" said Ganesh Gomare, a farmer. \"But the economy of Marathwada is dependent on sugarcane.\" \"Sugar is tempting. It enjoys relatively high profits.\" So Mr. Gomare, 38, devoted more of his land to sugarcane this year. It backfired. He expects his yields to be half of normal. Nearby, Dagdu Venkat Rao Padile, 66, uprooted a stalk of sugarcane from his field. The plant was emaciated. Its sugar content would be paltry. \"It will cost me more to cut it than what I'll get for it,\" Mr. Padile said. His yields would be one-fourth of normal. The rains were so poor that he had left most of his land fallow. Tanisha Gaikwad had all but cut sugar from her diet. But she joined the line at the sweet shop for a small box of laddoos to offer at the altar for Mahalaxmi, a goddess of wealth and power. Her prayers, she said, could not be sugar-free.","2026-10-18_001":"The Tesla Roadster flew, drove upside down, and hit 60mph in under a second — 2026/10/18 The Tesla Roadster flew, drove upside down, and hit 60mph in under a second Elon Musk promised a flying car. And the Tesla Roadster did exactly that. Tesla demonstrated its next-generation Roadster at an event in Waco, Texas, where the electric supercar drove upside down, briefly flew, and accelerated to 60mph in under a second. It was an impressive demonstration of a car that likely will come with some heavy caveats and restrictions if it ever goes into production. The event was less than 30 minutes and felt more like a stunt show than a product reveal. Indeed, there was no mention of price, trim levels, release date, or other such mundane details. Musk promised the hypercar would go into production in 2028, but after nearly nine years who's counting. The people were here to see a car fly, and that's what Tesla gave them. First, the Roadster drove up a very tall, multi-story ramp, using its incredible downforce to cling to the vertical roadway. When it reached the top of the ramp, it flipped around using its cold-gas thrusters to bring it gently back down to earth. The car uses a fan-based suction system, supplied by SpaceX and mounted underneath the vehicle, which is capable of generating up to two Gs of suction force. Next, the Roadster flew over the rest of Tesla's lineup, including the Model 3, Model Y, Cybertruck, Semi, and Cybercab — doing what Tesla VP for Engineering Lars Moravy called \"a kick flip\" in the process. Musk explained that it features attitude-control thrusters as well as main thrusters at the rear, giving the Roadster rocket-like capabilities. \"So it can basically fly like a rocket,\" Musk said. \"And it could do rolls. It could do, like, crazy gymnastics. Whatever you want, really. Now, I should say that this is not necessarily a safety-optimized car. But it takes the fun meter to 11 out of 10.\" Of course, it seems extremely unlikely that this version of the car will ever be available to the average consumer — even as Musk claimed \"This will be an actual car that's on the roads.\" The company would have to satisfy strict regulatory requirements from the Federal Aviation Administration, as well as the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration. The Roadster could go into production in 2028, as Musk promised — or it could be another decade of missed deadlines and broken promises. Again, who cares? Car flew. Tesla has also said that the Roadster can accelerate from 0-60mph in less than a second. And during the event, the company claimed it did so in 0.81 seconds. Musk explained that increasing the applied force while reducing the vehicle's mass help boost acceleration. \"You've got to crack halfway to the speed of sound, I think,\" Musk teased. \"Mach 0.5. Maybe a little higher.\" The next-gen Roadster certainly took the scenic route in getting here. A prototype version was announced as a \"one more thing\" style surprise during the 2017 Tesla Semi event. The specs sounded ludicrous even back then: 0-60 mph in 1.9 seconds, a top speed over 250 mph, a 200 kWh battery delivering up to 620 miles of range, and a $200,000 starting price tag. Musk said deliveries would start in 2020, which sounded perfectly doable at the time. Early adopters quickly threw down $50,000 deposits (or $250,000 for the limited Founders Series), effectively interest-free loans to help fund Tesla's scaling ambitions. But then Tesla seemed to forget about the Roadster, cementing its status as the ultimate vaporware. Production deadlines slipped as Tesla prioritized mass-market vehicles like the Model Y, while also wrestling with the overly complicated Cybertruck. Occasionally, when Musk would remember that the Roadster existed, he would up the ante about its capabilities. This culminated in the \"SpaceX package\" that would equip the supercar with cold-gas thrusters, bestowing hover powers and a 0-60 time under one second. Musk has also sought to downplay the Roadster's significance, calling it a \"small potato situation\" that will not \"move the needle in a major way financially.\" He's framed the supercar more as a joint engineering collaboration between Tesla and SpaceX, even questioning whether it's \"even a car anymore\" given its experimental nature. Some analysts theorize that the decision to re-release the Roadster is an attempt to set the stage for a rumored Tesla-SpaceX merger. Tesla's failure to bring the Roadster to market allowed other automakers to slip past the company with their own hyper-powered EVs. Rimac has smashed performance records with the Nevera, Porsche has refined electric performance with the Taycan, and Chinese giants like BYD are producing track-ready supercars at a fraction of the cost. The Roadster is no longer shocking the world as an impossible prototype; it's playing catch-up to an industry that spent nine years building what Tesla merely rendered. But then again, none of those cars flew.","2026-10-17_001":"Mark Carney shows middle powers how to handle a dangerous world — 2026/10/17 Mark Carney shows middle powers how to handle a dangerous world Entitled Canada has been feeding off America for decades. Like a parasitic bug, it enjoys the benefits of being America's cherished 51st state without giving anything back. Canadians know they couldn't live without America's protection, yet their ungrateful prime minister, Mark Carney, refuses to defer to the great and beautiful country over the border. That is how Donald Trump sees Canada, and it leaves no room for mutual benefit between the two allies. The bullying president is ratcheting up tariffs. Canadian consumers, in response, are spitting out their Kentucky bourbon and leaving Las Vegas. Mr Carney, in office for 18 months, remains defiant, the star of an extended, dramatic and riveting stand-off. By now you might have expected Mr Carney to have caved in. Canada is only a mid size power with a so-so economy and puny armed forces. It has vulnerabilities aplenty. One will be evident on October 19th, when the province of Alberta holds a referendum asking if it may one day secede, taking with it more oil in the ground than in the entire United States. In fact Mr Carney is riding high. Polls suggest that those Albertans will probably vote to stay. And under him, Canada is doing better than you might expect. In January it was Mr Carney, at the annual jamboree in Davos, who argued that what he called the \"middle powers\" must find ways to take on threatening ones. Nine months on, his country offers lessons for what these middle powers should do when the hegemonic giant pulls the mat from under them. Those lessons start with a bracing dose of realism. As we set out, middle powers will never replace the hegemons as the force that sustains powerful permanent institutions such as the UN. The world has grown more dangerous because both America and China are exploiting or undermining arrangements and norms in which power was shared. Emboldened, wreckers like Russia and Iran are becoming even more predatory. Co-ordinating the more herbivorous middle powers is often too hard to get much done—as you can see in even the most developed middle-power club, the European Union. Yet middle powers can still mitigate some of the damage, as Canada and its allies show. Mr Carney has zipped around the world making new trade deals as well as drumming up symbolic agreements, such as talk of associate EU membership. In the realm of defence, Canada is trimming its old reliance on America, and is considering placing an order for Swedish Gripen fighter jets. Canada's most important lesson, however, emerges from what the pragmatic Mr Carney is doing at home. A few years ago the country's woeful politics and ailing government seemed broken. Income per person had drifted from 90% of America's in the 1980s to just 71% in 2022, much of it following a recent burst of low-skilled immigration. Today Mr Carney is pushing through overdue measures to boost productivity, open the economy and lift defence spending from a pitifully low 1.3% of GDP to 2%. Among his first steps, he scrapped an unpopular consumer carbon tax and vowed to build a pipeline to carry oil from Alberta to the west coast. He has started to cut internal barriers to trade, called off a hefty rise in capital-gains tax, cut the lowest rate of income tax and set out plans to trim federal spending. Mr Carney has so far managed to resist what is known as the \"Juncker curse\", named after a president of the European Commission, Jean-Claude Juncker. Speaking of reform, Mr Juncker once said: \"We all know what to do, we just don't know how to get re-elected after we've done it.\" Yet Mr Carney's popularity has been surging even as he reforms. Today a maple-leaf love-in is under way: polls show the prime minister's approval rating nearing 70%. Internationally, he has replaced France's beleaguered Emmanuel Macron as the centrists' pin-up. The contrast with Mr Trump, unloved by his own voters, is stark. True, Canada must go further and faster with its liberalising changes. There is every chance that relations with America will improve when Mr Trump leaves office in 2029, but it is not certain. It should therefore work on keeping its options open. Other middle powers can learn from Canada. And they can fortify themselves abroad by harnessing the need for change at home. In a way, Mr Trump has done all democracies a favour, by giving them a reason to tackle long-standing problems that seemed too hard. The middle powers need to prepare for a dangerous world. They should look to Mr Carney and Canada for an answer on what to do.","2026-10-16_001":"Video Cameras Are Everywhere-and Now They Are Being Hacked for War — 2026/10/16 Video Cameras Are Everywhere-and Now They Are Being Hacked for War As U.S. and Israeli forces bombed Iran early this year, a poultry farmer in northern Israel decided to check on his chickens. When he launched the app on his phone for the webcam outside his coop, he was alarmed to see that its language had been switched to Arabic. He contacted Israel's National Cyber Directorate, which found that hackers had taken control of the Chinese-made camera. They weren't interested in chickens. The camera's orientation on the coop offered it a line of sight to the movements of Israeli security forces along the nearby border with Lebanon. The cyber directorate said it helped the farmer harden the camera's security and adjust its viewing angle to stop capturing military operations. The farmer's webcam was one of an estimated 1.2 billion internet-connected cameras worldwide, according to cybersecurity consultants S-RM. The devices have become a background fixture of daily life in the form of security systems, doorbells, traffic controls and even baby monitors—and potentially any of them are vulnerable to snooping, say cybersecurity experts and hackers. \"We are now seeing the conversion of civilian surveillance into wartime intelligence collection,\" said Alexander Leslie, a national-security expert at threat-intelligence consultants Recorded Future, who sees a global \"soft underbelly\" in omnipresent digital eyeballs. Intelligence agencies scour the internet for vulnerable cameras and robotically tap in to them by testing millions of passwords. Now, AI has made it even easier to hack into them and analyze their data at scale, transforming cameras that were designed to boost safety into tools for combatants to track, surveil and attack the enemy—often surpassing the abilities of human intelligence analysts. Algorithms digest endless reams of footage to find hidden gems and patterns for agents to monitor. Russia and Ukraine have ramped up camera-hacking in their war, aiming to spy on each other, find new targets and assess damage from their attacks. Israel, long a master of digital spycraft, hacked into Iranian traffic cameras to help forces collect intelligence used in the assassination of Iranian Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei earlier this year. Iran and its proxies have struck back by breaking into hundreds of Israeli webcams and security cameras that Israeli officials consider sensitive to national security, like the one on the chicken coop. In 2024, Russian hackers commandeered residential webcams in Kyiv, including one installed by a condominium association to monitor the neighborhood. Ukraine's security service, the SBU, said that Russia intended to use the hacked video feeds to find targets in the capital and \"to adjust missile attacks on Ukraine,\" refining their aim for subsequent strikes. The SBU said it has identified and secured, reoriented or blocked roughly 40,000 video cameras that posed potential security risks since Russia's 2022 invasion. Hacked cameras are taking over roles in targeting, damage assessments and other combat intelligence-gathering that were previously supplied by aerial reconnaissance or on-the-ground spies. Penetrating a well-placed camera can get analysts closer to targets and carries less risk than infiltrating even the canniest secret agent. Video cameras operate silently, often in restricted areas. While private individuals and enterprise-network managers are increasingly attuned to cybersecurity, web-connected devices like cameras may have fewer built-in protections and not be consistently monitored, patched or updated, said Xu Zou, senior vice president for cloud security services at cybersecurity company Palo Alto Networks. Information-security specialists inside companies and governments often aren't aware of connected cameras, which are frequently installed and operated by facilities managers or other non-IT teams, he said. Ondrej Svoboda in Prague was reading to his young children one evening in 2023 when the baby monitor in their room started emitting strange sounds of people talking and background music, as if from a bar. At first he thought his wife was using the two-way function but quickly realized that hackers had gotten into his system. \"I didn't think this was something that could happen to me,\" recalled Svoboda, who worked at a cybersecurity company. \"I was really scared-my heart was racing.\" Trying to avoid frightening his children, he silently stared into the camera to show the hackers he knew about them and then unplugged the camera. For days he suffered nightmares as he struggled to understand how he got hacked. Eventually he remembered he had tried connecting his webcams to another app, which likely was insecure. Vulnerable accessories linked into a network are among hackers' prime targets, expanding what technicians call the attack surface. Often, cameras aren't the target, but serve as the weak link in a larger hacking attempt. Ransomware group Akira tapped an unprotected webcam to get around one organization's network-security systems and extort it, S-RM reported last year. Once hackers are inside a local network, they can usually probe stored files, email accounts and other devices, including cameras. \"The Internet of Things is such a wild area that everything-whether you realize it or not—is an extension of your attack surface,\" said Paul Caron, S-RM head of cyber for the Americas.","2026-10-15_001":"China is turning trash into treasure on a vast scale — 2026/10/15 China is turning trash into treasure on a vast scale A fast-growing number of EVs will soon be scrapped and replaced. Shredding their batteries yields \"black mass\", a powder from which valuable metals such as lithium, cobalt and nickel can be recovered and reused. From next month the EU will make it harder to export this waste. The aim is to encourage more processing of black mass close to home (at the moment most is shipped to Asia). In August America, which has banned Chinese EVs but still imports lots of Chinese batteries, blocked all black-mass exports. China, however, is unlikely to suffer. Black mass might make up 20-30% of global demand for battery metals by 2050, says the International Energy Agency, a forecaster. So China is supercharging its recycling industries: not just to shore up supplies of battery metals, but also rare earths and much else. The goal is to make its economy less \"linear\" (where raw materials are made into products which are then thrown away) and more \"circular\" (closing the loop so recycled waste replaces raw materials). Once recycling efforts were driven by concerns about pollution. Now, amid a global scramble for the materials needed to build a high-tech economy, security has become a primary motive. In the 1980s China started to import waste, taking the hardware, paper and plastic that richer countries did not want. A sprawling informal recycling-sector emerged, led by scavengers picking through rubbish piles. Much ended up in landfills or burnt in the open air, leaching toxic chemicals and creating poisonous smog. China is still well behind squeaky-clean European countries when it comes to waste management (though it is hard to say how far, as it does not publish detailed national data for recycling). Its poorer rural areas are still often strewn with rubbish. But officials have made China's big cities, at least, cleaner and more pleasant to live in. Now their focus is shifting to the use of recycling for the sake of security. In July China released a plan for the development of the circular economy until 2030. \"The importance of resource security has become increasingly prominent, while geopolitical dynamics...have grown complex and severe,\" the plan noted. First, China wants to dominate high-tech manufacturing in everything from robotics to wind turbines. And second, to achieve this, it must secure supplies of raw materials, connect more power lines and build more data-centres. Massive amounts of aluminium, copper, battery metals and rare earths are needed. While China controls the bulk of the global refining capacity for these materials, it relies on imports for most of the ores. Meanwhile many Western countries want to reduce reliance on China's factories. Officials worry they might try to squeeze its supplies. Last year a researcher at a state-owned research facility said that without recycling China would \"keep facing strangleholds in the future\". That China already makes vast quantities of these goods should make recycling simpler to do. Factories can tweak designs so that products are easier to dismantle later. And many battery-producing techniques (such as using acids and heat to isolate valuable metals) are also used in recycling them. Take CATL, for example, the world's largest battery-maker. Most of the Chinese firm's sales are domestic, and will end up being processed by a CATL subsidiary called Brunp, which is the world's biggest battery-recycler. Such arrangements have helped China build 85% of global battery recycling capacity. China is trying to make the recycling of rare earths (which it claims now supplies 10% of them) more efficient. At present it is far more costly than extracting them from raw materials. Still, Chinese rare-earth companies, which are almost all state-owned, \"do not rely on economic viability in all cases\", notes Corby Anderson. And to make sure its recycling plants are fed, China is also quietly reassuming its old role in the waste trade. It is selectively rolling back import restrictions on some foreign waste. In 2024 it eased curbs on imports of some scrap copper and aluminum. Last year it started allowing imports of black mass. In January it slashed import tariffs on the stuff from 6.5% to 3%. Officials have pledged to continue to increase imports of \"high-quality\" recycled materials over the next five years. Still, now that America and Europe want to keep their valuable waste, could they eat into China's recycling dominance? Zhang Yuping, the deputy general manager of GEM, a big Shenzhen-listed battery-recycler, thinks not. \"From the perspective of national security, they might have some justification,\" he says. But the cost of recycling outside China would be high and scaling up difficult. Europe, at least, can't digest such a challenge, reckons Mr Zhang. \"They would pay a heavy cost. \" ■","2026-10-14_001":"Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? — 2026/10/14 Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? 2026/10/14 Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? 中国短剧杀进好莱坞：一集60秒、一年赚15亿美元，美国都急了！ In 2020, the entertainment mogul Jeffrey Katzenberg brought to life an idea that must have seemed — in the flash of its conception — foolproof: Netflix, but for people addicted to their phones. He called it Quibi: short for \"quick bites.\" It collapsed after eight months in a total void of public interest. And yet, the joke may very well be on us. Because, just six years later, short-form, mobile-first narrative entertainment is the fastest-growing form of streaming worldwide, per Bloomberg. Microdramas, sometimes called \"verticals,\" are TV shows for your phone. They are typically described as soap operas for Gen Z: hypersexual, over the top and — most important — short, with episodes rarely exceeding two minutes in length. The format was born around 2018 in China, where its revenue now exceeds that of the conventional film industry. As I write this, the form is on track to take in $1.5 billion in revenue in the United States by the end of the year. Each short episode opens with a hook and ends with a twist, dragging viewers, like recalcitrant pets, through entire seasons roughly the length of a feature film. In the first years of this decade, a number of Chinese companies began to export the form abroad, creating countless U.S. microdrama apps and coordinating U.S.-based microdrama shoots in order to supply those apps with content. These companies still retain a near monopoly on U.S. production, but that is quickly changing. Hollywood executives — noticing these apps outranking their own streamers on the charts — have started to spin up microdrama platforms of their own. I'd managed to wrangle three days of set visits on a show called \"Bound by Love,\" a production of ReelShort. Culturally, ReelShort is an interesting case: the flagship brand of an American company originally financed — and still partly owned — by a Chinese digital content group. The 9:16 format that fills a vertical phone screen leaves little room to maneuver, requiring actors to stand unnaturally close together. \"Bound by Love,\" which in its finished form would run more than two and a half hours, was to be shot at a variety of locations over the course of just 11 days. The key grip had an explanation for this: The scenes they were shooting that day were for an early episode, one that would appear before the viewer hit the paywall. As at least a dozen people would subsequently inform me, the platforms are not overly invested in the quality of the episodes that come after — i.e., the episodes a viewer has actually paid to see. The goal is to keep them up, which is distinct from telling a good or even coherent story. What is clear with hindsight is that Quibi failed primarily because it was TV — regular old TV — that happened to be on your phone. The intervening years have taught us that what tends to succeed on phones is phone stuff. This is the terrain that ReelShort and its peers have precision-engineered their content to compete on. Fox Entertainment has made an equity investment in the microdrama platform Holywater. Disney+ has begun to produce microdramas of its own, and Paramount+ has a slate scheduled for the fall. Today microdramas are — as more than one person put it to me — the only game in town. \"I was fighting it for a long time, but Old Hollywood is dead.\" Microdramas, she went on, are commercialized, rule-bound, utterly devoid of artistry. \"And this can't be the end of it for me, so I'm joining the bandwagon.\" The male leads I spoke to, specifically, seemed uniformly thrilled by this professional windfall. It's different for the women. Apparently, China's short-form video platforms had recently seen a milestone: more than $12 million spent in one day on advertisements for A.I.-generated microdramas. The country's microdrama production hubs, meanwhile — sites of industrial-scale production only months earlier — were emptying out. Actors who were in demand last year, stars of the form, were doing food delivery just to survive. Gelb was seeing the writing on the wall: \"Experience shows that, in the microdrama world, what happens in China does not stay in China.\" It's a preview for what's to come for the rest of the world. \" Soon after I returned, it emerged that an average of 470 A.I.-generated microdramas were released on Chinese platforms each day this past January. In the same period, COL Group — a part-owner of ReelShort's parent company — said that it planned to replace 75 percent of its actors with A.I. for titles released in China. I spoke to an executive at COL, a man named Timothy Oh. He seemed amused, if puzzled, by the continued American resistance to A.I.-generated content. \"The reality is, content is content,\" he told me. \"People are already watching A.I. slop on Instagram.\" As I write this, A.I. microdramas are all over the ReelShort homepage, and make up a large percentage of their most-watched charts.","2026-10-13_001":"After the deadliest attack in Saudi Arabia in more than a decade, Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman faces growing pressure to find a way to stop the Iran-backed Houthi militia in Yemen from firing missiles and drones at his country. Yet the prince, Saudi Arabia's de facto ruler, has few options as he confronts an asymmetrical conflict with an adversary that has become stronger since he led a disastrous war against it a decade ago, analysts say. The Houthis have carried out three fatal attacks on an international airport in the Saudi capital, Riyadh, over the past week. Those included a strike on a passenger terminal on Saturday that killed at least 12 people and injured more than 300, many of them critically, according to the Saudi authorities. They have also hit at the heart of Prince Mohammed's ambitions to transform Saudi Arabia from an insular, oil-dependent nation into a global hub for business and tourism. But there is no easy way for the crown prince to halt the Houthis' attacks. A political resolution with the militia has become increasingly difficult to reach as its confidence and demands grow, and attempting to defeat the Houthis militarily is unlikely to work either, analysts say.","2026-10-12_001":"An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid — 2026/10/12 An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid 2026/10/12 An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid Navi Pillay, who won this year's Nobel Peace Prize, fought racism in South Africa, shaping her international career as a judge and advocate for justice and human rights. She lived her childhood in a slum without sewage or water, attended a school for only children of Indian descent, and knew that her skin color prohibited her from going to the beach her father would drive past during the Christmas season. But she also described a pivotal moment that helped her rise from under the thumb of racism: Parents in her community in the coastal city of Durban pooled their money to send her to university, after her high school principal said she had potential. That opportunity began a barrier-breaking career that led Ms. Pillay to becoming a lawyer, a judge on multiple international courts, the human rights chief of the United Nations and, on Friday, a Nobel Peace Prize laureate. In announcing the prize, the Nobel committee said that Ms. Pillay had been \"instrumental in ensuring that war crimes, crimes against humanity and genocide are prosecuted.\" She becomes the fifth South African to win the award, joining the ranks of the country's most vaunted figures including Nelson Mandela and Archbishop Desmond Tutu. While much of Ms. Pillay's global recognition comes from human rights work she has done abroad, her experiences in South Africa have indelibly shaped her life, and she has remained deeply invested in her home country's sometimes-fragile democracy. \"I see this award as the voice of the majority of the world,\" Ms. Pillay said in an interview with the South African state broadcaster on Friday. She added that a majority of South Africans \"want the rule of law, they want the independent judiciary to be respected.\" She studied law at what is now the University of KwaZulu-Natal. Because it was segregated, Black students had to attend classes at night, said Jessica Neuwirth, a human-rights lawyer and activist who has known Ms. Pillay for four decades. During the day, Ms. Pillay would go to the library, where she found books about the Nuremberg trials, helping inspire her fascination with international justice, Ms. Neuwirth said. When the apartheid government locked up her husband, Gaby Pillay, who was also a lawyer, for political activities and cut off his access to the outside world, Ms. Pillay used his power of attorney to file an action on his behalf, Ms. Neuwirth said. She ended up winning an order prohibiting his torture. Ms. Pillay's struggle to maneuver within a racist system applying unjust laws in some ways helped her develop the persistence she needed, she has said in interviews. She said in a podcast interview last year that she would keep filing legal motions even if they had little chance of succeeding because she believed that was the only weapon she had to fight the system. \"It was clear to me the only thing I can do is keep going, seeking justice,\" she said. She rose to international prominence shortly after apartheid ended in the 1990s, as a judge on the tribunal on the Rwanda genocide, which broke new ground in prosecuting the crime of genocide and treating mass rape as a form of genocide. Ms. Pillay later served as a judge on the International Criminal Court, the U.N. High Commissioner for Human Rights, and a substitute judge of the International Court of Justice. The prize, however, was not welcomed everywhere. Speaking at a rally in Syracuse, New York, on Friday evening, President Trump spent about 10 minutes attacking the decision, describing Ms. Pillay as a \"mystery person\" he had never heard of and warning Norway that he would not forget it. On social media, he said he deserved eight Nobel Peace Prizes for the wars he claims to have settled, though some of those conflicts continue and others were ended largely by other parties. The chairman of the Norwegian Nobel Committee replied calmly, saying the panel is independent of the government and bound only by the will of Alfred Nobel. Ms. Pillay, for her part, had already joked to an applauding crowd in Nuremberg that if they kept clapping, she might share the prize with Mr. Trump.","2026-10-11_001":"Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself — 2026/10/11 Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself 2026/10/11 Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself 马斯克、奥特曼、黄仁勋都在上：播客为什么突然成了硅谷最值钱的流量入口？ EACH WEEKDAY morning Jordi Hays and John Coogan, two entrepreneurs-turned-podcasters, enter a sauna in Los Angeles with copies of the Wall Street Journal. They come out with jackets, ties and hot takes on the tech news of the day. Then they go on air as hosts of the \"Technology Business Programming Network\" (\"TBPN\") podcast, riffing with guests, banging gongs and blowing airhorns. Silicon Valley laps it up. It is not uncommon to see \"TBPN\", rather than CNBC, streaming on the TV screens in tech firms' offices. Podcasts are the latest example of a power law in Silicon Valley. Only a few become big hitters. Those that do attract the demigods of tech as guests, making them even more influential. \"TBPN\" has interviewed Sam Altman of OpenAI and Mark Zuckerberg of Meta. The \"All-In\" Podcast, hosted by four plutocrats who straddle tech and MAGA politics, held a summit in Los Angeles in September attended by Elon Musk and Jensen Huang. (When Donald Trump phoned Nvidia's boss on stage during the summit, Mr Huang put the president on speakerphone.) All share an in-crowd mystique bordering on incestuousness. In April OpenAI reportedly spent $100m-200m to buy then-18-month-old TBPN (though there was no mention of the affiliation during Mr Altman's interview). The \"All-In\" hosts are self-described \"besties\" with many of their billionaire interviewees. They are also an echo chamber, with a tin ear at times to the concerns of the rest of America. This new podcasting elite reflects a big change in Silicon Valley's relationship with the media. During the last great investment boom, the dotcom era of the 1990s, publications like Wired were tech-industry gospels. They covered the internet like Rolling Stone wrote about rock'n'roll, says John Battelle, a founding editor of Wired: \"We thought we were covering the counterculture.\" But the rise of social media and the growing power and wealth of big tech generated a rift between Silicon Valley and news outlets. Podcasters, who share their guests' techno-optimism, provide a friendlier platform. The watershed came during the 2024 presidential race. Led by Mr Trump, politicians saw the medium as a less filtered way of getting their message out than the press or broadcast TV. Two of its billionaire co-hosts, David Sacks and Chamath Palihapitiya, threw a fundraiser in San Francisco for Mr Trump. Shortly afterwards he appeared on their show. That helped raise the profile of the hosts, as well as of tech podcasting in general. Helping them thrive is what would be called, in Silicon Valley jargon, a flywheel. Just before he hit the big time in 2024, Mr Patel tweeted his bank balance. It showed savings of $0.14. Then Mr Zuckerberg came onto the show and the advertising dollars poured in. As more prominent executives take part, not only do advertisers notice; so do potential customers, investors and jobseekers, raising the quality of the audience. \"This is luxurious media with very valuable eyeballs,\" says Josh Kaplan, boss of Smooth Media, which represents podcasters. The format is particularly well-suited to Silicon Valley, where founders invent stories for a living. \"We're not journalists; we are enthusiasts,\" says Patrick O'Shaughnessy, whose podcast, \"Invest Like the Best\", has a strong following in VC circles. These offerings may be popular, but do not expect them to invest in the costly investigative work that uncovers frauds, such as that at Theranos, a health-tech startup, exposed by the Wall Street Journal in 2015. Instead, AI is at the centre of their discussions. Unlike many parts of America, they treat it as almost the only topic worth discussing. But this obsession has further pushed Silicon Valley into a \"monoculture\", says David Senra, an influential podcaster. Recently he has been to tech dinners where conversation veers only between AI optimism and doomerism. \"It sounds like they are all reading the same tweets,\" he says. Lulu Cheng Meservey, a PR consultant to tech founders, reckons nine-tenths of people in tech speak only to other people in tech. Podcasts exacerbate that. They may be well-meaning, she says, but \"when a billionaire AI salesman is telling you whether to buy AI, you are taking that from someone who is entirely unrelatable to you.\" It may get worse. Silicon Valley's podcasters sprinkle their conversations with reverence for space missions, robots and other techno-wizardry. In an Alpocalypse, they would come out better off than most people. Mr Battelle calls it the \"golden ticket on the rocket ship\". If all else fails, they can podcast from Mars. But will people still want to listen to them?","2026-10-10_001":"What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare — 2026/10/10 What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare 2026/10/10 What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare A 28-year-old worker at a research laboratory in Siberia recently died of pneumonia. The facility studies plague, among other pathogens, raising fears that she had been exposed at the laboratory. But Russian health officials so far have not linked the death of the worker, Darya Shipilova, to infection with Yersinia pestis, the bacterium that causes plague. And despite early reports that dozens had been quarantined near the research facility, health officials said that there was no risk of an epidemic. Still, the death has prompted concerns around the world, in part because of the Soviet Union's long history of advanced bioweapons research, which led to outbreaks of anthrax and smallpox. After unconfirmed reports of a second unusual case of pneumonia in the region, the World Health Organization sought additional information from Russian officials. President Trump said he planned to speak to President Vladimir V. Putin of Russia \"very soon\" about the death. Early reports cited local officials saying that Ms. Shipilova was exposed to Y. pestis during her work at the Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and the Far East. Y. pestis was responsible for two of history's deadliest pandemics: the Black Death and the Plague of Justinian. As a lung infection, plague is contagious, although not as infectious as some other respiratory infections. (The bacterium also infects the bloodstream and lymph nodes.) In its early stages, plague can be successfully treated with antibiotics. There are hundreds of cases each year, mostly in Africa, according to the W.H.O.. Russian health officials said that Ms. Shipilova had died of pneumonia of unknown cause. While plague can lead to pneumonia, the filling of the lung's air sacs with fluid, it can have many other causes, including seasonal flu. \"Unfortunately, young people can die from pneumonia, so that development alone wouldn't be enough to raise concern,\" said Dr. Tom Inglesby, an epidemiologist at Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security. \"What I do think is concerning is the Russian reaction so far.\" More than 200 people who had been in contact with the deceased were quarantined in local hospitals, and Russian officials have not explained why, Dr. Inglesby said. Nor have they announced the cause of death, if not plague. Lab accidents can and do happen, even when researchers are following safety protocols. But the lack of transparency surrounding the death and the number of people potentially exposed is unusual, other experts said. \"Something is not adding up about this situation,\" said Jennifer Nuzzo, an epidemiologist at Brown University. \"You don't quarantine 200 people for one plague case,\" she added. \"You don't close multiple hospital units in response to one infection.\" After an unusual infection like this, health officials often provide the genome sequence of the virus or bacterium involved. This helps researchers pinpoint exactly which pathogen is to blame and how to fight it. Public health experts suspect that Russian officials have already sequenced the pathogen involved in Ms. Shipilova's death, and they wonder why that information has not been made public. \"Without that, the risk is uncertain, and I think we should be putting pressure on Russia to share the genome sequence,\" said Dr. Raj Panjabi, a physician and former White House biosecurity official. \"If Y. pestis is confirmed and the presentation was pneumonia, the likely route is inhalation of an infectious aerosol during laboratory work,\" said Dr. Tom Frieden, a physician and former director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. But until more information is released, he added, \"anyone claiming to know more is guessing.\" Thanks to safety protocols and modern personal protective equipment, exposures to pathogens in labs are not common. Y. pestis is a particular concern, Dr. Frieden noted. It requires that laboratories maintain a certain airflow, HEPA filtration, respiratory protection, precautions against aerosols, trained staff members and health monitoring. \"Done right, it's safe,\" Dr. Frieden said. \"The problem is that 'done right' is not verifiable, and it's not easy.\" The last time a fatal exposure to Y. pestis occurred in a laboratory environment was in 2009, when Malcolm Casadaban, a biologist at the University of Chicago, died after contact with a weakened form of the bacterium. When accidents do happen, Dr. Panjabi said, it's important to have a system in place to characterize the outbreak, including how fast the threat is moving, who is at risk and what options are available to mitigate that threat. \"The larger issue of maintaining biosafety in high consequence labs that study dangerous pathogens is a national security issue in every country,\" Dr. Panjabi said. \"And it needs more attention.\"","2026-10-09_001":"Inside the Nobel Prize in literature — 2026/10/09 Inside the Nobel Prize in literature 2026/10/09 Inside the Nobel Prize in literature Alfred Nobel was a keen but mediocre writer. The Swedish dynamite tycoon wrote unpublished poems and a play featuring a 40-minute torture scene. To the dismay of relatives keen to inherit his fortune, his handwritten will of 1895 endowed five awards, including a prize for literature. Nobel instructed \"the Academy in Stockholm\" to give it to the writer of \"the most outstanding work in an ideal [idealisk] direction\". The Swedish Academy spent decades puzzling over what \"ideal\" meant, before quietly dropping the criterion. Next Thursday, a door will open in a gilded hall in Stockholm and the Academy's formally dressed permanent secretary will step forward to announce, in Swedish and English, the winner of this year's literature Nobel. Now prediction markets favour Indian writer Amitav Ghosh, the Chinese avant-garde author Can Xue or the American novelist Thomas Pynchon to win the SKr12mn (£910,000) in prize money — though nobody really knows, as today's Academy is less leaky than it was before a #MeToo scandal prompted a sweeping overhaul. And the pundits usually guess wrong. The prize is ritually mocked. The Nigerian poet Chinweizu Ibekwe once marvelled at \"the conceit that a gaggle of Swedes... should pronounce on intellectual excellence for the whole wide world\". And yet, says Daniel Medin, professor of comparative literature at the American University of Paris: \"The Nobel is the big one for international fiction. There is no comparison.\" The Nobel shapes the global canon — even if the globe can push back. There has always been a comic discrepancy between the weight of the prize and the parochialism of the judges. The Academy's 18 life members — known as \"The Eighteen\" — are Swedish writers, literary historians, linguists and a jurist, some of them obscure even in Sweden. There were barely any female members until this century. The very first award, in 1901, made international news. Somehow nobody nominated Leo Tolstoy, whereas 17 members of the Academie Francaise endorsed the French poet Sully Prudhomme, and he won. The literature prize has never been as controversial as the frankly political Peace Prize; but because greatness in literature is more subjective than in the sciences, the Academy's choice reliably angers critics. They delight in drawing up lists of overlooked greats — James Joyce, Franz Kafka, Virginia Woolf, Marcel Proust, Constantine Cavafy, et cetera — though some of these achieved renown only after death. The prize was at its most politicised during the cold war, when the Academy chose the unreliable Soviet writer Boris Pasternak (1958) and dissidents Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn (1970) and Joseph Brodsky (1987). Pasternak initially accepted the nobelevka, as the award is popularly known in Russia — but then, under pressure from the regime, rejected \"this undeserved prize which has been presented to me\". Jean-Paul Sartre, who refused the Nobel in 1964, noted among his reasons that the prize was \"reserved for the writers of the West or the rebels of the East.\" By the 1980s, the Academy — earlier than most of the Western literary establishment — began looking beyond the west. A \"Nobel committee\" of four or five academicians whittles down the nominations to a shortlist of five, which the Eighteen spend the summer reading. They decide in early October. Translation has become central to the award. The Nobel has helped make the international canon more inclusive. After going global, the prize has recently become gender-equal. This followed a #MeToo scandal in 2017, when 18 women accused the French husband of Academician Katarina Frostenson of sexual assault and rape. He was later jailed. Several members quit — although the body's rules forbid resignation — and the post-scandal \"Eighteen\" included many more women. They awarded the 2018 prize, a year late, to Polish writer Olga Tokarczuk, and there have been three female laureates since 2018. In fact, notes Kaj Schueler, author of a Swedish book on the prize, there's been a precise pattern: male and female victories alternate. If that continues, then after Krasznahorkai's win in 2025, this year's laureate would be a woman, perhaps it will be Xue's year? The winners' reactions are part of the award's lore. John Steinbeck in 1962, when asked whether he deserved the Nobel, replied, \"Frankly, no.\" Many critics agreed. Samuel Beckett (1969) was on holiday in Tunisia with his wife Suzanne when a telegram from his French publisher arrived: \"In spite of everything, they have given you the Nobel Prize. I advise you to go into hiding.\" Suzanne, when told the news, exclaimed, \"Quelle catastrophe.\" Beckett dubbed December 10, the annual date of the award ceremony, \"Nobloodybeldamday\". Bob Dylan, winner in 2016, ignored the Academy's phone calls and didn't acknowledge the prize for over a fortnight.","2026-10-08_001":"Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? — 2026/10/08 Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? 2026/10/08 Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? \"Just go to sleep,\" Martin Chalfie's wife told him late one October night in 2008. Chalfie, a Columbia University chemist, had co-authored an influential paper describing a new method for studying cells with green fluorescent protein, and he was anxiously waiting to see whether it would be recognized. His wife, a fellow academic, tempered his expectations. \"It's a wonderful tool, and lots of people use it,\" he recalls her saying. \"But, to tell you the truth, it will never win the Nobel.\" So when the call from Sweden came in, Chalfie was asleep. This past week, the 2024 Nobel class was introduced to the world, creating a new crop of Olympian figures. Chalfie might tell you that the award hasn't changed his day-to-day life all that much, that his grant applications still sometimes get denied. But the medal radiates from his chest even when he's not wearing it. The Nobel is the honor above all honors. The achievements it rewards—in science, literature, and peace—contribute \"to the greatest benefit of mankind,\" a tagline any other respectable award committee would be too shy to claim. How did a single award from the small nation of Sweden become the undisputed highest honor in the world? Establishing a prize is easy. The hard part is getting anyone to care. Alfred Nobel, a businessman who made his fortune selling dynamite and explosives in the 19th century, was not an obvious candidate to become the namesake of the most prestigious distinction in the world. For the Nobel to become the Nobel, a few people had to get a few things really right. When rich individuals bequeath all their wealth to something other than their would-be heirs, their would-be heirs tend to resist. The Italian businessman Jerome Ponti, for example, had more or less the same vision as Nobel. But after his surviving relatives learned that he had donated his fortune to national academies headquartered in London, Paris, and Vienna in 1874, a group of them successfully litigated to void the will. In the 1890s, the executors of Nobel's estate had to be a bit more clever. They preemptively reached an agreement with Nobel's two living nephews, one of whom was particularly gracious and persuaded the rest of the family to honor the late Nobel's wishes. A second reason the Nobel prevailed over prizes of similar ambition was that Sweden turned it into a nationalist project. Nobel himself did not mention anything about Swedish interests, but the government and the press interpreted his bequest as a call to action for the nation as a whole. \"The Swedish scientific society could not have been invested with a more glorious task,\" a newspaper editorial declared. The effort turned out to be well timed. In the early 1900s, a new prize in Sweden might have seemed unlikely to compete in prestige with one given by the French Academy of Sciences or the Royal Society in London. But when World War I struck, neutral little Sweden had a claim to objectivity that the great powers didn't. Finally, both Nobel and the executors of his will designed the rules wisely. Most prestigious prizes at the time were international only in theory; judges tended to favor their countrymen. Nobel requested that each award be given to the \"worthiest person, whether or not they're Scandinavian.\" The Nobel Prize committee became the first to systematically solicit nominations from institutions abroad, turning the award into a sort of contest among nations. And one of the executors of the will had the idea of rewarding achievements no matter when they had been made, rather than just the work or discoveries of the previous year. This was a clever move, not just because some discoveries can take time to reveal their worth, but also because it turned winning the Nobel into a more sweeping victory. Fortified with its elegant design choices, the Nobel quickly established a first mover advantage. The Scottish economist Angus Deaton told me that nothing comes close to the Nobel ceremony. On the night after he found out he won, Deaton couldn't keep the news to himself. He told his Uber driver on the way home from dinner in Princeton and watched him become ecstatically happy. The driver at first assumed that Deaton had won the peace prize; when he learned it was for economics, his enthusiasm dimmed, but only slightly. A Nobel is a Nobel. Prizes aren't all great. The main ideology the Nobel Foundation appears to be committed to, however, is the importance of preserving the prestige of the Nobel Prize—and in this it has succeeded. If there were a prize for prizes, the Nobel would win every year.","2026-10-07_001":"China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them — 2026/10/07 China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them 2026/10/07 China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them A Post investigation found that criminal networks in Myanmar enjoyed the protection of Chinese officials as well as the military government in Myanmar. For the scion of a crime family linked to human trafficking and enslavement, money laundering and global cyberscams, Wei Qingtao was brazenly public. His Douyin account, the Chinese-language version of TikTok, flaunted the excesses of his life in a remote corner of Myanmar by the border with China: Bentleys and Lamborghinis, rare cigars and private jets. When the 27-year-old partied at the multistory glass-walled nightclub he owned in a region called Kokang, he'd throw crisp Chinese yuan bills into the crowd. In November, the good times rolled to a stop. Wei's social media presence vanished. He soon appeared in a different kind of video: reading a scripted confession while in Chinese custody. \"The money we make from cyberscams comes from the pensions of everyday Chinese people,\" Wei said. \"This time, the Chinese government has made up its mind. ... We must not cling to any illusions.\" Within weeks, his uncle was in cuffs on a plane to China, flanked by dozens of police officers. The clans controlled every aspect of life in Kokang, becoming lawmakers, militia heads, local administrators and powerful business executives. But at the heart of the Kokang empire were casinos, underground banking and money laundering, according to U.N. officials and U.S. diplomatic cables released by WikiLeaks. Newly built mega-casinos in the Kokang capital, Laukkaing, opened 24 hours a day. Behind the vast casino floors, a more sinister industry started to grow. Chinese court documents show that from at least 2018 \"criminal syndicates\" were smuggling people into the Crouching Tiger Villa, a banquet hall and hotel, and forcing them to work as scammers. Border closures that followed the outbreak of the coronavirus pandemic shut off the cash flow to the casinos, accelerating a shift to this new scam economy. Workers, mostly Chinese nationals or ethnic Chinese, were bought, sold and traded; and then beaten, tortured or killed when they didn't reach financial targets or tried to escape, according to firsthand accounts. Many of the compounds for running scam operations were hosted in the same hotels and casino complexes long established by the clans. Others were purpose-built, but had telltale signs like barred windows, high walls and even Myanmar military snipers on the rooftops, witnesses said. A handyman, hired to build soundproof phone booths within a scam compound in a hotel owned by the Liu family, said he was interrupted by screams as he worked. He said he saw a supervisor beating three men, their hands tied behind their backs, with lead pipes. A Taiwanese woman, who was trafficked into Laukkaing, told The Post she was waterboarded when she tried to escape. A Chinese man, who was forcibly brought into Kokang by knife-wielding men after responding to an acting job online in China, said he saw others in his compound chained and beaten. At one point, four people were shot when they fought back during beatings and tried to seize guns from the guards, he added in an interview. The Crouching Tiger Villa was among the most brutally run compounds, witnesses said. There, caged lions, tigers and bears were used to threaten workers who stepped out of line, according to a person who led a team of workers there. By 2023, Beijing was coming under growing domestic and international pressure to act against these operations. The United Nations estimated in a report last year that more than 120,000 people were being forced to work in Myanmar. Signs of official Chinese concern burst into the open in August when the Chinese ambassador warned Myanmar authorities to \"eradicate the cancer of gambling and scams\" that was \"deeply loathed by the Chinese people.\" At the Crouching Tiger Villa on Oct. 20, guards opened fire when more than 100 people tried to leave. The Post could not verify how many died, but four undercover Chinese police officers were among those killed. Confession videos featuring Wei Qingtao and two others — individuals from the Liu family and the Ming family — began to appear on Chinese media. Two of the Mings were arrested in Myanmar by local police and handed over to China. In December, China issued arrest warrants for 10 more heads of the Bai, Wei and Liu families, and their children. On Jan. 6, Laukkaing, the capital of Kokang, fell to the MNDAA, returning full control of the region to the rebel group for the first time in 15 years. Since last July, 49,000 scam workers have been returned home to China, according to the Ministry of Public Security. The casinos and hotels owned by the Kokang crime families still stand, but have gone dark. Hanley Interstellar, Wei's club, is deserted. So too is the Crouching Tiger Villa, its walls crumbling and pockmarked with bullet holes, verified videos show. But some of the exotic animals — a tiger and five bears — survived the conflict. They still roam their cages, depending on rebel soldiers to bring their daily meal of chicken.","2026-10-06_001":"The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind — 2026/10/06 The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind 2026/10/06 The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind There's a common fear among people who work in Silicon Valley: snag one of the fast-growing, high-paying jobs in artificial intelligence, or get trapped in the \"permanent underclass\", a phrase describing the fate of those who won't have upward mobility in the age of AI. The phrase usually describes a future in which AI automates human jobs. But for women in tech, it's starting to look like the present. \"A lot of people say: 'Oh, AI is lowering barriers, it's equalizing the playing field for women,\" said Urvashi Batra, co-founder and CEO of Prioriwise, an AI platform for IT service providers. \"I actually think it's the opposite.\" Batra said people take her less seriously as the founder of an AI company than her male co-founder. When pitching investors, she and her co-founder have learned that they are more likely to get an investment if he does the pitching. Women made up only about a quarter of new hires in AI roles in the last year, compared to 50% of new hires in non-AI roles, according to a recent report from LinkedIn. In executive roles, that number drops to just 13%. At the same time, LinkedIn's research shows that women are more likely to work in roles with high exposure to AI disruption, like customer service, meaning they are also at higher risk for job loss due to AI. AI jobs, whose postings have doubled since 2023, come with a salary premium, on average paying more than twice as much as roles that don't involve working with AI. But women who do have jobs in AI are disproportionately concentrated in low-paying roles, like data annotators. Across all AI occupations, men have $45,000 higher median pay than women, partly due to the types of jobs they are likelier to have. The ongoing trend could produce the greatest gender pay gap in generations and leave women out of building a technology that shaped the economy. \"What we're witnessing is a sort of backsliding,\" said Brenda Darden Wilkerson, president of AnitaB.org, a non-profit devoted to advancing women in tech jobs. Initiatives to promote women in the tech industry have been ongoing for years, with mixed results. Women now hold about one-third of tech jobs in the US. But research has found that women leave the tech industry at a much higher rate than their male counterparts, citing factors like non-inclusive cultures and barriers to advancement. Women working in AI may face even greater challenges. \"AI moves extremely fast, and the pressure to keep up is immense,\" said Jayeeta Putatunda, an AI engineering lead at investment firm Turing. \"I have seen throughout my career so many amazing women I know kind of fall through the cracks.\" Putatunda said it's not uncommon to be the only female engineer on a team, and that when women are outnumbered, they have to speak up louder to make their ideas known. Mentorship in the AI field, especially by other women, can be hard to come by. And the breakneck pace of AI advancements means anyone working in the field should expect to put in long hours in order to keep up. Those kinds of work expectations can make it impossible for everyone to stay in the field. \"I went on maternity leave for four months, and by the time I came back, there were completely different frameworks and levels of models,\" Putatunda said. Getting caught back up was overwhelming. She said it was only possible with the help of supportive co-workers and a husband who equitably split childcare. The AI industry is so new and fast-moving that it should in theory be more meritocratic. No one has a decade of experience working with a model developed one year ago, which should mean everyone has a level playing field. But instead the field has indexed more on personal networks and other loose signals, according to some women who work in AI. The political climate has made it more challenging to get AI companies to support initiatives focused on women, said Felicia Newhouse, founder of AI Powered Women, an organization that promotes women's participation and leadership in AI. Newhouse, a product marketer who has worked in technology companies for two decades, points to the Department of Justice targeting corporations with DEI programs, claiming the initiatives violate anti-discrimination laws. Still, she said the risk that women are left behind in the AI workforce was something that kept her up at night. \"We're talking about who captures a major new source of economic mobility,\" she said. \"The deepest risk is that a participation gap becomes a power gap.\"","2026-10-05_001":"Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs — 2026/10/05 Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs 2026/10/05 Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs Patience, consistency and frugality are fine habits for an investor. They are not, however, common virtues among newborn babies. The little bundles of joy are impulsive short-term thinkers. They are also some of nature's biggest freeloaders, living entirely beyond their means. All the same, for new parents with a soft spot for the magic of compounding, like your columnist, a baby presents a tantalising investment opportunity. Many decades of life and potential returns stretch out into the future: $1,000 invested today with a real annual return of 5.2% (the average for global stocks over the past 125 years) would be worth $58,000 by the time the child is 80. Yet these juicy financial returns must be considered alongside another sort of investment: in the children themselves. Spending on a child's health, education and skills can yield even higher returns (on top of helping them lead a fulfilling life). So how should a new parent think about their budding little portfolio? Potential financial investments are manifold. Many countries offer tax-advantaged ways for parents to save for their children. American tykes are now eligible for Trump accounts, which start with a $1,000 freebie from the federal government that can be put into mutual funds or index trackers until the child turns 18, while taxes are deferred. Earlier options include the even more tax-friendly 529 accounts, but these may be used solely for specified educational purposes. In Britain, the Junior Individual Savings Account, into which up to £9,000 ($11,900) a year can be invested, is tax friendlier still. Investing for babies is a joy even without the tax perks. Long horizons smooth out volatility, allowing for more risk. Ian Ayres and Barry Nalebuff, two economists, propose that young workers should borrow to buy stocks, since the long-term returns to equities are (hopefully) higher than the interest on borrowing. If the logic is true for 20-somethings, it is truer still for infants with a horizon a couple of decades longer. Some parents fear that creating a large trust fund will turn their offspring into layabouts. Andrew Carnegie, a 19th-century American steel magnate, gave away most of his wealth out of fear that it would dull the work ethic of his descendants. A more recent study of Norwegian inheritances between 2000 and 2004 found that those bequeathed NKr300,000 (some $40,000 at the time) or more reduced their working hours by 7-10% in the six years that followed. This makes investments in human capital more appealing. In the 1970s and 1980s Gary Becker and Nigel Tomes, another pair of economists, theorised that parents were better off investing in education and skills early on. For example, teaching a child to read is not hugely expensive, but opens up huge opportunities for future earnings. Investing in financial assets starts to make sense later, once those human-capital investments encounter diminishing returns. A wealth of empirical evidence has since backed up the theory. Beyond basic skills, however, investing in human capital gets fraught. Such investments are sunk costs that cannot be liquidated. They are also infungible: tutoring in advanced maths is not the same as Chinese lessons. Children react differently (and unpredictably) to each. The extraordinarily long timeframes that make investing for babies attractive also raise uncertainty. Perhaps the skills that are valuable today will be obsolete in an AI future? So what is a new parent to do? The most obvious financial options involve accepting free money. The $1,000 in a Trump account is a no-brainer. Even rabid anti-Trumpers would be mad not to take it. All parents should also accept similar grants from several American states. Those who expect to be on the hook for university tuition would be wise to take advantage of 529 accounts or similar vehicles as early as possible, so that compounding can work its magic to the full. Any money that children are likely eventually to inherit is better off in tax-friendly shelters than in their parents' brokerage accounts. Investments can misfire: violin lessons may generate little more than screeching and even a modest trust fund could encourage laziness. But with a bit of luck, investing in a child's future and on their behalf will pay off handsomely. Fortunate parents with the ability to do both should approach this like the rest of parenting: try different things and don't agonise too much over your decisions.","2026-10-04_001":"A hurricane is coming to tax systems — 2026/10/04 A hurricane is coming to tax systems 2026/10/04 A hurricane is coming to tax systems THE YIELD on ten-year US Treasuries has charged through the 5% barrier. The spread between French government bonds and German Bunds is at levels not seen since the euro-zone crisis in 2012. Britain's newish prime minister has this week set out a plan for spending bolstered by a taboo-busting promise to curb state-pension increases—and yet is still in a fiscal pickle. Across the rich world, countries are struggling to coax more growth out of their economies. And that's not the worst of it. As we explain this week, the tax base in all countries, which depends heavily on taxing labour, may be upended by artificial intelligence. Add these problems together, and it is as if a mighty hurricane were about to hit the rich world's tax systems—which are hopelessly unprepared. The way the state raises money is wasteful, unfair and perverse. You would have thought governments would be scrambling to strengthen the system before the hurricane makes landfall, but as ever they are running away from tax reform instead. The accumulated flaws of modern tax systems are so ubiquitous that they have become part of the economic backdrop. Taxes are full of needless complexity. In Germany the rate of value-added tax (VAT) on oat milk is more than double that on cow's milk, for example. Such widespread special treatment has ruinous fiscal consequences. Across the OECD, a club of mostly rich countries, consumption taxes raise only 58% of what they could if there were no carve-outs and no evasion. Tax systems also create perverse incentives. When a parent in Britain increases their net income from £99,999 ($131,500) to £100,000, they automatically lose free child care worth thousands of pounds. This can prompt some of Britain's most productive workers to cut their hours or overstuff their pensions. And taxes are widely seen as unfair. In Britain the median employee pays 55% more tax than their self-employed counterparts. Many people feel the rich aren't doing their bit. In California voters will decide in November whether to charge a one-off 5% levy on residents with wealth of over $1bn. No one knows when the fiscal weather will turn. But rapid advances in AI could accelerate matters. Taxes on labour are the workhorse of most tax systems worldwide, raising around half of all revenue in the OECD. In the coming years AI could well displace workers or cut their wages even as corporate profits rise, shrinking labour's share of GDP. This in turn could reduce revenues and increase benefits payments, making deficits even harder to support. Optimists may hope that superfast growth will make the maths easier to handle. Yet The Economist has looked at what would happen in rich countries if labour's share of national income fell by ten points. By our calculations, Italy's budget deficit would double as a share of GDP and Germany and France would not fare much better. In addition, the capacity for tax avoidance would no longer be limited by human ingenuity. Point countless AI agents at Britain's 23,000-page tax code and hundreds of new tax-avoidance strategies are likely to emerge—and don't bet on the Treasury to keep up. What to do? Given that AI is likely to mint vast fortunes for a select few, countries may be inclined to follow California's proposal and introduce wealth taxes. Yet such taxes will probably backfire, either because they are too difficult to administer or because they cause rich people to flee. Previous attempts have proved unworkable. In 1990, 12 rich countries taxed fortunes; today just four do. A more fundamental overhaul is needed. Wealth should be taxed more via capital-gains taxes, which currently overpenalise normal returns and undertax supernormal ones. A start would be to introduce a higher headline rate and allowances that exempt normal returns. At the same time, the burden on labour should lighten. That would be a hedge against disruption from AI, and remove an obstacle to employment in countries where labour taxes are crushing. In Belgium they constitute 52% of the costs of employing a person. Consumption should be taxed more heavily, with a focus on ending the litany of exemptions. That would have the useful side-effect of taxing the spending of the very rich. This might not curb the influence Elon Musk can buy, but it would ensure the state gets a bigger cut when Mr Musk buys another plane. Finally, codes should be radically simplified. None of this will be easy. The losers will hiss more loudly than the winners will cheer. But change is coming, like it or not. The country that gets ahead of the hurricane will one day thank the politicians who had the foresight to act.","2026-10-03_001":"A Near Disaster on Flydubai — 2026/10/03 A Near Disaster on Flydubai 2026/10/03 A Near Disaster on Flydubai This unbelievable sequence of events has huge implications. Today, somewhere over Saudi Arabia, the co-pilot of an Israel-bound Flydubai Boeing 737 stabbed the other pilot and pitched the nose of the plane toward the desert below. The jet descended 17,000 feet in two minutes, at an acceleration violent enough to make your phone and in-flight snack jump off your tray and be pinned to the ceiling. Other crew members and at least one Israeli passenger broke into the cockpit and subdued the assailant. Relief pilots then took over and landed the plane in Tabuk, Saudi Arabia. After a second Flydubai plane took the 174 passengers to Tel Aviv, one of the passengers who’d stormed the cockpit told Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu that his actions had been effective in part because he had watched the disaster-porn documentary series Mayday, about aviation catastrophes, and had known to pull back on the yoke. Whether today’s near-disaster was terrorism or merely attempted mass murder depends on the attacker’s motive. Either way, it is a public-relations catastrophe for Flydubai, the budget cousin of the airline Emirates. Since the United Arab Emirates opened diplomatic relations with Israel in 2020, the airline has connected the two countries with few interruptions, even for acts of war and terror. Keeping that air route open and safe has taken a great deal of effort. Last year, I witnessed clever security engineering at Dubai’s terminal 1: placement of the check-in desk for El Al, the Israeli carrier, adjacent to the desk for Iran’s Mahan Air. Anyone tempted to attack Israeli passengers would hit Iranian ones, and vice versa. Checked baggage costs extra, but the experience of being a human shield while waiting in line is complimentary. Mahan Air no longer flies into Dubai. U.S. sanctions have halted much of its operations, and Dubai had already curtailed its flights, on the grounds that Iran can fly either missiles into Dubai or commercial flights, but not both. Israel, by contrast, has emerged from the 2026 Iran war closer to the UAE, which has absorbed more Iranian attacks than any other country. Although the Arab countries of the region have been allied as the Gulf Cooperation Council since 1981, that alliance proved useless as soon as the missiles and drones started flying. But Israel responded as an ally, by sending defense systems and advisers. At least one Iranian strike during the early phases of the war hit near the Israeli consulate in Dubai. One possible political motive for the Flydubai incident would be to strain and punish the relationship between the two countries. The relationship between Israel and the UAE is unlikely to snap over a single attack. The relationship between the UAE and Netanyahu himself is more delicate. In the past several weeks, the president of the UAE, Mohammed bin Zayed Al Nahyan, was implicated in a report from Haaretz that severely damaged Netanyahu’s chances of remaining in office after next month’s Israeli election. The report alleged that days before the massacre of October 7, 2023, MBZ had personally warned Netanyahu that an attack was imminent but Netanyahu had done nothing to act on the warning. The UAE government at first refused to comment on the story. On Sunday, however, Netanyahu visited Abu Dhabi, and the AP reported that he asked MBZ to deny that he’d warned of an attack. Yesterday, in New York, UAE Foreign Minister Abdullah bin Zayed did exactly that. \"Sheikh Mohammed did not call Prime Minister Netanyahu and say there is going to be an attack on Israel,\" Abdullah said in a meeting at the Council on Foreign Relations, calling his denial \"categorical.\" He said that using a leader-to-leader call to pass along secrets was \"not our style,\" and he left open the possibility that a warning had been passed between governments, if not between leaders. This denial releases some pressure on Netanyahu without absolving his government of its failures. The New York Times and others have reported that the first officer who allegedly tried to bring down the plane is an Omani national. Both are reportedly still in Saudi Arabia. The next part of the story will involve determining whether the pilot acted alone—not a good look for Flydubai, to have suicidal or terrorist pilots—or at someone’s behest. The latter possibility could, if confirmed, have serious implications, depending on who directed the attack. Many have a motive: Sunni jihadists (al-Qaeda or the Islamic State), Hamas, and of course Iran and its proxies. This Mayday moment will have huge implications if any of these groups are involved.","2026-10-02_001":"How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry — 2026/10/02 How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry 2026/10/02 How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry \"Halt, stop right there,\" shouts a Peruvian Navy Seal, firing warning shots into the air. But the fleeing man pays no attention, scrambling up to the rim of a football pitch-sized crater and disappearing into the dense rainforest as the commandos give chase, swearing and shooting. Meanwhile, other marines set dynamite charges on a makeshift wooden raft that floats in the crater, which is essentially an enormous pit of opaque, stagnant water. The raft has a diesel-fuelled suction pump motor with thick plastic tubing used to suck up and sift through river sediment for tiny particles of gold. Soon after, a mushroom of orange and black fumes envelops the trees – and seconds later comes the sound of the explosion, shattering the peace of the Tambopata National Reserve, one of Peru’s most prized Amazon parks. In total, more than 140,000 hectares have been lost to mining in Madre de Dios – a shocking 97.5% of the total deforestation caused by mining in Peru’s Amazon rainforest, an area twice the size of the US state of California. Military crackdowns on illegal mining in the Amazon are not new in Peru. But the skyrocketing international price of gold has caused this destructive activity to spread, empowering local gangs and attracting transnational organised crime. \"Dirty\" gold has become the country’s most profitable illicit industry, even surpassing drug trafficking in the world’s second-biggest producer of cocaine after Colombia. \"Gold prices have risen,\" said Gen Victor Cotrina, the military commander of Madre de Dios, who is leading the crackdown on illegal gold mining. \"That’s why, if a miner’s motor is destroyed, he’ll replace it quickly because gold is at its peak price: the very next day he’s back to his mining activities. He’s back at work again. \"For troops used to combating drug trafficking by targeting well-hidden plantations of coca, which is used to make cocaine, the fight against illegal gold presents new challenges as the end product is not an illicit drug but a legally traded commodity, Cotrina said. \"The difference is that an open-pit mining operation doesn’t require covert operations, whereas drug trafficking does.\" He said previous anti-mining crackdowns had been ineffective due to corruption and patchy enforcement. But now, with \"aerial surveillance and military personnel who are available 24/7 to conduct patrols, we are determined to eliminate mining in this region\". The evidence is not difficult to find or destroy. Behind the wall of green that marks the edge of the reserve, troops and environmental prosecutors set ablaze a network of well-supplied mining camps, built with timber and blue tarpaulins and stocked with food and beer. A lone cockerel crows as the abandoned camps go up in flames. Two women had been left behind by the fleeing miners: Andrea, 30, wept as she begged the officers not to arrest her, explaining she was a single mother who had been working as a cook and could find no other work to support a sick mother and two children. Liz Compi, 20, was found sitting on a boat with her one-year-old son. She said she had taken the job as a cook for $450 a month after being abandoned by her partner and sacked from a previous job. The dirty gold departs Peru as contraband to Bolivia and Brazil, but much of it is laundered into the legal trade via a controversial registration system called Reinfo. The system, in theory, should bring informal miners into the legal economy, but critics say it allows them to continue working without environmental permits and laundering illegal gold through formal companies. The trade is so lucrative that members of Congress have backed five repeated extensions of Reinfo, despite pushes to reform or eliminate the registry. Since the beginning of Covid-19 pandemic, the price of gold has tripled. Tens of thousands of miners have poured into protected Amazon parks and Indigenous reserves, stripping away vast tracts of rainforest and leaving behind a denuded, pocked terrain, stagnant pools and disrupted river courses. Mercury is the cheapest and easiest way to extract gold because it binds with the particles in the river sediment. \"The government’s response is moving very slowly,\" said Flores after another day of relatively fruitless jungle raids, destroying camps that are likely to spring back up soon after. \"Meanwhile, illegal mining operates 24 hours a day. The miners measure time in grams, not minutes, so we need to rethink how we investigate mining,\" he said.","2026-10-01_001":"A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant — 2026/10/01 A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant 2026/10/01 A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant For three and a half years, ever since the spring of 2023, when SpaceX launched the first of its massive Starship rockets, the company has been promising that before too long the towering, stainless steel machine would get to orbit. This morning, on the 14th such launch, it made good on that boast. \"Starship performs its orbital insertion burn and enters orbit of Earth for the first time,\" SpaceX announced on X at 9:15 a.m. ET, along with a video of that engine maneuver. It took just 25 minutes and 17 seconds for the spacecraft to travel from its launch pad in Starbase, Texas, to its circular orbit 170 miles high. There is no overstating how important today's success was to SpaceX's—and NASA's—long-term plans. Starship is pivotal to getting human beings to the moon and Mars, and SpaceX sees it as an ultimate replacement for its workhorse Falcon 9 rocket, which accounted for nearly 51% of the global total of commercial launches— including 85% of all satellites—in 2025. Starship's far larger payload bay can carry a cargo equivalent of nearly five Falcon 9s, ultimately consolidating launches and cutting costs. In 2021, NASA selected Starship as one of two spacecraft that will land astronauts on the moon as early as 2028.(Blue Origin, the aerospace company founded by Amazon boss Jeff Bezos, will provide the other.) In 2027, NASA is planning a crewed test of either or both spacecraft in Earth orbit during the mission of Artemis III. Early on, routine was not remotely how Starship launches were being described. Four minutes into the rocket's inaugural launch on April 20, 2023, it exploded spectacularly. Concrete and steel debris rained down in a near-400-acre footprint in the surrounding Boca Chica State Park, and a 3.85-acre fire burned south of the launch site. But the flights proceeded apace at a rate of once every three months, none achieving stable orbit— until today. The launch this morning was both imperfect and stripped down to its orbital essentials. On the way up, one of the Starship's six engines failed to burn properly, requiring the other engines to compensate for the missing thrust to get the ship in orbit. In addition, the return to Earth was simplified. Starship's first stage, meantime, performs what has become known as a chopstick recovery, with the booster navigating its way back to the launch tower where two giant metal arms pluck it from the sky. For the current mission, the chopstick recovery was done away with to simplify the flight objectives; instead the first stage made a soft, engine-assisted splashdown in the Gulf of Mexico. The Starship spacecraft was planned for a six-orbit, 10-hour mission. Today's launch was not just a test flight; it was also an operational mission designed to deliver 26 Starlink satellites to orbit, adding to the whopping 11,000 that already comprise the Starlink constellation—with an eventual goal of 100,000. SpaceX released footage of Starship deploying the satellites with what it has dubbed its Pez dispenser system, because that's exactly what the spacecraft resembles as it releases its payload one satellite at a time. Ultimately, the spacecraft was brought down early, after just three orbits, since its satellites had all been deployed and the ship proved its flight worthiness quickly. The launch of Starship 14 is in many ways only a very small—if critical—step. Even the biggest SpaceX supporters don't pretend that Starship is anywhere near ready to land astronauts on the moon in 2028. The spacecraft—which is now essentially a giant, flying, cargo truck—has to be adapted to carry crew, with all of the environmental and habitat modifications that implies. It will also have to solve a stubborn refueling problem: before heading out for the moon, Starship will need to enter a parking orbit around the Earth where up to a dozen other Starship-sized tankers will top off its tanks. It will have to fly its Artemis III test flight in 2027, and later, an uncrewed landing on—and liftoff from—the moon before it will be trusted to carry astronauts in as little as two years' time. Still, SpaceX promises that an Earth-orbital launch that today is headline-making could become almost as commonplace as the takeoff of a commercial airliner. Elon Musk, the company's founder and CEO, has spoken openly about Starships launching as frequently as once a day—or even once an hour. Maybe. For now the company is celebrating one launch on one day, on a mission that didn't even last past east coast lunchtime. But spaceflight is iterative and incremental. And today SpaceX moved one big increment indeed.","2026-09-30_001":"How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West — 2026/09/30 How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West 2026/09/30 How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West It's the rare factory that doubles as a tourist attraction. But more than 300,000 visitors have streamed through Xiaomi's sprawling \"hyperfactory\" here since the company began offering tours of its automobile assembly lines two years ago. On display is a vision of manufacturing far removed from the labor-intensive operations that characterized China's emergence as an economic power a quarter century ago. Industrial robots painted a brilliant white pivot and twirl around half-finished electric sedans. Compact unmanned load carriers, chirping a melodic warning for the few humans they encounter, scoot across the floor in a vast factory larger than the Pentagon. \"They have gone all in on automation and robotics and minimizing labor and maximizing efficiency,\" said Michael Dunne, an auto industry consultant in San Diego. \"You will find in China's auto industry the most advanced plants in the world, by far.\" And not just in the auto industry. Chinese manufacturers' automation, innovative designs, world-class logistics and government support have vaulted them to global supremacy in an ever-longer list of technologically advanced products. Once known for using cheap labor to produce inexpensive clothes and toys, Chinese factories increasingly specialize in some of the world's most sophisticated merchandise, including feature-rich electric vehicles and robotics. Only Britain at the dawn of the Industrial Revolution and the United States following World War II have so dominated the global production of goods. China now accounts for 32 percent of global manufacturing value added, twice its share in 2010, and more than the combined total of the next four biggest manufacturing powers - the United States, Germany, Japan and South Korea - according to the U.N. Industrial Development Organization. Take BYD, China's largest carmaker. The BYD Seal, which is comparable to the Tesla Model 3, includes as standard equipment a rotating 15.6-inch touch screen, dual wireless charging pads and an interior multicolor lighting system that pulses along with ambient sound or music. But its late-2025 sticker price of $24,190 was more than $8,000 lower than the Tesla sells for in China, according to the Rhodium Group, a New York consultancy. Government subsidies, meanwhile, account for just $235 of the $4,700-per-vehicle price advantage BYD holds across its fleet over Elon Musk's Tesla, Rhodium said. \"There's a lot of technological advancement taking place in China,\" said Louis Kuijs, chief economist, Asia Pacific, for S&P Global Ratings. \"The Chinese companies are just very good and very fast, and the price-quality ratio is very attractive.\" For years, China's manufacturing gains were seen as the flip side of American factory losses; jobs disappeared from the U.S. industrial heartland only to reappear in Asia. But in recent years, instead of adding bodies to its assembly lines, China has been deploying collaborative robots or co-bots, designed to work alongside humans; conveyors; and automated visual quality inspection systems, according to AlixPartners, a New York-based consultancy. Robot-rich Chinese auto plants can produce automobiles for 25 to 30 percent less than in the U.S. or Europe. While Chinese wages have increased in recent years, overall labor costs per vehicle remain much lower than in advanced economies. The reason: Chinese cars are designed to be easier to make, meaning fewer hours of labor are needed. \"It isn't because of low-cost labor. It's because of efficiencies attributable to proximity to supply chains and this high degree of automation,\" said Dunne, formerly president of General Motors operations in Indonesia. Inside Xiaomi's facility about 90 minutes from the center of Beijing, 700 robots perform an industrial choreography that delivers a new car every 76 seconds. \"Xiaomi's car factory has achieved a high level of automation, with most processes completed entirely by machines,\" Lei Jun, Xiaomi's CEO, told Chinese reporters last year. Xiaomi (pronounced Shao-mee) rose to prominence in China as a leading maker of smartphones before branching out into wearables, tablets and Internet-connected appliances. It began making passenger cars in early 2024, and its tech-forward approach quickly won glowing reviews. Ford flew a Xiaomi SU-7 electric sedan from Shanghai to Chicago so that CEO Jim Farley could eyeball the competition. After driving the car, Farley pronounced Xiaomi \"an industry juggernaut.\" Many of those extra vehicles are bound for other markets. Through August, Chinese auto exports rose by more than 50 percent compared with the same period in 2025, approaching 7.5 million vehicles, according to China's General Administration of Customs. In response, the company is adopting a familiar strategy: Some time next year, the first Xiaomi cars will begin arriving in Germany.","2026-09-29_001":"Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift — 2026/09/29 Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift 2026/09/29 Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift Pain at the pump for rural Republican is shifting the political map Diesel prices soaring to record highs this past week have caused Republican candidates in solid-red parts of the country to call for an end to President Donald Trump's war with Iran and for a ban on diesel exports, as the issue rattles the party's MAGA base in a way that few have in this midterm election year. \"Michigan families are struggling with the price of everyday life,\" Mike Rogers, the state's GOP nominee for U.S. Senate, said in an ad this past week. The war has sent oil prices surging, with disruptions to shipping through the Strait of Hormuz, a vital route for global energy supplies, reverberating through fuel markets. But while poll after poll has shown voters concerned about the cost of gasoline, it's the price of diesel — a far less obvious consumer product — that has riled up the industrial and agricultural interests that have been among the most faithful Republican supporters. The political fallout is landing in deep-red voting districts the hardest. Federal data shows these tend to be the places where diesel makes up the largest proportion of the overall fuel mix. This is especially the case in farming communities, where diesel powers the fuel-thirsty tractors and other machinery that the agriculture industry relies on. That has led lawmakers to demand what was unthinkable in the GOP only a couple of months ago: a ban on diesel exports from the United States. The proposal seems to make sense on its face: restrict the flow of the fuel out of the U.S. to boost the supply domestically, quickly lowering prices. The problem is that almost nobody who understands oil markets — including Energy Secretary Chris Wright — believes it would work out that way. Analysts who have been modeling the impact of a diesel export ban are consistently warning it would create chaos and hurt consumers. The Gulf Coast refineries that typically export diesel abroad would get overloaded with a glut of crude that would have to be diverted to storage. The diesel they make would mostly be stranded in their region, because there are no pipelines to get it across the country. Parts of the country that rely on imports of diesel could then see prices soar even higher than they are now. \"It is not at all clear that the national price of diesel would fall,\" the Energy Policy Research Foundation said Thursday in a report. In Iowa, where farmer anger over the price of diesel has reached a boiling point and endangered the GOP's hold over a Senate seat in a state Trump won by 13 percentage points in 2024, conservative lawmakers are rushing to embrace an export ban. The Iowa Corn Growers Association, which has endorsed all Republicans in this year's Iowa congressional races, wrote to the state's delegation in Washington this past week pleading for action to lower diesel prices. The record highs are especially ill-timed because they hit right at harvest, the association noted. \"This time of year should be positive for Iowa farmers, but the concern of affordability looms large as diesel prices go above $6 per gallon,\" the letter said. \"This is accelerating the economic and mental stress farmers are feeling.\" Steve Kuiper, a farmer and head of the association, said that diesel is indispensable to his work. As the harvest gets in gear, the tractor-trailers that deliver equipment to farms and haul away harvested crops, as well as the tractors and combines and harvesters that do the work, all run on diesel, Kuiper said. His own operation uses some 500 gallons of diesel per day, he said. Fuel he contracted for last year is running at about half the cost of fuel purchased now; topping off one truck Thursday cost $500, Kuiper said. \"Farming is not a huge-margin business. This quickly cuts into it,\" he said. The price of fertilizer is going up at the same time, he said, and all of those costs will be passed on to consumers. Kuiper does not specifically blame Trump or other Republicans for the situation, which he said has been building for years and could be mitigated by steps such as an embargo on diesel exports or allowing more ethanol to be mixed in with petroleum products. But the situation puts all politicians in a negative light. \"All our elected officials in D.C. need to do their job and get things taken care of,\" Kuiper said.","2026-09-28_001":"The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining — 2026/09/28 The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining 2026/09/28 The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining When Andrew Stinger's mother, Nancy, died of cancer in 2024, her grief-stricken husband and sons began the journey of managing her estate in probate court — a process that would stretch almost two years. Then, in January 2025, Stinger's father, Bill, died unexpectedly. And yet, there was one happy spot amid the heartbreak: the jewelry. \"There were pieces that my mom inherited from her mother that it made sense for my mom's sisters to have, and to be able to pass down to their daughters,\" Stinger says. \"My mom's engagement ring will go to her only granddaughter when she grows up. These things are part of our family lineage. \" In the next 20 years, some $93 trillion in assets in the US will be passed down from baby boomers and the Silent Generation to their heirs. Along with that will come a whole lot of physical items: the china, silver, coin and stamp collections, and millions and millions of baseball cards that make up what's known as the Great Stuff Transfer. Jewelry is the rare exception. The reasons are both obvious and intimate: To begin with, silver, gold, platinum and gemstones have material value. With gold prices climbing back toward their January high (when the spot price was $5,589.38 per ounce, five times what it was 10 years earlier) and silver on a similar trajectory, it's a good time to come into some precious metal. According to a 2026 report from the jewelry tracking startup Unvault Inc., the average US household is sitting on $5,700 in unappraised gold jewelry, with roughly $750 billion in gold weight in drawers across the country. Add to that silver, platinum and pieces with artistic merit that can command high premiums. Unlike art, old furniture or Dickens houses, the infrastructure for offloading jewelry at almost any price point is expansive and well established. From estate-sale specialists, pawnshops and local jewelers to high-end auction houses to eBay and the RealReal, there are options for pretty much everything you could inherit. If you've come into some jewelry or even expect to, it's crucial to know its value — for insurance, for deciding who in a family will get what and for potential division of cash outcomes, says Nidhi Singhvi. A former hedge funder who later worked in crypto, Singhvi started Unvault last October to bring transparency to the secondhand jewelry market. \"I'm Indian. We do gold very differently in India,\" she says. Gold jewelry, like 22-karat bracelets and necklaces, are \"transferred from generation to generation with the idea that it can be used for anything,\" she says. \"My mom used gold to send me for a school exchange program. The market is very transparent, and there is very good financial infrastructure for selling it. \" To Singhvi, the American system of aggressive storefronts with big \"WE BUY GOLD\" posters in the window was a turnoff. She knew a lot of women who wouldn't set foot in a pawnshop. Instead, her platform uses artificial intelligence to assess the jewelry in clients' collections and assign it a clear value based on the weight of the metal and the carats and quality of the gemstones. With Unvault, you can build a digital portfolio of what you own by sending in photographs and watch its value rise and fall with the price of gold. When you're ready to offload it, you can sell directly to Unvault, which will deduct a 10% to 20% fee and then pass the interesting pieces and stones to a network of estate jewelers, and melt down the rest. \"For me, a key part of such intergenerational transitions is not being afraid to repurpose, reset or recast things,\" says Andrew James Hamilton, a curator at the Art Institute of Chicago. \"My grandmother had a pair of opal earrings that my mother inherited and turned into two tie tacks for her gay grandsons, my brother and me,\" he says. \"Earrings can become cuff links, rings, pendants. And with the price of precious metals as high as it is, things can be melted down and recast as something else equally precious. \" \"After the Holocaust, my grandparents immigrated to Israel, and my grandmother always wore a gold Magen David Jewish star and an infinity necklace that looked like an 'S' to honor her roots and love of my grandfather,\" says Ronnit Hershowitz, a marketing director in New York. \"It was passed down to me. My grandfather always got choked up seeing it because it was more than just a necklace. For them, it represented eternal strength and love. \"","2026-09-27_001":"Scientists create mice with part-human brains — 2026/09/27 Scientists create mice with part-human brains 2026/09/27 Scientists create mice with part-human brains Researchers have created mice with half-human brains in an effort to understand and develop new treatments for disorders such as schizophrenia, epilepsy, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability and rare forms of dementia. The scientists transplanted lab-grown human brain cells into animals that were engineered to be born without a cortex or hippocampus. This made space for the human tissue to grow inside the rodents' skulls. The procedure means scientists can now take cells from patients with brain disorders, turn them into brain tissue in the laboratory, and grow that tissue in living animals. The animals can then be studied to see how the disorder takes hold in human brain tissue, and how drugs might treat the conditions. \"We've been trying really hard as a community to find therapeutic solutions for these conditions, but the reality is that in psychiatry and neurology we've been left behind [by] every single branch of medicine and we have fewer therapeutics than, again, every single branch of medicine,\" said Sergiu Pașca, a professor of psychiatry who led the research at Stanford University. \"That could be because the human brain is very complex, but it's also because the human brain is inaccessible,\" he added. \"To a large extent, our goal has been to make aspects of human brain development and function accessible for investigation.\" While potentially transformative for brain medicine, organoids have sparked a wave of ethical concerns, not least around whether the clumps of tissue could become conscious or feel pain, and the welfare of animals implanted with organoids. Pașca said the work had received extensive ethical oversight from the start. Experts said this would need to continue. Emily Jackson, professor of law at London School of Economics and chair of a recent report on neural organoids for the Nuffield Council on Bioethics, said: \"Animal welfare is a really important concern, and it will be necessary to closely monitor these animals in order to evaluate the impact on them.\" Writing in Nature, Pașca describes how the mice were injected with human brain organoids that were themselves created by reprogramming donated skin cells. The newborn mice received several injections, each containing about 100,000 human brain cells, into the space where their own brain tissue was missing. In total, the rodents lacked about 14m mouse brain cells and ended up with about 4m human ones – half the brain by volume. Three months after surgery, the human tissue had hooked up to the mouse's blood supply and almost entirely filled the cavity, taking up about half the size of the rodent's brain. Some of the human neurons formed connections with mouse brain cells and spinal cord. The human neurons were not structured or wired up in the same way as in people, and the brain tissue was immature, equivalent to that found halfway through human pregnancy. Tests on the \"xenocortical\" mice showed the animals were not enhanced by the transplants, but their shaky gait and cognitive problems did improve a little. To demonstrate how the mice could shed light on human brain disorders, the researchers exposed some of the animals to five hours of low oxygen. This showed how vulnerable human nerve cells are to oxygen deprivation, which in pregnancy and birth can cause cerebral palsy. The researchers found that human brain tissue in the mice contained rare cells known as von Economo neurons, which have only been seen in postmortem examinations. The cells are among the first to die in frontotemporal dementia, a rare form of the disease, which Pașca now hopes to study in xenocortical mice. Prof Madeline Lancaster, group leader at the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology in Cambridge, said the approach was best suited to questions about disorders and treatments where a whole animal was needed. \"It's less clear to me how this will inform our understanding of human brain development since it is rather artificial, and not at all like how the brain develops naturally,\" she said. Many researchers working on brain organoids are growing them in vitro, or in dishes. The hope is they help to reduce the number of animals used in research. \"It's obviously ethically sensitive and there needs to be a very good reason to do this type of animal experimentation, which most scientific avenues do not require,\" Lancaster added. \"The field is still aiming for fully in vitro solutions, but this work can also inform on what may be needed to improve those in vitro models to reach more mature stages.\"","2026-09-26_001":"China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year — 2026/09/26 China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year 2026/09/26 China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year World’s second-largest economy shelled out almost $160bn on bullion in eight months to August China has spent a record sum importing more than 1,000 tonnes of gold this year as the central bank and local investors pour cash into bullion amid rising geopolitical tensions abroad and poor returns on local assets The world's second-largest economy spent $158.8bn on gold in the first eight months of the year. That compared with spending of $96.5bn for all of 2025 on 886 tonnes of gold. The volume was the highest for the period since comparable customs records began in 2017. The jump comes after China reined in gold purchases last year during an intense rally that sent bullion prices soaring from about $2,625 a troy ounce at the start of 2025 to a peak of $5,595 in January Rather than deterring buyers, this year's swings have drawn them in. Gold slid below $4,000 by late June before clawing back most of that ground, settling at about $4,293 on Wednesday. Chinese buying has picked up on the dips: imports topped 150 tonnes a month through the spring, and June's haul was the largest since March 2024. First half imports reached 764 tonnes, up 138 per cent year on year China is also the world's biggest producer of bullion, with the total for last year standing at 384 tonnes, according to the World Gold Council. There are signs that Chinese investors are increasing gold purchases as part of broader efforts to diversify their assets. Chinese holdings of US Treasuries fell to $618bn in July — the lowest level since August 2008, and less than half the peak of more than $1.3tn reached in 2013 Both the central bank and private investors are diversifying their reserves and savings towards an asset with no counterparty risk, as part of a broader long-term wealth preservation strategy,\" said Lisa Liu, managing director at Gold Mountains Asset Management, part of Zijin Mining Group — China's largest gold miner The official sector accounts for only a fraction of the total. The People's Bank of China added 20.2 tonnes in August, its largest monthly purchase since October 2023, lifting reported holdings to about 2,387 tonnes and extending its buying streak to 22 consecutive months. Even so, disclosed official purchases came to less than 80 tonnes over the eight months — leaving the bulk of the imported metal to commercial banks, jewellers, fund managers and households. Much of that demand has moved into financial products rather than physical bars. Chinese gold exchange traded funds took in Rmb40bn ($5.6bn) in the first half, their second-strongest start on record, and added a further 11 tonnes in August to hold 293 tonnes. Physical offtake has gone the other way: withdrawals from the Shanghai Gold Exchange fell unseasonably last month as high prices squeezed jewellery sales Domestic investment options in China are more limited since the country's property market began collapsing in 2021. The benchmark CSI 300 index is down 1.8 per cent for the year and is still more than a fifth below its peak in early 2021. Meanwhile, yields on Chinese government bonds are close to record lows. \"This isn't a short-term trade, it's a multiyear repositioning of household and official assets. The scale and persistence of Chinese buying is now a core driver of global gold prices and we expect it to hold as long as uncertainty over growth and geopolitics remains,\" said Liu Gold is becoming an increasingly important diversifier for global investors at a time when government bonds no longer move inversely to stock markets. Christopher Hamilton, Invesco's head of client solutions for Asia Pacific ex-Japan, said gold prices had ceased to behave as they did during periods of rising real yields, which in the past caused gold prices to drop. \"You're in an environment where you want to own real assets,\" he said Institutional purchases were still the most significant driver of the gold price, Hamilton said. The People's Bank of China purchases more gold than its official disclosures indicate, according to analysts at Goldman Sachs. In a note earlier this month, they estimated that the Chinese central bank bought 35 tonnes of gold in July — considerably higher than its officially stated amount of 20 tonnes. The bank assumes central banks worldwide will keep absorbing about 50 tonnes a month this year, and forecasts gold at $4,900 an ounce by the end of 2026 China's gold purchases come at a time of growing demand from sovereign nations to move bullion closer to the ultimate owners of the metal. Last month the Dutch government moved a portion of its gold reserves from New York to London","2026-09-25_001":"OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says — 2026/09/25 OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says 2026/09/25 OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says Artificial intelligence agents for OpenAI infiltrated a government-run public health care website in Australia in June and accessed nonpublic information, Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said on Wednesday. The case prompted a government investigation into the breach and raised alarm about rogue activities by fast-advancing A.I. systems. OpenAI informed Australia of the unauthorized access on Sept.10, nearly three months after it happened, Mr. Albanese said in disclosing details of the incident, the first known instance of a breach into government systems. Calling the hack \"unacceptable,\" Mr. Albanese, who was in New York for the United Nations General Assembly, said he had personally raised his concerns with Sam Altman, chief executive of OpenAI, earlier in the day. The breach occurred on June 18 by agents operating under an OpenAI research team that was carrying out research into public medicine spending using an internal model, on a data portal for Australia's public health insurance system, Medicare. After encountering \"repeated blocks\" in its attempts to access the Medicare Statistics Reporting Portal, the agent found alternative ways, ultimately accessing parts of the site that were not public, according to the prime minister. He said \"nonsensitive\" data relating to spending was accessed, and no personal information was part of the breach. \"Didn't accept 'no' for an answer, if you like,\" Mr. Albanese said. \"The model attempted alternative ways to obtain the info that it wanted, and this led to unauthorized access into some other areas. \" OpenAI said in a statement that its models were attempting to look up available statistics for questions about Australia during an internal evaluation, and that it accessed no patient records, only aggregate health statistics and internal file names. The company said it identified the activity during its own review of \"misaligned model activity. \" Mr. Albanese said he expressed his disappointment to Mr. Altman on how the government was alerted to the breach — in an email to a general public inbox. In the conversation, the prime minister said he was \"very frank about what I saw as Australia's national interest and what I saw as a failure which occurred here. \" In response, the executive \"acknowledged their issues with protocols,\" Mr. Albanese said. Nicholas Davis, a professor of emerging technologies and codirector of the Human Technology Institute at the University of Technology Sydney, said that because no medical data was accessed, the incident may serve as a much-needed \"warning shot\" to get companies and governments serious about the risks. It raised thorny legal liability questions given that under Australia's criminal law, the type of unauthorized access carried out by the agent comes with a prison sentence of up to two years, he said. \"While it may not have been a particularly hard hack to do, it seems clear that the system, the agent, didn't accidentally get in. It had some intention itself of circumventing controls,\" Mr. Davis said. The Australian government said that it was setting up a rapid task force to review what happened and would explore whether OpenAI would face any legal consequences. It said that the website accessed by the agent was a public-facing portal used by researchers and academics and separate from any systems containing patient medical data. \"All of this, I think, we can take as a warning in relation to the development of A.I. itself,\" Richard Marles, the country's deputy prime minister, said. The Australian Signals Directorate, a government agency overseeing cybersecurity, issued a \"high\" alert about the A.I. agent activity on Thursday, advising organizations with public facing websites to take swift action to shore up weaknesses. The incident was notable because the model went beyond the intentions and authorization of its human operators and \"independently identified vulnerabilities\" in the targeted site, the agency said in the alert. Australia had previously announced it would set A.I. standards to regulate the technology. The country's efforts to be at the vanguard of imposing restrictions and guidelines on increasingly influential aspects of digital life including social media and algorithms have run up against major U.S. tech companies' interests. This week, the U.S. embassy in Australia took the extraordinary step of weighing in on a proposed \"Digital Duty of Care\" law that would give users the ability to opt out of algorithms, saying it would amount to \"facilitating censorship. \" Unauthorized activity by A.I. agents has recently erupted into public view, sparking widespread concerns and calls for guardrails. The need for global regulation has been top of the agenda at this week's session of the U.N. General Assembly.","2026-09-24_001":"Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine — 2026/09/24 Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine 2026/09/24 Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine Wall Street has turned bullish on Meta Platforms, betting that its new AI agent Muse could open a new revenue stream for the social media giant and reshape the company's AI monetization prospects. Muse, launched on September 8, is an AI assistant that can send emails, book travel and complete transactions such as selling a car on behalf of a user. Internally code named Hatch, the agent is built on the open-source framework OpenClaw and is designed to plug into a person's apps across categories including email, calendar, payments, health, shopping and the smart home. Meta has said users choose which apps to connect and can revoke that access at any time, and each agent runs on its own cloud-based virtual machine so it can keep working on tasks even when the user isn't actively engaged with the app. The basic version is free, with subscriptions priced at $20 and $100 a month for heavier use. Meta executives have said the product was originally slated for an April release but was held back for additional months of security work before the company judged it ready to launch. The rollout is currently limited to the US and Canada, available through a standalone app, on iOS and Android, and through WhatsApp, with Meta saying it plans to bring the agent to its Ray-Ban smart glasses in the future. Since its launch, shares of Meta Platforms, have surged more than 20%, outpacing the \"Magnificent 7\" group of US tech companies during the period. Shares have added over $200 billion in market capitalization to touch their highest level in more than seven months as of last close. Shares were up 1% by noon on Tuesday, after surging more than 11% in the previous session. \"While it is still early, we believe that Muse has the potential to become the most widely used consumer AI application since ChatGPT,\" J.P. Morgan analysts said in a note, crediting the app's early traction to strong product-market fit, Meta's distribution reach and a free tier that lowers the barrier to trial and repeat use. If compared on similar metrics to the first 12 days of OpenAI's ChatGPT, Muse has 1.8 million downloads versus 1.3 million for ChatGPT. The app briefly overtook ChatGPT as the top free app on Apple's App Store. Early reviews suggest \"functionality is resonating with users, particularly agent's ability to execute multi-step tasks and workflows with limited user intervention,\" BofA Global Research analysts said. A New York Times reporter who spent two weeks testing Muse described the agent successfully navigating a call with a dental insurer, flagging and canceling duplicate subscriptions after being linked to a bank account, and negotiating down the price of ceramics on Facebook Marketplace — though it struggled with tasks like signing into some third-party loyalty accounts. Enthusiasm around Muse sparked a Wall Street tech rally on Monday, lifting Meta shares more than 11% and boosting chipmakers, including AMD, and Intel, with AMD crossing the $1 trillion valuation mark for the first time. \"The market has interpreted Muse as a boon for the already significantly bottlenecked CPU market,\" said Michael O'Rourke, chief market strategist at JonesTrading. Brokerage Jefferies estimated that if Muse could see 1 billion users by end of 2027 and at least 3% transition to a paid version, it could result in $10.8 billion in annualized revenue, as the brokerage raised its price target on Meta's stock to $875. UBS Global Wealth Management analysts said in a note Meta's Muse provides \"early evidence that consumer AI agents could achieve broad adoption, offering new AI monetization opportunities beyond enterprise application.\" Of the 64 brokerages that cover Meta's stock, 57 rate it \"buy\" or higher with a median price target of $760, according to data compiled by LSEG. \"Prior to Muse, investors had completely dismissed the idea that Meta would be able to release a personalized consumer AI agent, favoring (Alphabet) instead,\" Jefferies analysts led by Brent Thill said. Muse's rise hasn't gone unchallenged. Amazon has blocked the agent from accessing its online store, arguing that unauthorized AI agents violate the terms customers agree to, and Meta separately announced a Shopify partnership to cover some of Muse's shopping functions instead. The standoff echoes a similar fight Amazon waged last year against Perplexity's Comet browser agent, a case in which a federal appeals court sided against Amazon's attempt to block bot-driven purchases. Meta's Muse follows a series of setbacks with its Llama 4 models last year, including criticism over the benchmark results reported for early versions.","2026-09-23_001":"Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack — 2026/09/23 Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack 2026/09/23 Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack Ukraine has hit Moscow with what local authorities said was the largest ever drone attack on the capital, setting a major oil refinery ablaze and killing at least two people, as voting in Russia's stage-managed parliamentary elections entered its final day. Moscow's mayor Sergei Sobyanin said air defences shot down 450 drones as they approached the capital on Saturday night, calling the attack \"unprecedented\". Sobyanin claimed the attack was \"clearly planned to disrupt the elections\", adding that \"the enemy did not succeed\". Several drones hit the Moscow Refinery in the south of the capital, one of Russia's largest. Videos on social media showed a drone exploding into the refinery, with plumes of black smoke shrouding the sunrise, and several areas of the facility on fire. A nearby power station, warehouse and market were also hit. Drones also hit residential buildings near the refinery and at least 11 suburban towns in the surrounding region, authorities said. Two people died in the attacks and a further 20 people were injured, including three children, according to Andrei Vorobyov, governor of the Moscow region. The consequences of the Moscow attack extended beyond the initial fires. Russia's Moscow refinery halted crude processing after both of its main crude distillation units were damaged, according to industry sources. One accounts for 53 per cent of the refinery's capacity and the other for the remaining 47 per cent. Repairs could take several weeks. The plant processed 11.6mn tonnes of crude in 2024 and produced 2.9mn tonnes of gasoline and 3.2mn tonnes of diesel. Flights were grounded at all three of Moscow's main airports. Russia's defence ministry claimed to have shot down a total of 1,110 drones across the country overnight. Kyiv and Moscow have continued striking each other's energy infrastructure throughout the week despite Donald Trump's claim on Monday that the warring sides had agreed to halt such attacks, which the US president blamed for the surge in global diesel prices. Trump has not commented on the attacks, which Russia and Ukraine continued within hours of his statement. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy posted on social media that Ukraine had used domestically produced drones and cruise missiles to target facilities he said earned \"billions of dollars that support the war machine\". Zelenskyy said a plan to de-escalate the strikes on energy and food export infrastructure was \"on the table\" but required \"political will\". The damage has added urgency to diplomatic efforts to halt attacks on energy infrastructure. Zelenskyy is due to meet Trump at the United Nations, where an energy truce with Russia is expected to be discussed. Ukraine says it is prepared to take matching steps if Russia stops targeting its energy sector, critical infrastructure and food exports. Russia has significantly ramped up the use of jet-powered drones to attack Ukraine in recent months and in July fired the largest number of ballistic missiles at Ukraine in a single month since the start of the war. The latter is a huge problem for Ukraine as they can only be intercepted by Patriot air defence missiles that are in short supply. Kyiv also fears Moscow could intensify attacks on heating and power facilities this winter and is seeking additional US-made Patriot air-defence missiles. Russia has rejected calls for a ceasefire of attacks on energy and other infrastructure ahead of what is expected to be a major campaign of air strikes against Ukraine this winter. Russian President Vladimir Putin expects the strikes to strengthen Moscow's position and help extract concessions from Ukraine, according to people familiar with his thinking. Polls in the three-day parliamentary elections in Russia, the first since Putin ordered the invasion of Ukraine in 2022, were set to close on Sunday evening. Turnout at noon local time on Sunday was 48 per cent, according to the central election commission. The vote has proceeded without drama even by Russian standards as an array of Kremlin-run, pro-war parties compete for seats in parliament. Russia's supreme court removed Yabloko, the only party that had expressed even mild opposition to the war, from the ballot last month.","2026-09-22_001":"An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize — 2026/09/22 An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize 2026/09/22 An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize Donald Trump is about to have the opportunity of a lifetime—if he is up for it. As OpenAI's Sam Altman and others have noted in recent days, making a deal with China around artificial intelligence could actually win Mr. Trump the Nobel peace prize he has long sought. Where better to seal such an agreement than his one-day summit with the Chinese leader on September 24th? \"We're leading China in AI,\" he said on September 13th after Mr. Altman and Elon Musk joined Dario Amodei of Anthropic in calling for the pace of AI development to slow to avoid a catastrophe. \"We're the most sophisticated country in the world, and frankly I want to keep it that way because whoever wins AI wins.\" But the reality is that China has already largely caught up, and the AI race has not worked out particularly well for either country. Incidents such as OpenAI's models attacking Hugging Face, another AI firm, show the threat AI agents can pose to the stability and infrastructure of both countries. If the AI bubble pops, both countries may suffer economically. More Americans are concerned about AI than are excited about it, which has fuelled a growing resistance to data centres, and China has its own growing segment of doomers and sceptics as well. I've written about global AI policy for the past decade, and continue to speak regularly with key international players. I firmly believe that China would be open to discussing some kind of collaboration in AI. On September 2nd Kyle Chan of the Brookings Institution, who follows the situation closely, wrote that China seems to be signalling that it \"is willing to agree to joint restrictions [on AI] with the US as long as the terms…are not unilaterally set by the US\", a characterisation I have personally heard dozens of times. It is time to stop casting AI as a winner-take-all, zero-sum race. America and China must instead work together to reconceive AI as a grand collaboration across the globe. Hawks will raise legitimate concerns. America should not share its crown-jewel intellectual property with China; nobody could realistically expect the two countries to waive the possibility of using autonomous weapons. But pragmatically—and for the best outcomes—the Sino-American relationship around AI should have three strands: one that is adversarial, one that is competitive and one that is collaborative. On the adversarial side, neither country is, for example, going to want to give up the freedom to use drones in war. And although the world might be better off if there was a mutual agreement to forsake drone warfare, reaching such an agreement is so unlikely that it would be a waste of negotiating energy to try. On the competitive side, both countries will want to be—and should be—free to develop chips that are faster and better. Both will keep trying to make the best, cheapest AI that they can. And both would like to sell their wares. Tussling over export controls might not be the best use of energy in the upcoming meeting. If the two leaders are open to making progress on the third strand, collaboration, where would a breakthrough be most likely? One promising area is medicine, particularly cancer research. China now contributes quite a bit to developing new cancer treatments. Its pioneering immunotherapy drug Ivonescimab, for instance, outperformed a comparable American drug, Keytruda, in a trial, with patients surviving without observable tumours for about twice as long. By running joint drug trials, and sharing (suitably anonymised) data, the countries could collaboratively run bigger, more accurate studies more quickly, facilitating the development of medical AI worldwide—and thus potentially come up with better candidate drug targets, faster. All of this could begin with an agreement, formal or informal, between the US and China. There would be difficult details to sort out, such as the sharing of intellectual property, including both in advances made directly in technical AI and for any drugs that might be developed. Restrictions around espionage and mutual monitoring and data exchange would no doubt be tricky, too. But there is a substantial—and growing—incentive for both sides to be reasonable, and to reach a deal. The alternative is grim. As the author Robert Wright recently put it, \"America's preoccupation with 'winning' the AI race with China could well lead to unprecedented catastrophes, even catastrophes on a global scale.\" Next week's summit is a chance for Mr. Trump to change tack, help defuse AI's most explosive risks, and in doing so secure that cherished Nobel honour. The prize is there for the taking.","2026-09-21_001":"US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO — 2026/09/21 US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO 2026/09/21 US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO The US, Denmark and Greenland struck a security deal that expands Washington's role on the Arctic island, ending for now a long-running diplomatic spat without ceding land to the US. President Donald Trump said Friday that his administration had reached an agreement handing the US \"permanent control over security\" in Greenland. Leaders of Denmark and Greenland confirmed that an accord is expected to be signed next week at the United Nations General Assembly in New York. Full details have yet to be released. Denmark's Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen said the pact \"recognizes the sovereignty and territorial integrity\" of the Danish kingdom and the Greenlandic people's right to self-determination, indicating that Trump gains no ownership or sovereignty over the island. That marks a significant pullback from Trump's earlier push to control Greenland or make it part of the US. His repeated demands to acquire the territory had fueled distrust between Washington and its European allies, presenting one of the most serious strains on the North Atlantic Treaty Organization in decades. For Denmark and Greenland, the agreement is significant because it appears to bring an end to a nearly two-year standoff without ceding land to the US, Marc Jacobsen, associate professor at the Institute for Strategy and War Studies at the Royal Danish Defence College. \"Donald Trump needs to be seen as a winner,\" he said in an interview. \"So a new agreement was necessary, allowing him to say: look, my pressure worked. \" But when it comes to Trump's ambition to acquire or politically control Greenland, \"he did not get his way on this,\" Jacobsen said. Trump announced the arrangement on social media on Friday, saying it would give Washington the power to block US adversaries from establishing bases or a military presence in Greenland, as well as from making sensitive investments there. Trump also said the US would immediately begin expanding its military footprint on the island and work with Greenlanders on developing and building the facilities, all at \"NO COST to the United States. \" Based on what Trump has disclosed so far, the new pact appears to grant Washington many of the same rights it already enjoys under the existing security arrangement, Jacobsen said. A 1951 agreement with Denmark, updated in 2004 and still in force, gives the US broad latitude to station forces in Greenland. Washington is obliged to consult and inform Copenhagen about its plans, but that was seen as a largely pro forma condition that required little more than notification. The deal includes some elements that appear to be new, said Jacobsen. One is a US veto over whether other countries can establish a military presence in Greenland. Another is that the arrangement would be permanent. The 1951 agreement is explicitly tied to a key NATO accord and therefore the alliance's existence. \"This one is about the US and is not linked to NATO's lifespan,\" he said. Several important questions remain unanswered, Jacobsen said, including where any new bases would be located, what capabilities and personnel they would host, and who would pay for them. Another question is what Greenland receives in return, beyond the evident removal of the threat of US annexation. Greenland's Prime Minister Jens-Frederik Nielsen, who has previously ruled out ceding sovereignty, said he was encouraged by the accord, which \"recognizes Greenland's interests and our place in the international cooperation. \" Since returning to office last year, Trump had repeatedly pressured Denmark, a NATO ally, to give up Greenland. Tensions escalated in January after he refused to rule out military action, prompting Denmark and other NATO allies to send troops to the island. While the deployment was publicly presented as an exercise, Copenhagen sent forces with live ammunition who were prepared to blow up airport runways to prevent US troops from landing if Trump attempted to seize Greenland by force. The confrontation began to ease after Trump held talks with NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. There, the two agreed on the contours of a framework agreement regarding Greenland. Trump's interest in taking over Greenland dates back to his first term in the White House. He has warned that Russia desired a foothold there and that only the US could keep them out. But Danish and Greenlandic leaders have long ruled out the idea of giving up control of the island. \"Next week could be a good week. For Greenland, Denmark and the United States alike,\" Danish Foreign Minister Lars Løkke Rasmussen said in a post on Instagram after the deal was struck. \"Hopefully, a long period of uncertainty will be replaced by a binding agreement that strengthens security in the Arctic and North Atlantic, and thereby our shared security in NATO and Europe — while respecting the Kingdom's red lines. \"","2026-09-20_001":"Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will — 2026/09/20 Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will 2026/09/20 Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will Kevin Warsh has rebuffed Donald Trump’s demands for low borrowing costs, as the president’s pick to run the Federal Reserve led it to the first rate rise since 2023 less than four months into the job. The central bank chair on Wednesday offered the strongest evidence yet that he is prepared to disregard Trump when he corralled all 12 rate setters on the Federal Open Market Committee into backing a quarter-point rate rise. “The plain fact is that inflation is too high and has been for too long,” Warsh said, as he repeatedly told journalists that he would be “hard pressed” to describe current interest rates as so high that they would slow the economy. Warsh’s signal to Wall Street--just weeks before pivotal midterm elections--that he is ready to take further measures to tame the inflation pulsing across the US economy contrasts sharply with the president’s understanding that he was choosing a Fed boss who “certainly wants to cut rates”. Trump was quick to hit out at the Fed on Wednesday, saying on his Truth Social platform that “Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less because we are the Best Credit in the World--BY FAR”. Trump later said he had confidence in Warsh, with whom he has a longstanding personal relationship, and deflected blame on to the Fed board, which includes former chair Jay Powell, whom the president has previously called a “moron” for not lowering rates. It also includes governor Lisa Cook, whom Trump tried to sack before he was stopped by the Supreme Court. “I told Kevin, I said, you might as well vote with the board because it’s just not going to matter. The board is very hostile,” Trump told reporters. Trump’s apparent concession that he is unable to bend the Fed to his will comes as his tariffs and the Middle East conflict have conspired to inflame an affordability crisis that he has dismissed as a “hoax”. “There’s a lot of very clear evidence that the policies of the White House--tariffs and the conflict in the Middle East--have pushed up inflation,” said Claudia Sahm, a former Fed official who is now chief economist at New Century Advisors. Since Warsh’s nomination in January, the big inflation driver has been a rise in petrol and diesel prices--an increase that has cost American households an extra $108bn since the outbreak of the president’s war with Iran, according to a Brown University tracker. “Trump’s tariffs and the war in Iran are driving prices up,” said Jeff Colgan, professor at Brown University. “To a large extent, the inflation that the Fed is fighting is a problem of President Trump’s own making.” Kush Desai, Trump’s spokesperson on economic matters, described the Fed’s move as “unfortunate”, claiming it would “punish businesses who are trying to expand and grow” ”To the extent that we still do have inflation, as the president and others have noted, it’s entirely driven by an energy supply shock, by what’s going on with oil prices in the Middle East,” Desai told Fox News. While another rate rise at the next Fed vote--set for late October, days before the midterms--is seen by some as unlikely, markets are pricing in at least one more increase before the end of the year. Short-term US Treasury yields, which reflect monetary policy expectation, rose sharply in response to Wednesday’s decision as traders interpreted Warsh’s remarks as hawkish. But many said Wednesday’s increase had helped allay those concerns. “Fed credibility is already on the line because we’ve had more than five years of high inflation--the longest bout of inflation since the late1970s, early 1980s, and it has compounded,” said Diane Swonk, chief economist at KPMG US. “Nobody necessarily wants to be at the helm at a pivotal moment, but Kevin Warsh is there. And he did his job. “ When quizzed about the president’s likely response to the decision earlier in the day, Warsh offered only a wry smile. “What the president does and doesn’t like is his prerogative,” said Joe LaVorgna, a former economic counsellor to US Treasury secretary Scott Bessent who is now chief economist for the Americas at SMBC Nikko Securities. “But his views aren’t going to persuade the committee if they’re inconsistent with the fundamentals.”","2026-09-19_001":"China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes — 2026/09/19 China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes 2026/09/19 China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes Newly opened Pinglu Canal will allow cargo from inland provinces to reach the Gulf of Tonkin, avoiding a 560km detour, deepening trade ties China officially opened its landmark Pingu Canal to navigation on Wednesday, creating a new maritime gateway for the country's southwestern hinterland and providing a shorter route to Southeast Asia as Beijing seeks to deepen trade with the region. The 134.2km (83.4 mile) waterway links Nanning, the capital of the southern region of Guangxi, to the Gulf of Tonkin, known as the Beibu Gulf in China, allowing cargo from inland provinces to reach the sea without taking a roughly 560km detour via Guangzhou, cutting both transit times and logistics costs. With a planned investment of 72.7 billion yuan (US$10.8 billion), the project, which began construction in August 2022, is designed to accommodate vessels of up to 5,000 tonnes and save more than 5 billion yuan in overall transportation costs each year. The canal is the first man-made waterway connecting a river to the sea to be built since the founding of the People's Republic of China in 1949, state-run Xinhua News Agency reported. Some observers have described it as the country's most significant canal undertaking since the Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal, and it forms a key link in the New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor. It is equipped with a tiered navigation zone system to facilitate the movement of inland vessels, allowing qualified vessels to travel through the canal and into the sea without transferring cargo to another ship. The canal's opening comes after a series of navigation tests. In mid-August, four merchant vessels ranging from 1,000 to 5,000 tonnes conducted phased trials along the canal's main channel, testing the safety and reliability of the waterway, locks, anchorages and service areas, state media reported. Earlier that month, a maritime patrol vessel completed the first full-route transit test along the waterway, according to the canal's operator. Late in August, the canal's first command and management vessel was delivered to oversee navigation and handle emergency responses along the waterway. Beyond the engineering work, the project has also involved efforts to limit its environmental impact. At the Qingnian navigation hub, engineers built a dedicated fish passage of more than 480 metres, containing 120 pools, to help aquatic species navigate a water-level difference of nearly 10 metres, with a separate route designed for eels and other climbing species. Builders also relocated thousands of mature mangrove trees along the route and have since planted hundreds of thousands of new ones to offset the disruption to local ecosystems. The canal aims to give China's less-prosperous inland regions quicker and easier access to global sea lanes, boosting local industry and strengthening trade with the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (Asean), China's largest trading partner as a bloc. That ambition is already taking shape. Last Thursday, a multimodal freight train carrying more than 370 tonnes of construction machinery and other products arrived in Guangxi, after departing from Hunan province, with the cargo scheduled for export following the canal's opening. The project is also part of a broader push to expand China's inland waterways, with several other provinces, including Jiangxi, Hubei, Hunan and Henan, planning or advancing ambitious multibillion-dollar waterway expansions to boost local economic growth. By using the canal, provinces including Yunnan, Guizhou, Hunan and Sichuan, as well as the metropolis of Chongqing, could see their logistics costs reduced by around 18 per cent to 30 per cent, making it \"the shortest, most economical and most convenient route to the sea in the western region\", according to an August 13 report by Yunnan authorities. The canal's construction has also spurred a broader build-out of supporting infrastructure and transport links. At Nanning Port's Liujing operations area, berths have been upgraded to handle vessels of up to 5,000 tonnes. The Beibu Gulf Port is also seeking to attract cargo from Chongqing, Sichuan and Yunnan, while cross-border railways such as the China-Laos Railway are expected to feed into the network, according to state media reports. The opening comes as China's trade with Asean continues to grow. Beijing has increasingly sought to strengthen supply chains and economic ties with the region as tensions with the West persist over trade and technology. Total trade between China and Asean reached US$862.75 billion in the first eight months of this year, up 25.6 per cent from a year earlier, customs data showed. China's exports to Asean stood at US$546 billion, up 25.8 per cent. In contrast, exports to the US rose 6.2 per cent in dollar terms over the same period. The opening came just ahead of this year's China-Asean Expo in Nanning, an annual trade and investment fair showcasing products ranging from agricultural goods and food to machinery and hi-tech equipment.","2026-09-18_001":"Is this the end for China's love of the English language? — 2026/09/18 Is this the end for China's love of the English language? 2026/09/18 Is this the end for China's love of the English language? Half a century ago, learning English went almost overnight from being discouraged to a national obsession. Throughout the 1980s and beyond, young and old alike would gather in parks, at what became known as \"English corners\", to practise with each other. The language became a core part of the national curriculum, on a par with maths and Chinese, right up to the gaokao, or university entrance exam. Now a prominent teacher and education blogger has reignited a national debate by questioning whether learning English is necessary at all. \"Our children desperately begin learning English even before they have properly mastered their native language,\" Tang Jiafeng wrote. \"Yet we are a civilisation that dates back thousands of years. English is just a tool. It's enough to be able to use it. \" The debate touches on questions of whether children are over-pressured by schools and parents and the suitability of traditional rote-learning techniques. It has also acquired a new dimension as artificial-intelligence translation tools improve. With technology increasingly capable of translating languages almost instantly, some young people are asking whether years of intensive English study will remain as valuable as it once was. At the same time, uncertainty about graduate employment has prompted a wider discussion about which skills students will actually need when they enter the workforce. Tang, who has a PhD in mathematics and offers tuition for postgraduate entrance exams, has established himself as a popular voice in the competitive world of online advice for students. His most recent post was a reaction to announcements by provincial education authorities that they were downgrading noncore subjects like biology, geography and history for high-school entrance exams to reduce the pressure on teenagers. Liaoning province, for example, recently removed history, biology and geography from its middle-school entry test as part of efforts to reduce students' workload. Tang claimed that this was effectively prioritising a subject, English, that would be \"completely irrelevant to the majority of people in their future professional lives\". English remains a compulsory subject in China's basic education system alongside Chinese and mathematics. Under the traditional 750-point gaokao structure, each of those three subjects is worth 150 points, even as the weighting and assessment of other subjects have undergone reforms. The most prominent immediate response came from Hu Xijin, a former editor of the English-language tabloid Global Times. He accused Tang of being \"biased and narrow-minded\" and urged education departments to ignore him. One argument favoured by critics of English is that when native English speakers are so poor at learning Chinese — a fairly indisputable claim — why should Chinese people learn their language? That ignored the fact that, like it or not, English was the world's lingua franca, Hu said. \"More than 90 per cent of international scientific academic papers are produced in English,\" he wrote. \"If a person wants to advance to a higher level, being familiar with and mastering English is one of the most important educational preparations. \" The debate has also raised questions about access to international knowledge and opportunities, particularly for students who may later work or study across borders. Another argument against English is that it is a sign of elitism; in practice, only the upper middle classes can afford to send their children to universities in the West, or travel there. Hu argued that downgrading English would make elitism worse, since the upper classes would find new ways to pay for their children to learn English anyway. Critics of weakening English in public education have similarly argued that wealthier families could simply buy private tuition, while students from less privileged backgrounds would have fewer opportunities to acquire the same skills. Another intervention, admittedly from a professor with a vested interest, was more blunt. \"Let me ask: is maths useful in everyday life? \" wrote Cao Wen, who teaches at Beijing Foreign Studies University, a breeding ground for diplomats. \"Ordinary people go to work, buy groceries, watch movies. Do we really need to use functions or calculate square roots every day? Yet we still expect children to learn maths. If you judge whether a subject is necessary simply by whether people will use it in their daily lives, can you really call yourself an educator? \"","2026-09-17_001":"Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids — 2026/09/17 Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids 2026/09/17 Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids The Bank of Mom and Dad is staying open for the grandkids as the cost of raising a child in the US tops $300,000 for the first time this year — and that doesn't include college tuition. Nearly two in five parents of young children expect to receive financial help — or ask for it — from their own parents or grandparents in the next year. Among them,47% said they will receive cash for day-to-day basics from relatives, according to the BMO Real Financial Progress Index survey. A quarter said they expect relatives to contribute to their children's 529 or other savings accounts. \"Families trying to figure out how they're going to solve for these increased costs are really trying to lean on their extended families,\" said Robin Growley, BMO's US head of consumer products. Parents with family support said they saved $1,915 a year on childcare and babysitting, and $1,443 on groceries. Gisele Ayora, a 36-year-old marketing professional and mother of three, said the biggest financial boosts she got from her in-laws were housing and childcare. They bought her family a $600,000, six-bedroom home in Manahawkin, New Jersey. They asked the couple to pay about $1,700 a month — roughly what they had been paying to rent a two-bedroom apartment — about half of what a mortgage would've cost them. Her mother-in-law also stepped in to watch the kids when Ayora had to work late or travel for her job. For some young families, the help can feel like getting part of an inheritance early, when they need it most, said Lindsey Stanberry, founder of the personal finance website The Purse. Baby Boomers are sitting on a lot of wealth, while their adult children are facing steep housing and childcare costs. But for grandparents without as much to spare, that support can eat into their own retirement plans. Haron Marlee estimates she spent $24,000 in one year covering everything from diapers to gas to car insurance for her 36-year-old daughter, Melissa, and grandchildren. She went from a semi-retired therapist to a regular babysitter and even picked up bartending shifts to help support them. \"Those burdens fundamentally changed my finances, my retirement plans,\" said Marlee, a 59-year-old who now lives in Ocean County, New Jersey. Her daughter, a law school graduate, said she was initially embarrassed by the situation. But when her marriage ended, she struggled to find a job and it was the best option to stay afloat. Financial advisers warn parents against sacrificing their own retirement savings to keep adult children afloat. Lorie Jones, owner of Fearless Financial Advisors in Colorado, said 20% to 25% of her clients are covering a significant share of their adult children's expenses well into their late 20s and early 30s. \"I do have to talk to some of those parents and say, 'You need to take care of you first',\" she said. \"'You've got to prioritize your own retirement'.\" Barb Green knows that strain firsthand. Her 29-year-old son moved back home in 2017 after a semester of college and has stayed there rent free since. Green said the job market and broader economy have made it difficult for him to move out. He doesn't yet have kids, but supporting him has put a dent in Green and her husband's retirement plans. \"The money that we've been planning on putting away for retirement to travel and do fun things during retirement — since we've spent all of our life working — I feel like we are not able to plan that or do that because it's like we're still raising him because he's still living there.\" She's also seen the dynamic through her work. Green is a coach at Community Connections for Children in Pennsylvania, an organization that helps families find and apply for childcare. She said that she's seen a roughly 60% increase in grandparents caring for their grandchildren in the past five years. For Marlee, the semi-retired therapist, the strain was more than financial. \"It took a toll on me that I had not anticipated,\" she said. She's since started a Facebook group and written an advice book for parents of adult children called Stop Raising Adult Children: Break the Cycle of Blame and Take Your Life Back. Marlee tells parents financially supporting their adult children to set ground rules and boundaries — and, importantly, an end date for their contribution. She also tells them to protect their retirement. Eventually, she and her daughter made a more formal arrangement. Melissa would help out through \"sweat equity\" by cooking meals and cleaning. Once she secured work, she could live with her for free for one more year. Since then, she's had to start contributing to household expenses and rent. \"You cannot jump into the riptide with your children,\" Marlee said. \"You can only lend them a hand.\"","2026-09-16_001":"HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself — 2026/09/16 HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself 2026/09/16 HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself The organisers of a viral sporting event in Beijing have faced backlash and criticism after an Australian woman was allowed to continue competing after soiling herself. Athlete Joanna Wietrzyk was participating in the global indoor fitness race HYROX in the Chinese capital at the weekend when she suffered a bout of faecal incontinence. Wietrzyk was allowed to finish the course and won her event, with videos and photos posted online showing her continuing through demanding exercises and using communal equipment while visibly soiled. Wietrzyk, who is a HYROX world record holder, was invited to compete at the Beijing event. In just four months, Joanna Wietrzyk has gone from discovering HYROX to dominating it. \"I didn't even know what HYROX was four months ago,\" Wietrzyk admits. But with a background in competitive tennis and distance running, she's adapted fast. The incident has gone viral on Chinese social media and sparked debate about HYROX's health and safety standards, as well as athlete responsibility. \"She's so selfish — she kept running the whole way even though she had diarrhoea,\" one user on Weibo questioned. \"What about the runners behind her?\" Another wrote: \"The host is ultimately unprofessional! \" On Weibo, the hashtag #Hyrox incontinence female competitor has not been punished# has more than 27 million views. An article on the Beijing incident was the most-viewed story on Chinese state media website the Global Times on Monday. After the race, she doubled down and even shared a selfie on Instagram, which has since been deleted, appearing to show her in a hospital bed. \"Go hard or go home...a win is a win.\" Footage and images of the incident subsequently spread across social media and fitness forums, with reactions ranging from praise for Wietrzyk's determination to concerns around hygiene. HYROX is a global fitness challenge in which participants race to complete often gruelling stations, including sandbag lunges, burpees, sled pushes and rowing. In a statement, HYROX China said the area was decontaminated after the race had finished. \"After the incident, the organising committee immediately closed off and isolated the affected area and track, and completed comprehensive disinfection,\" organisers said. \"Relevant equipment was removed from the venue, and waste was disposed of in accordance with municipal waste-management standards. \" The organisers also said they would replace flooring at the venue to protect future athlete health. HYROX World also issued a statement addressing the incident. \"HYROX has clear bio-contaminant and medical procedures in place,\" the statement said. \"In this instance, an unforeseen situation arose in which protocols designed for elite competition and mass participation became blurred, creating ambiguity in how they were applied and understood. \" But social media users and other athletes at the event have attacked the response and questioned why Wietrzyk was allowed to finish the race, potentially risking the health and safety of other competitors. \"For people who were exposed to that situation, there could be genuine physical and psychological consequences,\" one competitor named johopalooza posted on Instagram. \"I paid HYROX to provide a professionally organised and safe event. So while I understand the athlete perspective, ultimately my contract and expectation is with HYROX. Protecting athletes is important, but showing the wider community that HYROX takes responsibility when its own procedures fail is just as important. \" HYROX has also been accused of double standards over its hygiene rules, which punish athletes with time penalties if they litter on the course, spit or clear their nose onto the floor. Li Hongxin, an associate professor of sports management at China's Zhengzhou University of Light Industry, said the athlete should not be criticised or judged for experiencing a physiological problem. \"I think her determination and sportsmanship are very understandable,\" he said. But he said the incident raised questions about the responsibility of event organisers. \"Organisers should assess whether such a situation could affect the health or wellbeing of other competitors,\" he said. Dr Li said this was particularly important because HYROX was an indoor event. \"Take a marathon as an example. If a competitor is menstruating, organisers would generally respect their decision to continue because a marathon is an outdoor event,\" he said. \"They don't share equipment with other competitors, so it generally doesn't pose a public safety concern. But HYROX is an indoor event, where competitors share equipment. In that situation, I think the organisers should have staff step in and adjust the event, or take appropriate emergency measures.\"","2026-09-16_002":"The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way — 2026/09/16 The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way 2026/09/16 The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way There is a comforting myth about learning—that if you just find the right method, the right teacher, or the right shortcut, everything will suddenly become easy. People spend years searching for the perfect system, hoping it will eliminate struggle altogether. But the truth is far less appealing: real learning is not designed to feel easy. At its core, learning is a process of friction. It requires you to sit with confusion, to face what you don’t understand, and to stay in that discomfort long enough for clarity to emerge. This is what people often call hard learning—not because it is inefficient, but because it demands effort without immediate reward. When people avoid this stage, they turn to passive forms of learning. They watch videos, read summaries, highlight sentences, and feel productive. But passive exposure creates familiarity, not mastery. It feels like progress, but it rarely leads to real understanding. Hard learning looks very different. It is slower, quieter, and often frustrating. It involves rereading the same paragraph multiple times, attempting problems without knowing the answer, and failing repeatedly before getting it right. There is no instant gratification—only gradual improvement. One of the most important elements of hard learning is repetition. Not mechanical repetition, but deliberate repetition—returning to the same material with deeper attention each time. With every pass, your understanding shifts. What was once confusing becomes familiar; what was once familiar becomes clear; what was once clear becomes flexible. Another key aspect is active engagement. Hard learning requires you to think, not just consume. It asks you to explain ideas in your own words, test your understanding, and apply knowledge in new contexts. This is where knowledge transforms into ability. The difficulty of hard learning often discourages people. It feels inefficient compared to faster, easier alternatives. But what seems efficient in the short term is often ineffective in the long term. Learning that avoids struggle rarely survives beyond the moment. There is also a psychological barrier. Many people equate difficulty with failure. When something feels hard, they assume they are not good at it. In reality, difficulty is not a sign of inadequacy—it is a sign that learning is happening. If you look at people who achieve deep expertise in any field, their process is rarely glamorous. It is built on long hours of focused effort, repeated attempts, and a willingness to stay with complexity. They don’t avoid hard learning—they rely on it. So when people say, The best way to learn is to push through, it is not a rejection of good methods or smart strategies. It is a recognition that no method can replace effort. Techniques can guide you, but they cannot do the work for you. In the end, learning is not about avoiding difficulty—it is about building the capacity to move through it. And the sooner you accept that, the sooner your learning becomes real.","2026-09-15_001":"Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' — 2026/09/15 Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' 2026/09/15 Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' It was the kind of exit every entrepreneur dreams about. Anastasia Koroleva and her husband sold for nine figures: 100% cash on Day 1, zero earnouts, no strings attached. But what followed blindsided the founder. Instead of the soft life she'd expected, Koroleva found herself dealing with years of unease and a painful divorce. Today, Koroleva explores what happens when net worth removes the need to work on Exit Paradox. Her interviews with the extraordinary wealthy have uncovered a recurring theme: For many, the exit does not feel like a crowning achievement — or, at least, not for long. It can feel more like an existential rupture. Guests describe becoming staggeringly rich using the language of trauma: \"recovering from a $500 million sale,\" \"thrashing about for a decade\" or drifting in a state of aimless ennui. Financial hardship defined Michia Rohrssen's earliest memories. At age 4, he remembers watching his mother cry over a failing car they had no money to fix. Money was \"this constant source of pain in our lives,\" he recalls. Rohrssen moved to San Francisco in his 20s and went into startups, co-founding automotive software firm Prodigy in 2015. Then, in 2021, when Rohrssen was 31, Prodigy sold for $110 million. All his money problems were solved. Rohrssen was elated — and although he never thought money could replace inner work, the existential dread that slowly set in turned out to be darker than the poverty he'd survived. \"It's really bad,\" he says. \"When you're broke and things are hard, you have this belief that, well, one day when I sell the company then things can get better. It's actually much harder when you've achieved those financial outcomes and you still feel just as broken. You feel just as inadequate, and you're like, 'But I did the thing? \" He traveled around a bit but had little purpose day-to-day beyond waking up to see his wife. \"I just died,\" he says. There's a term for this phenomenon — \"sudden wealth syndrome\" — says licensed psychotherapist Annie Wright. One reason is velocity. The time between starting a company and hitting a liquidity event has compressed dramatically since the crypto boom, leaving founders less time to mature alongside their wealth. Wright says another factor gets the least attention but can exact a heavier toll: the moral weight of how the wealth was made. Unlike lottery winnings, a founder's fortune is deeply intertwined with their identity. That makes the success feel earned — but any subsequent dissatisfaction feels painfully personal. Many fall prey to the arrival fallacy: the assumption that reaching the number, the milestone they'd organized their lives around, would deliver lasting peace. When it doesn't, some jump straight into their next venture — less from inspiration than a need to prove the first success wasn't a fluke. Parr founded The Hustle, a digital business newsletter by HubSpot in 2021. These days his focus is Hampton, where he dissects what he calls \"champagne problems\" — problems nonetheless. His best advice? Don't do anything irreversible for at least a year after exit. The money, at a certain size, also stops feeling real. \"Up to about $20 million, you're still thinking about, 'How do I use this resource well? \" says Walling, the psychologist. \"You kind of watch it and pay attention and make good investments. And then there's a number beyond that where it's kind of like Monopoly money. That can have this delusional effect on someone's psychology. They are unmoored and unanchored to the value of anything. \" Finding that equilibrium can take years. Of roughly 1,200 post-exit stories Koroleva has studied, she estimates that a decade after reaching \"post-economic\" status, 15% are \"in a pretty sad place\"; 70% are comfortable but feel some emptiness; and just 15% are thriving. Walling says the people who handle a windfall best tend to hold two ideas at once: \"I worked really hard, and I got really lucky. \" Reward without inflated self-regard. Those who struggle most tend to be the ones for whom the company was everything — no healthy relationships, no hobbies, no sense of meaning outside the exit. \"That's where you tie your whole self-worth and your individual sense of well-being to this event,\" Walling says. No amount of money is \"going to be enough to sustain that for you. \" For now, these are problems for the very few and the very rich. But some thinkers believe AI could make the scenario universal. A harder view is that there is no lasting fix. Staying dissatisfied is how our species has survived, why yesterday's luxury reliably becomes tomorrow's basic need. That's the way Rohrssen leans, at least for now.\" There's probably something in the human condition that's hard to root out,\" he says. \"Maybe you're just fighting against gravity.","2026-09-14_001":"No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick — 2026/09/14 No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick 2026/09/14 No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick Socialism will not be stopped with more socialism. President Donald Trump's proposal to pay every adult citizen $5,000 if the GOP holds the House and Senate this fall is a desperate gimmick that betrays a profound lack of seriousness about the looming fiscal crisis. \"If the Republicans win, you win with us, and you get $5,000,\" Trump said Wednesday night at the party's midterm convention in Dallas. He dubbed the payout the \"Trump Dividend\" and said recipients would have to spend the money inside the United States — but offered no details on how, or whether, Congress would even be asked to authorize it. This would cost an estimated $1.2 trillion next year, according to the Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget — roughly $5,000 times some 245 million adult citizens. The money would need to be borrowed on top of the $40 trillion national debt — and paid back with interest. Even the administration can't agree on where the money is supposed to come from. Trump said revenue from tariffs would cover what he branded as a \"dividend,\" telling Fox News's Laura Ingraham that \"we're taking in $21 trillion\" from tariffs. The Treasury's own numbers put net tariff revenue this fiscal year at about $154 billion. Overall tariff and excise collections since the start of last year total just under $500 billion — but more than $100 billion of that has already been refunded after the Supreme Court struck down many of the administration's tariffs, meaning the government refunded more money in some months than it collected. The taxes on imports that remain in effect are expected to generate less than $200 billion this year, a sum already baked into this year's deficit of more than $2 trillion. The president previously said he'd earmark this same pot of tariff revenue to cover nearly $6 trillion in other costs. It cannot pay for everything twice. Flooding the economy with money would only worsen inflation, since it would mean more dollars chasing the same amount of goods. That would raise the prices of basic staples, as Americans experienced following the two rounds of stimulus checks during the pandemic. But don't count on checks in the mail. This is at least the fourth time in this term Trump has promised Americans a payout that failed to arrive. First it was Elon Musk's \"DOGE dividend,\" floated in February 2025 as $5,000 checks funded by savings the U.S. DOGE Service was supposed to identify; Musk left Washington a few months later having found nowhere near that amount, and the idea was quietly shelved. Then, last November, Trump promised $2,000 tariff rebate checks by the middle of 2026 — timing that would have landed the money in voters' pockets just before the midterms. That check never went out either. Now the same election-season logic is back: Trump told Ingraham the $5,000 dividend would wait until after the midterms because Democrats \"can't\" pass it now, even though Republicans currently control both chambers of Congress. (On Thursday, Trump separately announced that nearly a million Americans will receive $500 rebate checks before the election after being overcharged for their Obamacare plans.) Joe Lonsdale, co-founder of Palantir and a Trump donor, tweeted from the convention: \"I am hugely in favor of winning the midterms, and love much about this admin, but am strongly against bread and circus bribes.\" After Trump's speech, Fox News anchor Bret Baier asked Vice President JD Vance to respond to critics describing the checks as bribery that would bankrupt the country. Vance replied that tariffs are creating \"incredible wealth\" and have \"helped us pay down debt.\" That wealth might be real for lobbyists and lawyers who specialize in seeking exemptions for their clients, but they're raising prices for everyone else. Trump's speech underscored why so many Americans who care about fiscal sanity feel politically homeless. As Trump bragged in Texas about the government taking stakes in chipmaker Intel and Venezuelan oil fields, 27-year-old state Rep. David Morales — a Bernie Sanders-endorsed democratic socialist running on rent control — beat incumbent Democratic Mayor Brett Smiley in Providence, Rhode Island's primary. A Quinnipiac poll released the same week found that 59 percent of Democrats now consider themselves supporters of the Democratic Socialists of America, versus 26 percent of voters overall. Pandering politicians routinely promote expensive ideas in the months before elections. Usually they're offering new programs, more subsidies or tax cuts that won't be paid for. The electorate has become habituated to promises of \"free\" buses, \"free\" child care and \"free\" medical coverage, as well as student loan debt cancellation. Trump's checks-for-votes scheme is just more explicit."}};
